  "tnodes": {
    "srd.20260113205330.1": "Notes for CCSP exam\nUsing Leo in VSCODE for Literate programming and node aware outlines\n\n",
    "srd.20260113194242.1": "Domain 1: Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design\n17 %",
    "srd.20260113194548.1": "Understanding Cloud Computing  Concepts\n",
    "srd.20260121195852.1": "Cloud computing represents a paradigm shift from traditional computing methods, where physical infrastucture and software are already owned and managed by users, to mode where pgysical infrastucture and software are directly owned and managed by users, to a model where resources are dilivered over the internet as on-demand services.",
    "srd.20260121211842.1": "Elasticity, scalibility, Self-Servie Provisioning, Metered Services",
    "srd.20260121211910.1": "Rabidly scale up and down with demand, paying only for what you use.",
    "srd.20260121212045.1": "Users can spin up compute resources for almost any type of workload on-demand.",
    "srd.20260121212211.1": "Cloud systems automatically control and optimize resources by leveraging a metering capability at some level of abstraction appropiate to the type of service (e.g., starage, processing, bandwidth and active user accounts).",
    "srd.20260121200505.1": "Cloud comupting is defined by its abiility to privide scalable and elestic IT-related capabilities as aservice to external customers using internet technologies \n\nIt centraizes data storage, memeory, processing and bandiwith into virtualied resources that can be alllocate and istrubutes quickly\n\nThis mode offers significant costs benefits, transforming capital-intensive infrastructures into variable cost environments.",
    "srd.20260121200524.1": "Several service models exits catering to different business needs",
    "srd.20260121203211.1": "Examples: Amazon EC2 and Googlr Compute Engine\nThis foundational model provides virtulised phyical computing resources over the Internet\n\nIaaS allows business to rent servers, storage, and networking technology on-demand, therby eliminating the capital expenditiure of owning hardware.",
    "srd.20260121203254.1": "ExamplesL Google App Engine and Microsoft Azure\n\nThis service model provides a platform allowing customers to develop, run and manage application without the complexity of building and maintaining the infrastucture.",
    "srd.20260121203334.1": "Examples: Salesforce.com amd Microsoft Office 365\nSaas delivers software applications ove the Internet, on a subscription basis, freeing users from complex software and hardware mangagement.",
    "srd.20260121200556.1": "Cloud Computing deployment models define the types of access to the cloud environment, whaich can significantly impact the scalability and security of IT systems.",
    "srd.20260121204235.1": "Operates solely for a single organization. It cam be managed internally or by a third party and hosted internally or externally. Benefets include greate control and security. ",
    "srd.20260121204538.1": "Services are rendered over a network that is open for public use. Public clouds offer efficiency and scale benefits, and are owned and operated by businesses offering cloud services.",
    "srd.20260121204605.1": "This environment uses mix of on-premies, private cloud, and third-party, public cloud services with orchestration between the two platforms.\n\nIt alloews businesses to scale cloud-based services to meet flucuation workloads.",
    "srd.20260121204624.1": "Shares infrastucture between several organizations froma specific community with common concerns (security,compliance, jurisdiction, etc.), possibly managed by a third party or he organization in the community.\n ",
    "srd.20260113200102.1": "The following groups play key roles and hold specific responsibilities associated with cloud computing:",
    "srd.20260122171610.1": "An enitity that utilizes or subscribes to cloud-based resources or services",
    "srd.20260122171706.1": "A third Party enitity that manages and bears operational responsibility for cloud-based data backup services and solutions, offering these to constomuers central  data center data ",
    "srd.20260122171721.1": "A third party  provider offering a range of cloud based services, including  infrastuctur storage, application , and application and platform services, while utilizg a related Cloud servious provide.",
    "srd.20260122171736.1": "An Organization that acts as an intemediary bewteen the cloud service provider ad the cloud servious providr and the cloud customer, facilitating transactions or negotiations.",
    "srd.20260122174708.1": "Regulatory agencis like the FTC and SEXC enforce complaince with laws such as HIPPA, SOX, and GLBA\n",
    "srd.20260122171747.1": "Resource Pooling made possible through virtulaization\n\nNetwork advancements that facilitate seemless access and connectivity \n\nOrchestration, enhanciing the cost eand optimizaing resource utilization",
    "srd.20260121195227.1": "Describe Cloud Referance Architecture",
    "srd.20260122194911.1": "SaaS, PaaS IaaS Consumer's, Cloud Providers, Cloud Auditors, Cloud Brokers, and Cloud Carrier's are distingushed by different activities they are focused on.\n\nThe following capabilities form the fondation of cloud service categories\n",
    "srd.20260123163536.1": "The cloud consumer can provision and exercise significant configuration control over processing, staorage and netwirk resources.",
    "srd.20260123163754.1": " The customer can deploy code and applications using programming languages and libririe maintained and controlled by thr cloud provider\n ",
    "srd.20260123163911.1": " Customers utilizes a fully established application provided by the provider with limites user configuration options.\n",
    "srd.20260123160050.1": "Enables consumers to use the providerd applications running on a cloud platform and accessible via a web browser\n\nHuman Resources\nSocial Networks\nFinaicials\nContent Mangegment\nEmail and Office Productivity\nDocument Management\nGames \nVirtual Desktops\nCollaboration\nBilling\nCRM\nSales\nERP\n\nDelivers As:\n\nHosted Application MAnagement: commercially avaiible software delivered over the web\n\nSoftware on Demand: offer costomer network access to a single copy of an application specifically designed for SaaS delivery. ",
    "srd.20260123160117.1": " Provide the customer the capability to deploy thier own or acquired applications onto the cloud infrastucture to utilise programming languses, libraries,services and tools, i.e Googlr apps Etelos Long Jump etc.\n \n Database\n Application Deployment tools\n Integration\n Development & Testing\n Business Intelligence",
    "srd.20260123160425.1": "Aallows consumers to provision essiential resources, i.e processing power, storage and networking to enable the deployment and running of various software, applicatins and operating systems without owning the underlying infrastructure hardware but rather paying for its usage as needed.  \n\n Services Mangement\n Platform Hosting\n Compute (Virtual Machines)\n Backup & Recovery\n CDN\n Storage",
    "srd.20260123160537.1": "Service Deployment\nService Orchestration\nCloud Service Mangement\nSecurity \nPrivacy\n",
    "srd.20260123160753.1": " Is an entity capable of conducting an independent investigation of cloud service controls  with the objective of providing a conclusive assesment\n\nInvolves assessing the services offered by a cloud provider across varioos dimensions, including security controls, perfrmance, privacy impact and more\n\nA cloud auditor evaluates the security controls within the information system to determine thier accuracy of implementation, adherance to excpected operations, and the ability to produce the desired security outcomes in alignment with the systems securit requirements.\n",
    "srd.20260123161313.1": "Service Intermediation\nService Aggregation\nService Arbitrage\n\nInstead of directly contacting cloud providers, consumers can opt to request cloud services through a broker who acts as an intermediary , managing the use, performance and delivery of cloud services and facilitating negotiations and relationships between providers and consumers.",
    "srd.20260123162933.1": " Acts as a Mediator that ficilitates the connectiviity and transprotation of services between consumers and providors\n\n Access is provided through various network and telecommunication devices, i.e laptops, desktops, mobile phones and internet devices typically managed by network and telecom carriers or disignated transport agent.\n",
    "srd.20260123170010.1": "Deployment Models",
    "srd.20260123170121.1": "Offers resources dynamically provisioned via the Internet for flexibility and scalbility\n\nAvailible to the general public or large Industry groups\n\nOpeerated by Organizations providing divere computing needs\n\nExamples Amazon Elastic Compute Elastic\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Streamlined and easily provisioned that are scalable\n- Easy and Inexpensive to setup - the provider covers the cost of applications, hardware and bandwidth\n- No waste of resorces, payment is based upon consumption\n",
    "srd.20260123170201.1": " An internal or organizational cloud involving a distinct and secure cloud -based environment where only specific clients may operate\n\n Maganged by the organization itself or by third party, mayb hosted on-premise or off premise\n\n Benefits:\n\n Inceased Contro; over data, applications and underlying systems.\n\n Ownership and retention of governance controls\n\n Assurance of data location\n\n Elimination of legal / compliance requirements across multiple jurusdictions\n ",
    "srd.20260123170212.1": "Where two or more sprcific env's (public, private, internal or external) are combined\n\nFacilitates flexability of deployment options allowing workloads to move between priv and public resources as needed or as cost change\n\nOten used for archiving and backing up data by replicating local data to a public cloud\n\nBenefits:\n\nReuses Past Tech investment and allows for control over critical components and systems\n\nRetains ownership and oversight or critial tech assests, task, and processes while being a cost effective method for handeling non-critical business functions and/or bursting capabilities.",
    "srd.20260123170220.1": " Enables groups or Orgs acess to distrubuted systems formed by combining services from various clouds to meet a specific need of the community, business, or industry\n\n te Infrastructure is shared by organizations with similar concerns or tasks and is typically managed by a third party or through collaboration amoung community members.\n\n Benefits:\n\n Cost Effective\n Security of shared resources and infrastucure is often enhanced\n Collaboration and data sharing is Ideal amoung users.\n",
    "srd.20260123170231.1": " Utilizes multiple public  or private clouds and deployment strategies that intergrate resources from both env's often across vendord or regions but without combining public/private env like a hybrid.  public-to-public  or priv-to-priv\n\n Can offer tools to enhance the reliabilty of services and enhances availibility \n\n Benefits:\n\nUusing different providers enable mix -math of the best feture of ech provider service to meet a specific need of the business, application or workload.\n\nReduced latency  - select regions and zones that are close in proximity  to clients to min latency\n\nHigh Service Availibility - rare for two providers to experience an incdent and proximity enhances the user experience\n\n",
    "srd.20260123175956.1": "Irrespective of the serfice category  some aspects of cloud computing are univaersal\n",
    "srd.20260123180109.1": "Interoperability  refers to the ability and/or ease of which an applications components can be moved or reused\n\nThe underlying platform, API structure, operating system or cloud provider should not hinder the efficient trasition of services to a alternative solution\n\nOrganizations with a high degree of interoperablity  within its systems are not restricted to a single provider and can easily migrate to another for equal or greater service  or better pricing",
    "srd.20260123180756.1": " allows data to move between different providers easily \n Orgs that optimize ots data for portabilty gains flexibity to transition between providers and hosting models to leverage advantages\n\n Enables an Orgs to continuously explore cloud hosting services\n",
    "srd.20260123181039.1": "The capability of a customer to extraxt all thier systems and data from a provider with assurance that it all has been completly removed within the ageed amout of time.\n\nEncompasses the ability to trnsistion to a different provider",
    "srd.20260123181452.1": "Fundemental ascpects of a cloud to the inherent characteristics\n\nResiliency and availibility are distigused features often with a SLA \n\nPerformance is crucial and must be prioritiesd\n\nCustomrs are unlikly  to remain with a provider for long periords and will shift to another provider if they fall short \n\n",
    "srd.20260123182236.1": "Security is a major concern in multi tenant  envns eith in the same framework\n\nProvider must ensure the customer controls are met while also adapt to changing requriements\n\nAmid large organizational proiders , security controls are not public\n\nSecurity is often implemented by establishing minimum standerds with enhanced secuity as an extra cost",
    "srd.20260123182329.1": " Of Paramount importance due to numerous regulatory  and legal reqs byt can vary signaificantly  depending on use case and location\n\n The added complexity  arises from laws /regulations based upon \n - where data is stored (data at rest)  \n - where the data is accessed and used (data in transit)\n \n",
    "srd.20260123182255.1": "Involes assigning jobs, roles, tasks and responsibilities and ensueing  they are satisfactorily  performed\n\nCloud complexity can add to goverance req's due to protection requirements nad the involvement of the third party provider.",
    "srd.20260123182401.1": "The contract and SLA should explicitly outline maintenance responsibilities across different\ncloud service \n\nIn a SaaS implementation, the cloud provider assumes all responsibilities for upgrades,\npatching, and maintenance. However, with PaaS and IaaS, certain responsibilities shift to the\ncloud customer, while the provider retains others.\n\nIt is crucial to establish version numbers for platforms and software whenever a system\nupgrade or maintenance is performed.\n\nVersioning enables tracking and testing of changes, with known versions available for fallback\nin case of issues with new versions. The SLA should specify an overlap period during which\nprevious version(s) remain available.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260123182422.1": "The SLA explicitly outlines minimum requirements for uptime, availability, processes, customer\nservice and support, security controls and requirements, auditing and reporting, and\npotentially other aspects that define the business relationship and its success.\n\nIn the event of a failure to meet the SLA requirements, the customer is entitled to receive\nfinancial benefits or may use this as the basis for contract termination if the cloud provider\ncannot rectify the performance issues to an acceptable level.\n\nThe contract and SLA should explicitly outline maintenance responsibilities across different\ncloud service \n\nIt is crucial to establish version numbers for platforms and software whenever a system\nupgrade or maintenance is performed.\n\nVersioning enables tracking and testing of changes, with known versions available for fallback\nin case of issues with new versions. The SLA should specify an overlap period during which\nprevious version(s) remain available.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260123182430.1": "Auditability in a cloud environment is a crucial aspect that the Cloud Security Professional\nmust focus on, considering that customers do not have the same level of control as they would\nin a proprietary and traditional data center model.\n",
    "srd.20260123182449.1": "These requirements are imposed on a business and its\noperations by laws, regulations, policies, or standards and\nguidelines.\n\nThese requirements are specific to the locality in which the\ncompany or application is based or specific to the nature of\nthe data and transactions conducted.\n\nNon-compliance with these requirements can result in\nfinancial, legal, or even criminal penalties, whether\nintentional or unintentional.\n",
    "srd.20260123182517.1": "A cloud outsourcing company offers businesses managed cloud services, providing them with\nvarious advantages.\n\nBy opting for cloud outsourcing, businesses gain the benefits of cloud computing along with a\nspecialised team to manage their cloud services.\n\nThese service providers can design elastic cloud architectures and migrate existing\ninfrastructure and legacy applications to the cloud.\n\nThe provider's team ensures that the business's systems and processes operate efficiently in\nthe cloud, handling any necessary maintenance.\n\nAfter evaluating your existing technology utilisation, the managed solution provider develops a\nunique solution using one, two, or all three of the different cloud computing service models.",
    "srd.20260123182600.1": "Data Science\nMachine Learning\nArtificial Intelligence (AI)\nBlockchain\nInternet Of Things (Iot)\nContainers\nQuantum Computing\nEdge Computing\nConfidential Computing\nDevSecOps\n",
    "srd.20260121195346.1": "Understand Security Concepts Relevant to Cloud Computing",
    "srd.20260127164712.1": " Crypto is  a critical component in the architecture of cloud security that ensures ...CIA ...\n\n     Confidentiality -\n\n    Integrity - \n\n    Availibiliity -\n\nEffective Key Management Systems (KMS)  help mitigate  risk associated woth data breaches and unauthorized access.\n\n Pricipals of crypto KMS's include key lifecycle management, key storage solutions and the implementation of robust encryption stratiegies",
    "srd.20260127173418.1": "Key management in cloud environments encompasses several critical responsibilities and\nprocesses designed to protect cryptographic keys throughout their lifecycle.\n\nFrom generation to destruction, every step is vital for maintaining the security of data.\n\nEffective lifecycle management ensures that keys are used appropriately and are never exposed\nto unauthorised entities. Key lifecycle stages include:",
    "srd.20260127173448.1": "Secure storage of cryptographic keys is paramount to maintaining the security of a cloud\nenvironment. Options for key storage include:",
    "srd.20260127173510.1": "Implementing robust encryption strategies is essential for protecting data at rest and in transit.\nConsiderations for encryption include:",
    "srd.20260127174345.1": "Encrypting stored data ensures that it remains confidential even if unauthorised\naccess to storage occurs. Key management should be designed to facilitate seamless\nencryption and decryption processes.",
    "srd.20260127174419.1": "Protecting data during transmission is crucial. Implementing protocols such as\nSSL/TLS and IPSec helps secure data as it moves between network points.",
    "srd.20260127174438.1": "This method ensures that data is encrypted on the sender's side and\nonly decrypted by the intended recipient, minimising the risk of interception during\ntransmission.",
    "srd.20260127174758.1": "Identity and Access Management (IAM), also known as User Access Management (UAM),\ninvolves the administration of user identities and the control of their access to IT resources,\nincluding networks, systems, applications, and files.\n\nIdentity management is crucial in any modern organisation, both for security and productivity.",
    "srd.20260127175124.1": "Privileged access refers to computer access with higher rights than a typical user within an\norganisation.\n\nIt is primarily used for tasks such as upgrading, maintaining, and configuring critical servers,\napplications, databases, and IT infrastructure.\n\nExamples of privileged access include access to service accounts, root access, and\nadministrator access.\n\nIn many organisations, the majority of company users are limited to utilising applications\nthrough their user interface.\n\nConsequently, any access to a server's command line interface may be considered privileged\naccess in some cases.\n\nCertain privileged accounts have access to the operating system's command line, while others\npossess elevated rights within applications, such as the ability to modify application\nconfigurations.",
    "srd.20260127171220.1": "Internally, it is possible to sanitise storage media because users have physical access to it.\nUsers can decide how to perform sanitisation, including the option to physically destroy the\nstorage media. Moreover, users can choose when to schedule media sanitisation and data\nerasure.\n\nHowever, in a cloud environment, this task becomes more challenging. The physical medium\ncannot be accessed directly, and data storage is shared and distributed among various users.\n\nAs a result, users do not have the ability to access the physical disks, and cloud service\nproviders (CSPs) do not typically allow users to physically destroy them.\n\nData in the cloud is regularly relocated and backed up, making it difficult to ascertain if a data\nitem has been completely removed from all copies. This raises significant concerns regarding\nprivacy and security.",
    "srd.20260127175741.1": "Over time, cloud storage involves the overwriting of erased data.\n\nOnce a specific area is assigned to and used by the same or another\ncustomer, the previously deleted data gets overwritten. These\ndeleted data areas are marked for reuse.\n\nIt is worth noting that data or fragments may still exist for some\ntime even after overwriting, as there is no fixed timeframe for their\ncomplete removal.\n\nTo ensure the safety and confidentiality of data, encryption is\ncrucial. All data stored in the cloud can be encrypted, provided that\ncryptographic keys are inaccessible or securely erased.",
    "srd.20260127175913.1": "During this process, cryptographic keys are destroyed (crypto\nshredded), effectively eliminating the key necessary for data\ndecryption.\n\nEncryption, along with data and media sanitisation and\noverwriting, is crucial for maintaining the privacy and security\nof your data.\n\nSecure cryptographic key erasure virtually guarantees the\nprevention of data retrieval.",
    "srd.20260127164832.1": "The integrated approach to network and virtualisation security in cloud environments is crucial\nfor protecting data, applications, and the underlying infrastructure from threats and\nunauthorised access.\n\nThis approach ensures that security measures are not siloed but are comprehensively designed\nto cover all aspects of the cloud environment, from the physical servers to the virtual networks\nand the applications running on them.\n\nIncorporating advanced strategies, cutting edge technologies, and established best practices,\nthis integrated security framework leverages the synergies between network security and\nvirtualisation.\n\nBy doing so, it enhances the overall security posture, minimises vulnerabilities, and ensures\nthat security protocols are agile enough to adapt to the dynamic nature of cloud technologies.\n\nThis results in a more resilient infrastructure capable of defending against both conventional\nsecurity threats and emerging challenges in the digital landscape.",
    "srd.20260127185748.1": "Network security in cloud computing must address the challenges of\nmultitenancy, distributed resources, and the dynamic scaling of\nservices. Key aspects include:",
    "srd.20260127185821.1": "These are used to define security rules that govern the ingress and\negress of traffic at the virtual machine, network interface, and\nsubnet levels.\n\nNSGs act as virtual firewalls providing an essential layer of security\nto cloud resources.",
    "srd.20260127190034.1": "Effective network security requires continuous monitoring and analysis of network traffic to\ndetect anomalies, potential breaches, and DDoS attacks.\n\nTools used for this purpose analyse traffic flows and can automatically trigger protective\nmeasures when suspicious activities are detected.",
    "srd.20260127190057.1": "Geofencing uses geographical location data to control access to resources, enhancing security\nby restricting access based on physical location parameters.\n\nThe Zero Trust model, on the other hand, assumes no inherent trust within the network,\nrequiring verification and authentication for every access request, regardless of origin.",
    "srd.20260127191012.1": "Virtualisation allows for the efficient utilisation of physical resources to support multiple virtual\nenvironments. It also introduces specific security concerns that must be addressed:",
    "srd.20260127191154.1": "The hypervisor, or virtual machine monitor (VMM), is a primary target for attacks due to its\nrole in managing virtual machines.\n\nEnsuring the security of the hypervisor is paramount, involving regular updates, strict access\ncontrols, and monitoring to prevent breaches.",
    "srd.20260127191214.1": "Containers offer a lightweight alternative to full virtual machines, providing an isolated\nenvironment for running applications.\n\nSecurity for containers involves managing access rights, securing container orchestration\nplatforms like Kubernetes, and using trusted images.",
    "srd.20260127191647.1": "These technologies reduce the attack surface by limiting the lifespan of computing resources.\n\nEphemeral computing resources exist only while needed, minimising the window of\nopportunity for attacks.\n\nServerless computing abstracts the server layer, shifting security responsibilities to the cloud\nprovider, focusing on securing the code and data inputs/outputs.",
    "srd.20260127192033.1": "Combining network and virtualisation security involves several integrated strategies:",
    "srd.20260127192219.1": "These techniques divide the network into distinct\nsecurity segments down to the individual workload level, limiting the lateral movement of\nattackers within the network.",
    "srd.20260127192339.1": "This involves using a comprehensive solution that\nincorporates firewall, VPN, antivirus, and intrusion detection functionalities to protect both\nvirtual and physical networks.",
    "srd.20260127192509.1": "Automation plays a critical role in maintaining security across\ndynamic cloud environments. Automated security policies ensure that security settings are\nconsistently applied across all virtual instances, adapting to changes in the network\narchitecture.",
    "srd.20260127171245.1": "Automation plays a critical role in maintaining security across\ndynamic cloud environments. Automated security policies ensure that security settings are\nconsistently applied across all virtual instances, adapting to changes in the network\narchitecture.",
    "srd.20260127171313.1": "Security hygiene, also known as cybersecurity hygiene,\nencompasses a set of routine actions taken by businesses and\nindividuals to ensure the safety of users, devices, networks, and\ndata.\n\nCyber hygiene aims to protect sensitive information and defend it\nagainst theft and threats.\n\nThe concept operates in a manner similar to personal hygiene,\nwhere individuals can maintain their health by taking preventive\nmeasures such as flossing to prevent cavities and handwashing to\ncombat the spread of disease.\n\nSimilarly, organisations can maintain good cybersecurity health and\nprevent data breaches and other security incidents by practicing\nproactive cyber hygiene.",
    "srd.20260127193510.1": "Traditionally, network security focused on patching known vulnerabilities, but with increasing\nnetwork complexity, businesses often struggle to promptly patch every device, system, and\napplication.\n\nCybercriminals exploit delays in patching, typically taking organisations 100 120 days to apply\nfixes, during which systems can be compromised and sensitive data accessed.\n\nContinuous patching can be challenging, leading many businesses to adopt a monthly patching\nschedule, following the example of manufacturers like Microsoft who release fixes once a\nmonth.\n\nHowever, given the increasing number of reported vulnerabilities, checking for updates once a\nmonth or even once a week may no longer be sufficient.",
    "srd.20260127193650.1": "A set of procedures known as cybersecurity hygiene is employed by businesses to manage the\nmost prevalent and pervasive cybersecurity threats.\n\nIdentify and prioritise the most critical organisational services, products, and resources. Risks\nto the company's essential services and products should be identified, prioritised, and\nmitigated.\n\nDevelop an incident response strategy. Conduct cybersecurity education and awareness\ncampaigns.\n\nEstablish network surveillance and security. Manage user access accounts and restrict access\nbased on the principle of least privilege.\n\nUtilise standardised, secure settings, and control technological\nchanges.\n\nImplement strategies to protect data and ensure its recovery in case\nof loss or breach.\n\nImplement robust measures to prevent malware exposures and\ntrack their origins to safeguard data and system integrity effectively.\n\nManage cyber threats related to external dependencies and\nsuppliers.\n\nMonitor threats and vulnerabilities to address and mitigate risks\npromptly.",
    "srd.20260121195512.1": "Understand Design Principles of Secure Cloud Computing",
    "srd.20260127195415.1": "\nThe data lifecycle consists of the following key stages:\n\n1. Create: This stage involves generating new digital content or\nmodifying existing content.\n\n2. Store: Data is stored in a dedicated repository, typically right after its\ncreation.\n\n3. Use: Data is utilised, viewed, processed, or otherwise engaged in\nvarious activities.\n\n4. Share: Information is made accessible to others, including customers,\npartners, and users.\n\n5. Archive: Data transitions from active use to long term storage.\n\n6. Destroy: Data is permanently eliminated using physical or digital\nmethods.",
    "srd.20260127195523.1": "Business continuity management (BCM) is an essential process that involves the proactive and\nsystematic review and management of potential threats and risks to critical business functions\nand service availability.\n\nAs a crucial component of an organisation's comprehensive risk management strategy, BCM is\nregularly updated and refined to address new and evolving threats.\n\nThe primary objective of business continuity management is to ensure the uninterrupted\ncontinuity of business operations during and after a disruption, whether due to natural\ndisasters, technological failures, or human induced events.\n\nThis process helps organisations prepare for, respond to, and recover from incidents,\nminimising downtime and operational impact while safeguarding key business processes and\nmaintaining customer service and trust.",
    "srd.20260127195546.1": "Disaster recovery (DR) planning is the process\nthrough which appropriate plans and measures are\nimplemented to ensure that in the event of a\ndisaster.\n\nThe business can respond effectively to recover\ncritical and essential operations to a state of partial\nor full service in a minimal amount of time.\n\nThe primary purpose of DR planning is to swiftly\nestablish, reestablish, or recover affected areas or\nelements of the business following a disaster.",
    "srd.20260127195634.1": " Business Impact Analysis (BIA) is a systematic procedure used to identify and evaluate the\npotential consequences of a disruption to critical business operations caused by emergencies,\naccidents, or disasters.\n\nAn organisation's business continuity plan (BCP) must include a BIA. It consists of an\ninvestigative component to identify threats and vulnerabilities and a planning component to\ndevelop risk reduction strategies.\n\nThe result of a Business Impact Analysis is a report that outlines the unique risks specific to the\nenterprise under examination.\n\nIn an organisation, every part relies on the continued functioning of others, a fundamental\naspect of a BIA.\n\nIn the event of a disaster, certain components are more critical than others, requiring a larger\nallocation of financial and operational resources.",
    "srd.20260127195716.1": "In the adoption of cloud computing, cost is a recognised key driver. The cost benefit analysis\ndepends on the following factors:\n\n- Resource Pooling\n\n- Shift from Capex to Opex\n\n- Factors in Time and Efficiencies\n\n- Includes Depreciation\n\n- Reduction in Maintenance and Configuration Time\n\n- Shift in Focus\n\n- Utilities Costs\n\n- Software and Licensing Costs\n\n- Pay Per Usage\n\n",
    "srd.20260127195739.1": "The impact of a cloud investment on a company is often measured by its Cloud Return on\nInvestment (ROI).\n\nReturn on Investment (ROI), which signifies that a business decision has positively affected the\norganisation's bottom line, is typically regarded as a success indicator for any project.\n\nThe straightforward increase in an investment's value over time serves as the ROI benchmark.\n\nAn ROI is considered positive when the financial gain exceeds the initial investment.\n\nSeveral factors influence Cloud ROI, including the initial investment, the rate at which returns\noccur, and the cost savings resulting from the investment.\n\nFor example, transitioning to a public cloud provider reduces upfront costs but increases\nmonthly expenses.",
    "srd.20260127200016.1": "Transitioning to cloud computing or managing cloud services can enhance the security of a\ncustomer's information and processes.\n\nNevertheless, certain challenges exist in cloud computing, such as vendor lock in, portability,\nand interoperability.\n\nImplementing a vendor management strategy that clearly defines the responsibilities of each\nparty and establishes Service Level Agreements (SLAs) can help address these issues.\n\nFor complex or costly systems, the Request for Proposal (RFP) process can be utilised to\naccurately define customer requirements.\n\nThe RFP criteria should include a section on security standards, and the selection of bidders\ncan also consider security requirements.\n\nAny vendor unable to meet the client's security requirements can be promptly disqualified.\n\nThe following are the elements of the functional security requirements depicted as a trianle, i.e. with two opposing bases supporting a balenced objective:\n\n\n                                            - Portability\n\n\n\n\n- Vendor Lock-in                                                                - Interoperability\n\n\nDue to varying responsibilities between cloud customers and the unique characteristics of different\ndeployment models, each cloud category will have both common and distinct security\nconsiderations.",
    "srd.20260127200101.1": "The following are several key security concerns that are involved in IaaS security:\n\n- Multitenancy\n- Co-Location\n- Hypervisor Security and Attacks\n- Network Security\n- Virtual Machine Attacks\n- Virtual Switch Attacks\n- Denial of Service Attacks\n\nIaaS security places the majority of the security responsibility on the user.\n\nIaaS service providers protect their designated areas, which include servers, virtualisation, storage,\nand networking.\n\nThe security of the operating system and everything built upon it, including the tasks of PaaS and\nSaaS implementations, falls under the responsibility of the IaaS client.\n\nFollowing the shared responsibility model, everything above the hypervisor is the customer's\nresponsibility.\n\nThe specific responsibilities along this continuum can vary between the CSP and the client, as in\nother delivery models, and must be understood in each case.",
    "srd.20260127200241.1": "In a PaaS solution, the PaaS service provider remains responsible for maintaining the security of the\nunderlying infrastructure, which includes servers, operating systems, virtualisation, storage, and\nnetworking.\n\nThe security of any solutions created, the data used by their application, and the user\nresponsibilities within a SaaS application for user access and utilisation of the developed solutions\nare all within the developer's purview.\n\nThe customer holds accountability for the data, APIs, and applications under the shared\nresponsibility model, and possibly some middleware.",
    "srd.20260127200135.1": "In a SaaS system, security options are somewhat limited, as the SaaS provider primarily manages\nsecurity solutions.\n\nThe SaaS provider is responsible for maintaining the security of their service's infrastructure,\noperating system, applications, networking, and data storage.\n\nThe customer retains control over their data and may have some responsibilities related to the APIs\nunder the shared responsibility model, while the Cloud Service Provider (CSP) manages all other\nlayers.\n\nSaaS users also bear some responsibilities.\n\nIt is crucial to have as much knowledge as possible about the SaaS provider's security policies and\npractices before subscribing to a service, whether you are a company or an individual.",
    "srd.20260127200429.1": "Design patterns for cloud based applications are often overlooked until a company reaches a\ncertain scale.\n\nDespite the availability of various design patterns to choose from, effectively managing scale\nwhen needed remains one of the most challenging tasks.\n\nAny application experiencing rapid growth encounters both benefits and drawbacks related to\nrevenue growth and technological challenges.\n\nBy leveraging a few design patterns, any cloud based application can enhance its fault\ntolerance and resilience to issues commonly associated with increased traffic, ultimately\nleading to better scalability.\n\nImplementing these patterns not only improves system reliability but also ensures a more\nseamless and efficient user experience, crucial for maintaining service quality as demand\ngrows.",
    "srd.20260127200718.1": "- Data in Transit ->\n- Asset Protection and Resilence ->\n- Separation between Customers ->\n- Governace Framework ->\n- Operational Security  ->\n- Personal Security ->\n- Secure Development ->\n- Supply Chain Security ->\n- Secure User Mangement ->\n- Identity and Authentication ->\n- External Interface Protection ->\n- Secure Service Administration ->\n- Audit Information and Alerting ->\n- Secure Use of the Service\n",
    "srd.20260127200757.1": "The AWS Well Architected Framework is a valuable tool that evaluates your cloud based\napplications to identify improvements based on best practices. \n\nIt offers several key benefits, including:\n\n- Operational Excellence\n- Security\n- Reliability\n- Performance Efficiency\n- Cost Optimisation\n",
    "srd.20260127200843.1": "The Cloud Security Alliance is a nonprofit organisation established to promote the adoption of\nbest practices for ensuring security assurance within cloud computing.\n\nAdditionally, it aims to provide information on how cloud computing can enhance the security\nof other computing platforms.\n\nThe Cloud Security Alliance comprises numerous subject matter experts from various fields\nwho are dedicated to the following objectives:\n\n1. Promoting a common understanding of essential security criteria and attestation of assurance\namong both cloud computing users and providers.\n\n2. Promote impartial research on best cloud security practices and launch educational campaigns\nto increase awareness of proper cloud computing applications and security.\n\n3. Compiling lists of concerns and recommendations for enhancing cloud security assurance.",
    "srd.20260128171425.1": "The Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) Enterprise Architecture provides a comprehensive framework for developing secure cloud infrastructures, integrating best practices and methodologies to enhance cloud security.\n\nOverview of CSA Enterprise Architecture\n\nThe Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) is dedicated to defining standards, certifications, and best practices to ensure a secure cloud computing environment. The Enterprise Architecture (EA) is a key component of CSA's efforts, serving as both a methodology and a set of tools designed to help organizations assess and improve their cloud security posture.\n\nKey Features of the CSA Enterprise Architecture\n\n1. \nFramework for Security: The EA provides a structured approach to designing and implementing secure cloud infrastructures. It combines business drivers with security infrastructure to enhance the value proposition of cloud services within an enterprise model. \n\n2.\nIntegration of Standards: The CSA EA leverages established architecture models such as TOGAF, ITIL, SABSA, and Jericho. This integration allows organizations to utilize a comprehensive approach that aligns with industry standards and best practices. \n\n3.\nAssessment and Roadmapping: The EA enables security architects and risk management professionals to assess their current security capabilities, identify areas for improvement, and create roadmaps for technology adoption. This is crucial for aligning security measures with business needs. \n\n4.\nMapping to Cloud Controls Matrix (CCM): The CSA EA is closely linked to the Cloud Controls Matrix (CCM), which provides a set of security controls for cloud providers. This mapping helps organizations ensure that their EA is operating securely and meets compliance requirements. \n\n5.\nReference Guide: The Enterprise Architecture Reference Guide offers detailed insights into each domain of the EA, helping organizations understand how to apply these principles effectively. It serves as a resource for evaluating cloud providers and security technology vendors against a common set of capabilities. \n\n\n\nPractical Applications\n\nOrganizations can use the CSA Enterprise Architecture to:\n\nDevelop secure and interoperable identity, access, and compliance management configurations.\nIdentify reusable security patterns and assess various cloud providers against a standardized set of capabilities.\nCreate a common roadmap to meet their cloud security needs, guided by regulations and standards such as ISO-27002 and COBIT. \n\nIn summary, the CSA Enterprise Architecture is a vital resource for organizations looking to enhance their cloud security frameworks, providing a structured approach to integrating security into their cloud strategies. For more detailed information, you can refer to the Enterprise Architecture Reference Guide and other resources provided by the CSA.\n\nhttps://ea.cloudsecurityalliance.org/",
    "srd.20260127200916.1": "DevOps Security, also known as DevSecOps, encompasses a set of methodologies, cultural\npractices, and technologies that integrate software development (Dev), IT operations (Ops),\nand security (Sec).\n\nIts goal is to enhance an organisation's ability to deliver applications and services at a high\nvelocity while maintaining security.\n\nDevOps practices enable the rapid deployment of new application features and facilitate\nfrequent updates through incremental changes.\n\nThese processes often involve extensive automation, with applications structured around\nmultiple microservices and delivered in containerised and cloud environments.\n\nIn conjunction with cloud based elastic infrastructure, DevOps can respond to demand spikes\nthrough auto scaling procedures that provision additional computing resources, such as virtual\nmachines or containers, and deploy more instances of an application as needed.",
    "srd.20260121195633.1": "Evaluate Cloud Service Providers\n",
    "srd.20260128194355.1": "The following are security standards, frameworks, and controls commonly applied to cloud\nenvironments:",
    "srd.20260128195503.1": "Provides high level implementation guidance for controls specified in 27017 standards ",
    "srd.20260128195127.1": "Provides high level implementation guidance for controls specified in 27017 standards ",
    "srd.20260128194749.1": "ISO/IEC 27017 provides controls and implementation guidance designed for both Cloud Service\nProviders and cloud service customers.\n\nThis standard offers guidelines for information security controls relevant to the delivery and\nutilisation of cloud services.\n\nISO/IEC 27017 introduces additional controls along with implementation\nguidance specifically tailored to cloud services.",
    "srd.20260128194539.1": "SOC 1, SOC 2, and SOC 3 are different types of System and Organization Controls AND reports that provide assurance regarding the internal controls of service organizations.\n\n\nPurpose: SOC 1 reports focus on internal controls relevant to a user entity\u2019s financial reporting. They are essential for organizations that handle financial transactions or data, such as payroll processors or payment processors.\n\nThere are two types of SOC 1 reports:\nType I: Evaluates the design of controls at a specific point in time.\nType II: Assesses the operational effectiveness of those controls over a specified period (usually 6-12 months). \n\n\nSOC 2\nPurpose: SOC 2 reports are designed for service organizations that manage customer data, focusing on the security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy of that data. This report is particularly relevant for technology and cloud service providers.\nType of Report: Similar to SOC 1, SOC 2 also has Type I and Type II reports, with Type II providing a more comprehensive evaluation over time. \n\n\nSOC 3\nPurpose: SOC 3 reports are a public-facing version of SOC 2 reports. They summarize the results of a SOC 2 audit without disclosing detailed information about the controls or testing procedures. This report is often used for marketing purposes to demonstrate a commitment to security and compliance.\nType of Report: SOC 3 reports are always Type II and can be shared publicly, making them suitable for organizations that want to provide assurance to clients and stakeholders without revealing sensitive information. \n\n\n",
    "srd.20260128194614.1": "The NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 5 provides a comprehensive catalog of security and privacy controls for information systems and organizations. It is designed to protect operations, assets, individuals, and the nation from threats such as cyberattacks, human errors, natural disasters, and privacy risks.\n\nThe framework is flexible and customizable, allowing organizations to tailor controls based on mission needs, laws, regulations, and risk management strategies. It supports the Risk Management Framework (RMF) and aligns with other standards like the NIST Cybersecurity Framework and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 through mapping and crosswalks.\n\nKey Control Families include:\n\nAccess Control (AC) \u2013 Managing permissions and user access.\n\nAudit and Accountability (AU) \u2013 Logging and monitoring activities.\n\nIncident Response (IR) \u2013 Detecting, reporting, and responding to incidents.\n\nSystem and Communications Protection (SC) \u2013 Safeguarding data in transit and at rest.\n\nSupply Chain Risk Management (SR) \u2013 Addressing third-party and vendor risks.\n\nElments also support Benchmarking and Automation support",
    "srd.20260128194645.1": "PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard) is a\nsecurity standard established by major credit card companies\nincluding Visa, MasterCard, and American Express.\n\nIt mandates that all organisations involved in handling cardholder\ndata whether they are accepting, transmitting, or storing it must\ncomply with this standard.\n\nThis compliance is mandatory irrespective of the organisation's size\nor the volume of transactions it processes.\n\nThis ensures a uniform level of protection for personal financial\ninformation across all platforms and industries.",
    "srd.20260128201616.1": "System and subsystem product certification assesses the security claims made for a system and\nits components.",
    "srd.20260128201709.1": "The CC is an international set of specifications and guidelines developed for assessing\ninformation security products to ensure they meet an agreed upon security standard for\ngovernment entities and agencies.\n\nThe CC has two key components:\n\n1. Protection profiles.\n\n2. The Evaluation Assurance Levels (EALs).",
    "srd.20260128201917.1": "Federal Information Processing Standard (FIPS) 140-2\n\nEncryption and cryptography are primarily used to maintain the integrity and confidentiality of\ncritical data, particularly in various cloud computing deployment services.\n\nThis ensures secure handling and storage of sensitive information across platforms.\n\nThe Federal Information Processing Standards (FIPS) 140 Publication Series is issued by the\nNational Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).\n\nIt establishes unified standards and requirements for cryptographic modules. This alignment\nensures consistent security practices across cryptographic implementations.\n\nThe FIPS 140-2 standard provides four distinct levels of security designed to accommodate a\nwide range of potential applications and environments.\n\nRelted specifications include the following:\n\n- Cryptograhic Module Specifications\n- Authentication\n- Cryptographic Key Management\n- Cryptographic Module Ports\n- Physical Security\n- Design Assurance\n- Interfaces, Roles, and Services\n- Operational Environment\n- Controls and Mitigating Techniques Against Attacks",
    "srd.20260113194456.1": "Domain 2: Cloud Data Security\n20%",
    "srd.20260203171101.1": "This encompasses the entire lifecycle of data in the cloud, including\ncreation, storage, use, sharing, archiving, and destruction. Effective data lifecycle management\ninvolves policies and procedures for each stage.",
    "srd.20260203162630.1": "Data is constantly being generated, stored, utilised, and transmitted, and when it is no longer\nvaluable, it is destroyed.\n\nIn such a dynamic environment, it is useful to model the various stages that information\nundergoes.\n\nThis model provides a practical approach to identify broad categories of data risks and their\ncorresponding protections, rather than trying to pinpoint every individual data element within\nan organisation.\n\nThe secure cloud data lifecycle is typically linear, though some data may not pass through every\nstage. Additionally, data can exist in multiple phases simultaneously.\"",
    "srd.20260203162856.1": "Creation of Data\n\nData is 'created' when it is first entered into a system or when existing data is modified. This\nincludes a variety of scenarios such as a user creating and saving a document, a process that\ngathers data through an API and stores it in a database, or a user who opens and updates a\nshared document.\n\nWhile the data lifecycle often involves repeated modifications, it is also common for data to be\ncreated once and never updated.\n\nControls in the Creation Phase\n\nData classification is a crucial security control at this stage. It allows an organisation to assess the value of the data and apply appropriate security measures. During creation, data must be classified either by its owner or the person creating it.",
    "srd.20260203162912.1": "Storing Data\n\nThe act of storing data involves placing it in a location where it can be retrieved later, such as on a Solid State Drive (SSD), in hard copy records, or within an application database. Often, the storage phase occurs simultaneously with the creation phase. For example, a user might upload a new document to a file sharing service as soon as it is created.\n\nControls in the Storage Phase\n\nWhen data is stored, especially in cloud services, it may need protection during transit. This is particularly relevant when data must traverse public networks to reach an organisation's cloud applications.\n\nTo safeguard data in transit, various protections can be employed, such as Transport Layer\nSecurity (TLS), Secure Shell (SSH), or a Virtual Private Network (VPN). These measures ensure\nthat the data remains secure as it moves to its storage destination.\n",
    "srd.20260203163028.1": "Using Data\n\nThe 'Use' phase encompasses accessing, viewing, and processing data, often referred to as\nhandling. Data management during this phase can take various forms, such as through web\napplication usage, retrieving data via an API, or reading and manipulating files.\n\nControls in the Use Phase\n\nTypically, the 'Use' phase is the most prolonged part of the data lifecycle, analogous to\nhow systems spend most of their time in the operations and maintenance phase when\nactively utilised.\n\nThe controls in this phase are extensive and proportional to its duration and complexity.\nThey include data flow management with tools like Data Loss Prevention (DLP) and\nInformation Rights Management (IRM), system access controls such as authorisation and\naccess reviews, and the use of network monitoring tools.",
    "srd.20260203163036.1": "Sharing Data\n\nThe 'Share' phase involves granting access to data to other users or entities who require it. Not every decision to share data is made explicitly, and not all data is shared.\n\nHighly classified data may be strictly accessible to only a few key personnel, while less sensitive information might be more broadly shared, for instance, by storing it in a collaborative application.\n\nControls in the Share Phase\n\nAccess controls form the backbone of security measures during the 'Share' phase. These\ncontrols are both proactive and reactive.\n\n\nProactive measures include procedures for granting access and implementing role based\naccess authorisation.\n\nThese controls ensure that data is shared appropriately and securely, maintaining the\nintegrity and confidentiality of sensitive information.\n\nReactive controls, such as Information Rights Management (IRM), Data Loss Prevention\n(DLP), and access reviews, play a crucial role in the 'Share' phase.\n\nThese controls are designed to detect unauthorised sharing of data. In certain situations,\nthey can even prevent data from leaving the organisation's control.\n\nBy monitoring and responding to data sharing activities, these reactive measures help\nmaintain the security and integrity of the data throughout its lifecycle",
    "srd.20260203163042.1": "Archiving Data\n\nArchiving data involves storing it in a form that allows for long term retrieval. This process is particularly important for certain types of organisations and data, such as healthcare or\nfinancial institutions, which often have mandatory data retention requirements.\n\nControls in the Archive Phase\n\nSince archiving is essentially a specific form of storage, the data controls in the archive\nphase are similar to those in the storage phase.\n\nHowever, due to the extended duration of archival storage, there are additional\nconsiderations, especially regarding encryption.\n\nAlternatively, it may require the labor intensive process of decrypting data with the old key\nand then re encrypting it with the new key. These measures ensure the continued\nprotection and accessibility of archived data.",
    "srd.20260203163050.1": "Secure Destruction of Data\n\nData that is no longer valuable or subject to retention requirements must be securely\ndestroyed.\n\nThe methods of destruction vary in security level, ranging from lower security options like\nsimply deleting filesystem pointers and reusing disks, to more secure methods.\n\nThese secure methods include overwriting disks, physically destroying storage media, or\nemploying crypto shredding (cryptographic erasure), where the keying material is securely\nerased, rendering the encrypted data inaccessible.\n\nControls in the Destroy Phase\n\nChoosing the appropriate method of destruction involves balancing two key\nconsiderations: the options available within the cloud service environment and the value\nof the data being destroyed.\n\nFor data of low sensitivity, such as public information, extraordinary destruction methods\nmay not be necessary.\n\nHowever, for high value data, such as Personally Identifiable Information (PII), more\nsecure destruction methods are warranted to ensure that the data cannot be recovered\nor misused after it is supposed to be erased.\n",
    "srd.20260203165336.1": "\nData dispersion is a technique used in cloud computing to enhance data security and\navailability.\n\nIt involves breaking data into smaller chunks and distributing them across various physical\nstorage devices.\n\nThis concept is similar to striping in RAID (Redundant Arrays of Independent Disks), where data\nis divided and written across multiple disks.\n\nHowever, cloud based data dispersion also incorporates erasure coding, enabling data\nreconstruction in case some segments are lost.\n\nErasure coding works similarly to calculating parity bits in RAID storage. When data is written, it is divided into segments; a mathematical calculation is performed on these segments, and the  result (a parity bit) is stored alongside the data.\n\nIf some segments are lost, the parity bit and the remaining segments can be used to\nreconstruct the missing data, akin to solving an algebraic equation.\n\nSecurity Implications of Data Dispersion\n\nIn cloud environments, data dispersion can influence an organisation's security both positively\nand negatively.\n\nThe primary advantage is increased availability: even if a physical storage device fails, the data can be rebuilt.\n\nAdditionally, if a physical device is compromised, it would not contain a complete, usable copy\nof the data.\n\nHowever, challenges arise from the dispersion of data segments across different geographic\nlocations, each with varying regulatory or legal frameworks.\n\nThis dispersion can subject an organisation to unexpected laws and requirements. Many Cloud\nService Providers (CSPs) now offer geographic restriction features to allow clients to benefit\nfrom dispersion without added regulatory or legal complexities.\n\nRole of the CCSP in Managing Data Dispersion\n\nFor a Certified Cloud Security Professional (CCSP), configuring cloud services appropriately is\ncrucial to meet an organisation's compliance objectives.\n\nFor instance, organisations subject to the EU's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) may\nprefer to keep data within the European Union rather than dispersing it globally.\n\nThis configuration might be straightforward for certain services or could require complex IT\nproject planning and system architecture.\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260203170416.1": "Understanding data flows in cloud computing is a key part of this certification, as it relates to the design, implementation, and management of a secure cloud environment.",
    "srd.20260203170634.1": "This refers to data that is moving across the network. In cloud environments,\nthis could be data moving between user devices and the cloud, or between different cloud\nservices.",
    "srd.20260203170643.1": "Data at rest is data that is stored in cloud services. Securing data at rest involves\nencryption of the stored data, access controls, and possibly the use of specific storage services that offer enhanced security features.",
    "srd.20260203170652.1": "This refers to data that is being processed or used by applications. Protecting data\nin use is challenging and often involves ensuring that the underlying infrastructure (like virtual machines or containers) is secure.",
    "srd.20260203171120.1": "Understanding the legal and compliance aspects of data in\nthe cloud is crucial. This includes data residency requirements, compliance with regulations like GDPR or HIPAA, and understanding the shared responsibility model in cloud computing.",
    "srd.20260203171134.1": "Understanding different cloud data architectures, such as multi tenant\nmodels, hybrid clouds, and the use of public, private, and community cloud services, is important.",
    "srd.20260203171145.1": "Implementing DLP strategies in the cloud to prevent data breaches or\nunintended data leaks is essential. This can involve the use of DLP software, encryption, and\ntraining for staff on data handling best practices.",
    "srd.20260203183919.1": "Understanding the diverse types of storage and the specific threats each faces is crucial for\nmaintaining robust security frameworks.\n\nCertified Cloud Security Professionals (CCSP) need to be adept at navigating the complexities of\nstorage in various cloud service models Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), Platform as a\nService (PaaS), and Software as a Service (SaaS) and implementing strategies to mitigate\nassociated risks.",
    "srd.20260203184141.1": "\nEphemeral Storage (IaaS)\n\nEphemeral storage provides temporary data storage that only persists during the active life of\nthe cloud instance.\n\nCommonly implemented as RAM or other volatile memory systems, this storage type is wiped\nclean upon instance termination.\n\nCommon Use:\n\nThis is typically used for temporary data that requires quick access and processing but does\nnot need to be saved permanently, such as session states.\n\nSecurity Threats:\n\nThe primary risk is data loss if the instance is terminated unexpectedly. Vulnerability to\ncertain types of cyber attacks that target volatile memory is also a concern.\n",
    "srd.20260203184206.1": "\nVolume Storage (IaaS)\n\nVolume storage emulates physical drive storage systems in a cloud environment, providing\npersistent storage for applications and systems.\n\nCommon Use:\n\nIt is versatile, used for databases, application files, or any data needing persistent storage\nacross instance reboots.\n\nSecurity Threats:\n\nRisks include unauthorised data access and breaches, especially if encryption and access\ncontrols are not properly implemented.\n",
    "srd.20260203184236.1": "\nObject Storage (IaaS/PaaS)\n\nManages data as discrete units (objects) within a distributed storage environment. Each object\nis a self contained repository that includes the data, metadata, and a unique identifier.\n\nCommon Use:\n\nIdeal for managing large amounts of unstructured data, such as photos, video content, and\nbackup files.\n\nSecurity Threats:\n\nExposed to unauthorised access, data manipulation, and potential compliance violations if\ndata management practices are not aligned with regulatory requirements.",
    "srd.20260203184306.1": "\nDatabase and BLOB Storage (PaaS)\n\nThis storage type encompasses structured data storage options such as SQL databases and\nunstructured data storage such as Binary Large Objects (BLOBs) which handle large data files.\n\nCommon Use:\n\nDatabases are crucial for applications requiring structured query language (SQL) interaction,\nwhile BLOBs are used for storing large media files, documents, and other binary data.\n\nSecurity Threats:\n\nVulnerable to both structured query language (SQL) injection attacks and unauthorised\naccess to unstructured data.",
    "srd.20260203184326.1": "5.\nSaaS Storage\n\nIn the SaaS model, the cloud service provider manages all aspects of the storage\ninfrastructure. Storage types may be highly specialised to support specific applications offered\nas a service.\n\nCommon Use:\n\nUsed in applications such as CRM systems, email services, and collaborative tools where the\nend user interacts with data through a web interface.\n\nSecurity Threats:\n\nRisks include loss of control over data management and potential data leakage, especially if\nthe CSP fails to enforce robust security measures.",
    "srd.20260203185050.1": "General Threats to Cloud Storage and Mitigations\n\nUnauthorised Access\n\nImplement comprehensive access controls, use strong authentication mechanisms, and\nencrypt data both at rest and in transit to protect against unauthorised access.\n\nRegulatory Noncompliance\n\nEnsure that all storage solutions comply with relevant regulations such as GDPR, HIPAA, or\nCCPA. Regular audits and compliance checks can help mitigate this risk.\n\nJurisdictional Issues\n\nBe aware of data sovereignty laws that may affect where data can be legally stored and\naccessed. Choose CSPs that provide region specific storage options to comply with legal\nrequirements.",
    "srd.20260203185453.1": "Integrated Encryption and Key Management is crucial for ensuring that sensitive data stored in\nthe cloud is inaccessible to unauthorised users.\n\nEncryption transforms plain text into unreadable ciphertext, requiring cryptographic keys to\ndecrypt.\n\nKey management is the systematic management of these keys, including their creation,\nstorage, use, and destruction.",
    "srd.20260203185657.1": "Directly implemented within cloud applications, this method encrypts data entered by the user\nbefore it is stored, giving organisations direct control over encryption keys and limiting access by cloud service providers (CSPs).",
    "srd.20260203185833.1": "Focused on protecting data stored in databases, it\nencrypts entire files or specific columns/tables.\n\nDatabase encryption is particularly effective in Platform\nas a Service (PaaS) environments, where consumers control the encryption keys.",
    "srd.20260203185917.1": "Executed at the file system level by client applications,\nthis allows the encryption and decryption of files on the\nfly, managed directly by the user, thus securing data from\nother tenants and CSP personnel.",
    "srd.20260203190018.1": "Encrypts entire data volumes, with keys managed by the consumer,\nsafeguarding data against physical theft and unauthorised access by\nCSP staff or other tenants.\n",
    "srd.20260203190131.1": "A versatile tool that supports various cryptographic operations\nand the lifecycle management of keys within cloud environments.\n\nIt ensures that encryption operations and key management are\nconfined within a secure boundary, reducing exposure to external\nthreats.",
    "srd.20260203190417.1": "Allows customers to manage their encryption keys, integrating\nwith Cloud KMS for enhanced control over data encryption,\nparticularly concerning data at rest, thus adhering to specific\nsecurity policies and compliance requirements.",
    "srd.20260203190512.1": "Keys are generated using secure methods like cryptographically\nsecure pseudo random number generators to ensure they are\nstrong and random.",
    "srd.20260203190624.1": "Keys must be stored in secure environments, such as encrypted key vaults or using\nhardware security modules (HSMs), with restricted access to ensure they are protected\nfrom unauthorised use.",
    "srd.20260203190630.1": "Proper protocols and permissions must be in place to ensure that keys are used correctly\nand only by authorised personnel, with all usage logged and monitored.",
    "srd.20260203190651.1": "Sharing keys is generally discouraged but, if necessary, should be done through secure\nchannels, ensuring keys are transferred to legitimate parties only.",
    "srd.20260203190926.1": "Old keys that are no longer actively used but still needed for decryption of historical data\nshould be securely archived.",
    "srd.20260203191031.1": "Managing Secrets and Certificates\n\nSecret Manager\n\nManages and secures sensitive data such as API keys and passwords. Features like access\ncontrol and audit logging help maintain data integrity and confidentiality.\n\nCertificate Manager\n\nA system for managing digital certificates that ensures secure storage and handling of\nprivate keys and certificates.",
    "srd.20260203191153.1": "Hashing, often referred to as \"one way encryption,\" plays a crucial role in upholding the\nintegrity principle of the CIA triad Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability.\n\nINTEGRITY\n\nIntegrity focuses on preventing, detecting, and rectifying unauthorised or unintended changes\nto data, whether they occur accidentally or maliciously.\n\n\n\nIn cloud environments, hashes offer various SECCURITY SERVICES:\n\nVerify the accuracy of data copies, such as backups.\n\nEnsure the integrity of messages, such as emails\n\nWidely implemented in security tools to detect changes indicative of system compromise.\n\nHashing is a fundamental security measure for maintaining data integrity and is essential in\ncloud security practices.",
    "srd.20260203191344.1": "Hash Functions\n\nHash functions are cryptographic algorithms that take data of any length and perform\nmathematical operations to create a unique hash value.\n\nThis process can be repeated, and in the future, two hash values can be compared. If the input\ndata has been altered, mismatched hash values indicate data changes.",
    "srd.20260203191501.1": "\nHashes are integral to digital signatures, which enable users to verify the integrity and source\nof files, messages, or data, including applications.\n\nThe signing party computes a hash and uses its private key to encrypt the hash value.\n\nThe receiving party, such as a user receiving a message, downloading an app, or accessing\nsoftware from a repository, computes a hash of the received data and decrypts the digital\nsignature using the sender's public key.\n\nMatching hashes assure the data's originality, as only the original sender could change the\ndata, calculate the hash, and use the sender's private key to create the digital signature.\n\nCCSP's Role\n\nCertified Cloud Security Professionals (CCSPs) must be familiar with digital signatures as a\nmethod for verifying apps and messages in cloud environments, particularly when integrating\nthird party software.\n\n",
    "srd.20260203192105.1": "Data obfuscation, similar to data masking, is a technique frequently employed when sensitive\ndata needs to be used in different Factors.\n\nWhile testers may not be authorised to view sensitive data, they can effectively perform their\ntasks using synthetic data that closely resembles live data.\n",
    "srd.20260203192211.1": "Data obfuscation, also known as de identification, is often mandated by regulations when data\nis intended for purposes beyond regular operations.\n",
    "srd.20260203192236.1": "There are several methods for data obfuscation, often integrated into database export\nfunctions, data management tools like spreadsheets, or business applications. These methods\nenable users to access and utilise datasets without needing access to sensitive data elements.\n\no Substitution: Involves replacing some information with other data. This substitution can\nfollow integrity rules or be done randomly.\n\no Shuffling: This method involves rearranging data, often within a single column.\n\no Value Variance: Mathematical alterations applied primarily to numerical data, such as\ndates, financial information, or measurements.",
    "srd.20260203192525.1": "While encryption can be used for obfuscation, it presents challenges. Encrypted data is\nunreadable, making it less useful for testing or research purposes.\n\nData obfuscation plays a critical role in safeguarding sensitive information while allowing\nauthorised parties to perform their tasks effectively and comply with regulations.",
    "srd.20260203192624.1": "Tokenisation is a process that involves substituting a sensitive data element with a non\nsensitive equivalent, commonly referred to as a token.\n\nIt is important to note that tokenisation is not the same as encryption and offers unique\nadvantages and challenges.\n\nKey Differences from Encryption\n\n\u2022 While encryption uses a key to obscure data, tokenisation takes a different approach. It\ncompletely removes the sensitive data from the database and replaces it with a mechanism to\nrecognise and access resources.\n\nPurpose of Tokenisation\n\n\u2022 Tokenisation is primarily used to safeguard sensitive data in secure, protected, or regulated\nenvironments. It serves as an effective method for ensuring data security while allowing\nauthorised access when needed.",
    "srd.20260203192919.1": "Tokens generated through tokenisation can be traced back to the original information. This\nprocess typically involves access controls that verify the user's identity and authorisation,\nensuring that only authorised individuals can view the sensitive data associated with a token.\n",
    "srd.20260203193053.1": "Tokenisation is commonly employed in various online payment systems, especially when\nhandling credit card numbers.\n\nInstead of storing actual credit card numbers within an application, tokenisation is used to\nenhance security. The credit card numbers are replaced with tokens, providing an added layer\nof protection.\n\nTokenisation is a valuable tool in maintaining data security and privacy, particularly in scenarios where sensitive information needs to be accessed while minimising exposure.",
    "srd.20260203193335.1": "Data Loss Prevention, also known as Data Leakage Prevention, encompasses a set of controls\nimplemented by organisations to ensure that specific types of data, both structured and\nunstructured, remain within the organisation's control in accordance with established policies,\nstandards, and procedures.",
    "srd.20260203193425.1": "One of the primary functions of DLP is to detect and prevent the unauthorised exfiltration of\nsensitive data, which may include key data such as Social Security Numbers (SSNs) and account\nnumbers.\n\nTo achieve this, the monitoring engine of DLP is typically deployed near the organisational\ngateway when using a network based DLP solution.",
    "srd.20260203193546.1": "1. Policy Management:\n\nThis component involves defining and configuring policies that specify\nwhich types of data should be protected and how they should be handled. Policies are aligned\nwith organisational requirements and compliance standards.\n\n2. Monitoring and Enforcement: \n\nDLP solutions continuously monitor data flows within the\norganisation, both at rest and in transit. They enforce policies by detecting and responding to\npotential data breaches or policy violations.\n\n3. Incident Response and Reporting:\n\nDLP systems provide mechanisms for incident response,\nallowing organisations to take immediate action when data breaches are detected.\nAdditionally, DLP solutions offer reporting and analysis capabilities to gain insights into data\nsecurity incidents and trends.",
    "srd.20260203194037.1": "Structured Data: Organised and Controlled Information\n\nStructured data encompasses information of a predefined format and content type, making it\nhighly organised and easily manageable.\n\nOne of the most prominent examples of structured data is found within relational databases.\n\nRelational Databases: The Pinnacle of Structured Data\n\nRelational databases serve as a prime illustration of structured data management. In these\nsystems, data is meticulously organised into distinct fields, each with a predefined structure\nand permissible data values.\n\nAdditionally, stringent controls are typically enforced to validate and sanitise data before it is committed to storage.",
    "srd.20260203194545.1": "Key Attributes of Structured Data\n\nDefined Structure: Structured data adheres to a well defined structure, ensuring that each\ndata element is categorised into specific fields.\n\nData Validation: Before storage, structured data undergoes validation processes to ensure\naccuracy and consistency.\n\nEase of Search: The structured nature of this data type makes it exceptionally easy to search\nand retrieve specific information efficiently.\n\nAnalytical Power: Structured data is highly amenable to analysis, facilitating insightful data\ndriven decision making processes.\n\nStructured data's inherent order and control make it a foundational component of information\nmanagement, enabling organisations to harness data for strategic purposes with precision and\nconfidence.",
    "srd.20260203194110.1": "Unstructured Data: The Essence of Information Chaos\n\nUnstructured data represents the antithesis of structured data, characterised by its lack of\norganisation according to defined structures or formats.\n\nUnstructured Data Variety: Both Text and Binary\n\nUnstructured data is an expansive category that encompasses a wide range of content,\nspanning text and binary data types.\n\nExamples of Unstructured Data:\n\n1. Documents and Text Files: Rich textual content, often lacking predefined structure, falls into this category. Examples include articles, essays, and free form notes.\n\n2. Emails: The contents of emails, including subject lines, message bodies, and attachments, are\nquintessential unstructured data.\n\n3. Images: Digital images, photographs, and graphics, with their visual complexity, are prime\nexamples.\n\n4. Videos: Video files, from home videos to professional productions, contain unstructured visual and auditory information.\n\n5. Office Documents: Spreadsheets, presentations, and word processing files may exhibit\nunstructured elements.\n\n6. Scientific Data: Complex datasets generated in scientific research, often with varied formats, can be unstructured.\n\n7. Sensory or Imagery Intelligence: Data from sensors, surveillance, and imagery sources often\nlacks standardised structure.\n\n8. Weather Data: Meteorological information, including satellite imagery and weather\nobservations, can be considered unstructured due to its diverse formats.",
    "srd.20260203194143.1": "Semi-tructured Data: Bridging the Gap Between Structure and Freedom\n\n\u2022 Semi structured data represents a unique middle ground in the data landscape, distinct from\nthe rigidly structured tables of relational databases.\n\n\u2022 Unlike structured data, which adheres to a strict tabular format, semi structured data\nintroduces a layer of flexibility through the use of markers or tags.\n\n\u2022 These markers serve to delineate semantic components and establish hierarchies among fields\nand records within the data.\n\nKey Characteristics of Semi-tructured Data:\n\n1. Flexibility in Entity Attributes: Entities of the same class within semi structured data can\nexhibit varying attributes, providing versatility in representing information. These attributes\nare often grouped together with others, blurring the fixed boundaries seen in structured data.\n\n2. Dynamic Attribute Ordering: In semi structured data, the order of attributes within entities is not prescribed or fixed, allowing for fluid arrangements based on Factor.\n\nThe Rise of Semi- Structured Data\n\n\u2022 With the advent of the internet, semi structured data has gained prominence. It serves as a\ncrucial format for sharing information in scenarios where traditional structured data or full text documents no longer suffice.\n\n\u2022 Moreover, semi structured data finds extensive use in object oriented databases,\naccommodating the needs of various applications.\n\nSemi Structured Data\n\n\u2022 A Hybrid of Information: Semi structured data exhibits qualities that straddle the line between structured and unstructured data.\n\n\u2022 It may contain metadata tags, such as those indicating location, date, or the source, yet the\ninformation encapsulated within these tags remains unstructured.\n\n\u2022 This hybrid nature makes semi structured data a versatile and adaptable format for a wide\nrange of data types.\n\n\u2022 In essence, semi structured data provides a bridge between the structured rigidity of\ntraditional databases and the free form chaos of unstructured data.\n\n\u2022 It offers the flexibility to represent diverse information while introducing an element of\norganisation through tags and hierarchies.",
    "srd.20260203194249.1": "Data Mobility in Cloud Computing: Navigating the Cloud's Geographical Reach\n\nCloud computing offers an unparalleled level of data mobility, enabling seamless access to\nresources from virtually anywhere in the world.\n\nYet, this newfound freedom comes with nuances, particularly concerning the location of data,\nthat organisations must navigate.\n\nIn many cases, cloud customers may not be acutely aware of the precise geographical\nwhereabouts of their data, and for everyday operations, this may not be a significant concern.\n\nHowever, when an enterprise entrusts sensitive data to cloud storage, understanding the\ndata's location becomes paramount.",
    "srd.20260203200033.1": "1. Sensitive Data Management\n\nEnterprises that deal with sensitive or regulated data may have stringent compliance\nrequirements.\n\nKnowing the geographical location of such data is essential for regulatory adherence and\nmaintaining data integrity.\n\n2. Preferred Data Location\n\nSome organisations may wish to define a preferred data location, whether for legal,\ncompliance, or operational reasons.\n\nThis preference often necessitates a contractual agreement between the consumer and the\ncloud service provider, specifying that the data must reside in a specific geographical region or on known servers.",
    "srd.20260203200219.1": "Navigating the Cloud's Geographical Landscape\n\nCloud service providers operate globally, often relying on distributed data centers and\nredundant infrastructure.\n\nWhile this offers benefits like redundancy and high availability, it also adds complexity to\ntracking data locations.\n\nIn response, cloud consumers should proactively engage with their providers to gain clarity on\ndata storage locations and ensure alignment with their specific needs.\n\nThis collaboration helps strike a balance between harnessing the cloud's mobility and\nsafeguarding data according to regulatory and operational requirements.\n\nIn essence, while cloud computing grants unmatched data mobility, organisations must remain\ndiligent in understanding the geographical aspects of their data, especially when dealing with\nsensitive information.",
    "srd.20260203200417.1": "Data Classification Policy: Safeguarding Organisational Information\n\nA data classification policy is a comprehensive strategy employed by organisations to\ncategorise their stored information based on its sensitivity level, ultimately mitigating\norganisational risks and ensuring proper handling.\n\nThis policy serves as a crucial framework consisting of procedures, processes, and guidelines\nfor classifying data into different categories, each with its corresponding level of sensitivity.\n\nThe overarching goal of a data classification policy is to establish standardised data\nmanagement practices within the company.",
    "srd.20260204154840.1": "1. Protecting Sensitive Data: A core function of the policy is to identify and safeguard sensitive or\nconfidential data. Through precise categorisation, it ensures that appropriate security\nmeasures are applied to protect such data.\n\n2. Lifecycle Management: The policy extends its influence throughout the entire lifecycle of data.\nFrom creation to disposal, it guides stakeholders on how to handle and protect data at every\nstage.\n\n3. Risk Mitigation: By adhering to data classification guidelines, organisations can significantly\nreduce risks associated with privacy breaches, compliance violations, and data security\nincidents.\n\nTailored to Each Organisation\n\nIt is important to note that a data classification policy is not one size fits all. Rather, it is\ncustomised to the unique needs and requirements of each organisation.\n\nIt is heavily influenced by industry standards and regulations applicable to the organisation's\nspecific sector.",
    "srd.20260204155044.1": "Compliance with relevant laws and regulations is a critical aspect of data classification policies.\nThese policies help ensure that data handling practices align with legal requirements,\nbolstering data security and enhancing trust among stakeholders.\n\nIn summary, a data classification policy is a fundamental component of modern data\ngovernance.\n\nIt provides a structured approach to managing data based on its sensitivity, reducing risks, and\nenhancing data security and compliance.\n\nAs it is tailored to each organisation's specific Factor, it serves as a crucial tool in protecting\nvaluable information assets.\n\n",
    "srd.20260203200509.1": "Mapping Strategy for Data Management\n\nAn effective mapping strategy is indispensable for organisations seeking to gain insights into\nthe location of data within their applications and broader storage infrastructure.\n\nThis strategic approach enables organisations to precisely identify where data resides,\nfacilitating data management and governance efforts.\nEnforcing Data Security\n\nA profound understanding of both data and metadata is essential for establishing and enforcing\nrobust data security policies and protocols within an organisation.\n\nWithout this knowledge, organisations may struggle to effectively safeguard their data assets.",
    "srd.20260204155228.1": "Data Discovery Software and Metadata: Enhancing Data Management:\n\nData discovery software plays a pivotal role in scanning data sources, utilising a variety of\nvaluable attributes.\n\nThese attributes offer different degrees of efficiency when processing vast volumes of data.\nAmong the critical elements used in this process is metadata, which is essentially data about\ndata.",
    "srd.20260204155245.1": "\nMetadata encompasses information about the data itself. It includes details about the data\ntype, how it is stored and organised, and its origins and purpose.\n\nThis comprehensive understanding of metadata provides crucial insights into the\ncharacteristics and properties of the underlying data.",
    "srd.20260203200531.1": "Data Labelling and Classification: Enhancing Data Management\n\nData labelling is a process that involves adding additional tagging information to data, typically\nincluding details such as department, location, and creator.\n\nIn certain cases, data classification is a key component of data labelling, allowing data to be\ncategorised based on specific requirements. Classification often includes labels such as \"top\nsecret,\" \"secret,\" or \"classified.\u201c\n\nClassification in Data Labelling\n\nClassification serves as an integral aspect of data labelling. It can be executed through manual\nprocesses, often delegated to the user responsible for creating the data, or through automated\nmechanisms governed by policy rules.\n\nThese rules may consider various factors, including data location, creator, content, and more.",
    "srd.20260204155846.1": "Complexity of Data Labelling\n\nData labelling can be a complex endeavor, primarily because traditional physical labels cannot\nbe directly applied to digital collections of 0s and 1s.\n\nTo address this challenge, various labelling methods are employed for different types of assets.\nSome of these methods include:\n\n1. Metadata Tagging: Associating metadata with digital assets to provide Factor and information.\n\n2. Header Labels : Incorporating labels within the headers of digital files for quick\n\n3. Access Controls: Implementing access controls and permissions to restrict data access based\non user roles.\n\n4.Policy Based Labelling: Automatically assigning labels based on predefined policy rules.",
    "srd.20260204160145.1": "Information Rights Management (IRM) extends beyond standard encryption policies to\nenhance the confidentiality and control of data. Below are some key objectives of IRM:\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260204160635.1": "IRM introduces an additional layer of access controls over data objects and documents.\nThese controls determine who can access a document and specify the permissible actions,\nsuch as printing, saving, copying, and more.\n\n\nThis layered approach aligns with the principle of least privilege, a core concept in modern\nsecurity frameworks.",
    "srd.20260204160718.1": "IRM leverages Access Control Lists (ACLs) to define and enforce access permissions. ACLs\nprovide detailed information about document access and usage rights, ensuring that\nsensitive data is safeguarded in line with predefined policies.",
    "srd.20260204161812.1": "A fundamental aspect of IRM is its location agnostic nature, enabling the protection of data\nirrespective of its storage location.\n\nBy embedding ACLs directly into the original file, IRM ensures consistent access control,\ncrucial for cloud environments where data mobility is common.\n\nThis feature supports the CCSP's emphasis on data security across diverse storage solutions\nand platforms.",
    "srd.20260204161932.1": "IRM is a versatile tool employed to safeguard a broad spectrum of sensitive content within\norganisations.\n\nIt protects diverse data types, including financial documents, emails, database columns,\nweb pages, and other data objects.\n\nIRM is a foundational component in the development of information protection policies\naimed at securing critical assets.\n\nIRM\u2019s ability to consistently enforce access controls, regardless of the data's location, combined\nwith its adaptability in protecting various data types, renders it an invaluable asset for\norganisations striving to maintain the confidentiality and integrity of their sensitive\ninformation.\n\nIts alignment with the comprehensive security frameworks recommended by CCSP ensures\nthat IRM remains a relevant and effective tool in the evolving landscape of cloud security.",
    "srd.20260204160204.1": "Revocation of certificates in a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) is a critical aspect of ensuring the security and trustworthiness of digital certificates. Key points regarding certificate revocation and the use of Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs) include:\n\nEffective certificate revocation maintenance is pivotal in PKI security. While CRLs play a\nsignificant role, alternative methods like OCSP are also used to overcome challenges associated\nwith CRLs, ensuring a more efficient and secure PKI environment.",
    "srd.20260204165434.1": "The CRL, maintained by the Certificate Authority (CA), lists information about revoked\ndigital certificates.\n\nIt typically includes the serial number of the revoked certificate and the time of revocation.\nThe CRL is an essential tool for identifying invalid or compromised certificates.",
    "srd.20260204165453.1": "Clients, such as web browsers, perform several checks when establishing a secure\nconnection with a server presenting a digital certificate. One crucial check involves verifying\nthat the certificate is not listed on the CRL, ensuring its validity and trustworthiness.\n",
    "srd.20260204165654.1": "Clients parse the entire CRL to confirm whether the certificate\nis revoked, which can be resource intensive, especially with\nlarge CRLs containing numerous entries.",
    "srd.20260204165708.1": "\nCAs distribute CRLs through various channels, including\nwebsites or secure protocols.\n\nClients require access to the latest CRL to ensure accurate\nverification.",
    "srd.20260204165725.1": "CRLs have an expiration date. Clients must use the most current\nCRL to avoid inaccuracies due to expired lists.",
    "srd.20260204170113.1": "OCSP offers an alternative to CRLs for checking certificate status. It allows real time queries\nto the CA or an OCSP responder, providing a more immediate check.",
    "srd.20260204170238.1": "CRL checks can be cumbersome, especially for parsing large lists. Some systems use caching\nmechanisms to minimise the frequency of CRL retrievals, enhancing efficiency.",
    "srd.20260204171207.1": "Data Protection Policies should encompass guidelines for different phases of the data lifecycle.\nData Retention Policies, a crucial part of these policies, establish protocols within an\norganisation for retaining information to meet regulatory and operational compliance needs.\n\nThese policies strike a balance between regulatory, legal, and business requirements for data\narchiving and the challenges associated with data storage costs, complexities, and other\nconsiderations.\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260204171702.1": "Key objectives of a Data Retention Policy include:",
    "srd.20260204171356.1": "Ensuring that data retention practices comply with industry specific\nregulations and legal requirements. This involves retaining necessary records for the legally\nmandated periods.\n",
    "srd.20260204171422.1": "Optimising the availability and accessibility of important data for\nbusiness operations, while preventing the unnecessary retention of Redundant, Outdated, or\nTrivial (ROT) data.",
    "srd.20260204171444.1": "Managing the costs associated with data storage and archiving, which\nincludes minimising expenses by retaining only necessary data and disposing of data that is no\nlonger required.",
    "srd.20260204171459.1": "Reducing the risk of data breaches and ensuring data privacy by securely\nstoring sensitive information and safely disposing of it when it is no longer needed.",
    "srd.20260204171523.1": "Ensuring that retained data can be efficiently accessed and\nretrieved when needed. This is crucial for responding to legal inquiries, audits, and business\nanalysis.",
    "srd.20260204171543.1": "Regularly reviewing and updating the data retention policy to\nreflect changes in legal requirements, business needs, and technological advancements.",
    "srd.20260204171902.1": "A good data retention policy is essential for effective data management and compliance. It\nshould define the following key elements:",
    "srd.20260204171921.1": "Clearly state the purpose of the policy and its scope within the\norganisation. This includes specifying the types of data covered and the departments or\nbusiness units it applies to.\n",
    "srd.20260204171937.1": "Define specific retention periods for different types of data, based on legal,\nregulatory, and business requirements. This should detail how long data should be retained\nand the criteria used to determine these periods.",
    "srd.20260204171947.1": "Include guidelines for classifying data based on its importance, sensitivity,\nand legal requirements. Data classification helps in applying appropriate retention periods and\nsecurity measures.",
    "srd.20260204172012.1": "Outline the roles and responsibilities of employees, management, and\nother stakeholders in implementing and adhering to the retention policy. This ensures\naccountability and clarity in policy enforcement.",
    "srd.20260204172025.1": "Detail the storage methods and security measures to protect data during its\nretention period. This should include physical and digital security practices, access controls, and encryption methods.",
    "srd.20260204172036.1": "Provide clear procedures for the secure disposal or destruction of data after\nthe retention period has expired. This should ensure that data is disposed of in a manner that\nprevents unauthorised access or recovery.",
    "srd.20260204172052.1": "Address legal and regulatory compliance requirements\nrelevant to data retention, including specific industry regulations and data protection laws.\n",
    "srd.20260204172129.1": "Establish a process for regular review and updating of the retention policy to reflect changes in legal requirements, business needs, technological advancements, and organisational changes.",
    "srd.20260204172146.1": "Include provisions for training and raising awareness among employees about the\nimportance of the retention policy and their role in its implementation.",
    "srd.20260204172201.1": "Outline procedures for auditing and monitoring compliance with the retention policy to\nensure its effectiveness and identify areas for improvement.",
    "srd.20260204172257.1": "The components of a Data Retention Policy typically encompass several key elements to ensure\nthat it is comprehensive, effective, and compliant with legal and regulatory standards. These\ncomponents include:\n",
    "srd.20260204172329.1": "An overview of the reasons for implementing the policy, such as legal compliance, data management efficiency, and risk mitigation.",
    "srd.20260204172344.1": "A description of where and to whom the policy applies within the\norganisation. This can include specific departments, types of data, and data storage locations.",
    "srd.20260204172357.1": "Guidelines for categorising data based on its importance, sensitivity, and\nthe legal or business requirements for retention. Common categories include confidential,\ninternal, public, and sensitive data.\n",
    "srd.20260204172508.1": "Detailed schedules for how long different types of data should be retained.\nThese should be based on legal requirements, business needs, and industry best practices.\n",
    "srd.20260204172545.1": "Clear definitions of who is responsible for implementing and\noverseeing the policy. This typically involves data managers, IT staff, legal teams, and\ndepartment heads.\n",
    "srd.20260204172547.1": "Instructions on how data should be stored and\nmanaged during the retention period. This includes physical and digital storage solutions, data\naccess controls, and security measures.",
    "srd.20260204172717.1": "Processes for how retained data can be accessed and retrieved\nwhen needed. This should be efficient and comply with security protocols to protect data\nintegrity.",
    "srd.20260204172816.1": "Procedures for the secure and proper disposal or deletion of\ndata once the retention period has expired, ensuring that data is irrecoverable to prevent\nunauthorised access.\n",
    "srd.20260204172835.1": "A section addressing the legal and regulatory\nrequirements affecting data retention, including specific laws and industry regulations.\n",
    "srd.20260204172852.1": "A mechanism for regular review and updating of the policy to\nreflect changes in legal requirements, technology, business operations, and organisational\nneeds.\n",
    "srd.20260204172906.1": "Initiatives to educate and inform staff about the retention\npolicy, their responsibilities, and the importance of compliance.\n",
    "srd.20260204172926.1": "Strategies for regularly auditing the effectiveness of the policy and\nmonitoring compliance to identify areas for improvement and to ensure the policy is being\nproperly implemented.\n",
    "srd.20260204173223.1": "The components of a Data Retention Policy typically encompass several key elements to\nensure that it is comprehensive, effective, and compliant with legal and regulatory standards.\nThese components include:\n",
    "srd.20260204173256.1": "An overview of the reasons for implementing the policy, such as legal\ncompliance, data management efficiency, and risk mitigation.\n",
    "srd.20260204173448.1": "A description of where and to whom the policy applies within the\norganisation. This can include specific departments, types of data, and data storage locations.\n",
    "srd.20260204173503.1": "Guidelines for categorising data based on its importance, sensitivity, and\nthe legal or business requirements for retention. Common categories include confidential,\ninternal, public, and sensitive data.\n",
    "srd.20260204173517.1": "Detailed schedules for how long different types of data should be retained.\nThese should be based on legal requirements, business needs, and industry best practices.",
    "srd.20260204173605.1": "Clear definitions of who is responsible for implementing and\noverseeing the policy. This typically involves data managers, IT staff, legal teams, and\ndepartment heads.\n",
    "srd.20260204173626.1": "Instructions on how data should be stored and\nmanaged during the retention period. This includes physical and digital storage solutions, data\naccess controls, and security measures.\n",
    "srd.20260204173644.1": "Processes for how retained data can be accessed and retrieved when needed. This should be efficient and comply with security protocols to protect data integrity. \n",
    "srd.20260204173704.1": "Procedures for the secure and proper disposal or deletion of\ndata once the retention period has expired, ensuring that data is irrecoverable to prevent\nunauthorised access.",
    "srd.20260204173732.1": "A section addressing the legal and regulatory requirements affecting data retention, including specific laws and industry regulations.",
    "srd.20260204173753.1": "A mechanism for regular review and updating of the policy to reflect changes in legal requirements, technology, business operations, and organisational needs.",
    "srd.20260204173818.1": "Initiatives to educate and inform staff about the retention policy, their responsibilities, and the importance of compliance.",
    "srd.20260204173830.1": "Strategies for regularly auditing the effectiveness of the policy and monitoring compliance to identify areas for improvement and to ensure the policy is being properly implemented.",
    "srd.20260204173931.1": "Data Archiving is the process of identifying and moving inactive data into specialised long\nterm archival storage systems. This process not only optimises the performance of primary\nresources but also ensures cost efficient storage. Elements of data archiving policies include:",
    "srd.20260204174302.1": "Legal Hold refers to the specific process of preserving data in response to legal proceedings.\nThis process becomes relevant when organisations or individuals receive official judicial or law\nenforcement orders related to civil or criminal proceedings.\n\nThese orders typically detail the scope and requirements for data preservation, specifying what\ndata needs to be retained and how.",
    "srd.20260204174403.1": "The legal hold notice will define the types of data that must be\npreserved. This can range from emails and electronic documents to database records and\nother digital information relevant to the legal matter.",
    "srd.20260204174837.1": "Legal holds often stipulate specific requirements or\nformats for data preservation. This ensures that the data remains in a state that is acceptable\nand usable for legal purposes.\n",
    "srd.20260204174856.1": "Data under legal hold is usually required to be preserved until the\nconclusion of the legal proceedings. The duration can vary depending on the nature and\ncomplexity of the case.\n",
    "srd.20260204174921.1": "In some instances, the organisation itself must retain custody\nand ensure the protection of the data. This involves implementing and maintaining\nappropriate security measures to safeguard the data from alteration or destruction.",
    "srd.20260204174943.1": "There are circumstances where the data may\nneed to be held in escrow or under independent custody. This means that a third party may be\nrequired to store and manage the data to ensure its integrity and impartiality throughout the\nlegal process.\n",
    "srd.20260204174958.1": "Organisations must ensure compliance with the legal hold\norder and document their actions. This documentation is critical to demonstrate adherence to\nlegal requirements and to protect the organisation in the legal process.",
    "srd.20260128204211.1": "The definition of \"Event Sources\" and the \"Requirement of Event Attributes\" are key concepts in the Factor of data management, security, and information systems.",
    "srd.20260204203639.1": "\nEvent Sources:\n\nIn the Factor of information technology and cybersecurity, an event source is any software,\nhardware, or system that generates data or activity logs.\n\nThese logs are known as 'events'. Common examples of event sources include servers, network\ndevices, applications, security systems (like firewalls and intrusion detection systems), and  even end user devices like workstations and mobile devices.\n\nIdentifying event sources is crucial for effective data monitoring, security incident detection, and compliance management.\n\nBy understanding where data or activity logs originate, organisations can more effectively track, analyse, and respond to various activities within their IT environment.",
    "srd.20260204203737.1": "These are specific details or characteristics recorded in an event log.\n\nEvent attributes typically include information such as the timestamp of the event, the type of event (e.g., login attempt, file access, system error), the source and destination of the event, user details, and the outcome or status of the event.\n\nThe requirement of event attributes is fundamental for accurate and meaningful analysis of events.\n\nDetailed attributes allow for more granular and precise monitoring, helping in tasks such as troubleshooting, security analysis, auditing, and compliance reporting.\n\nThey enable organisations to construct a clear and comprehensive picture of what is happening across their systems and networks.",
    "srd.20260204203822.1": "The logging, storage, and analysis of data events are critical components in the management\nand security of information systems.\n\nEach aspect plays a crucial role in ensuring that data is accurately recorded, securely stored,\nand effectively analysed for various purposes, including security, performance monitoring, and\ncompliance.\n\nBelow is an overview of each component:\n\n1. Logging of Data Events\n\nThis involves the recording of events that occur within an information system. An event log\nmight include a wide range of data such as user activities, system errors, security alerts, and\ntransaction histories.\n\nImportance: Logging is essential for tracking the behavior and performance of systems and\napplications. it is also crucial for security monitoring, as logs can provide the first indicators of a security incident or breach.\n\n2. Storage of Data Events\n\nOnce events are logged, they must be stored in a secure and manageable way. This involves\ndetermining the appropriate storage location, format, and retention period for log data, which can vary based on regulatory requirements and organisational policies.\n\nImportance: Secure and organised storage of log data is vital for ensuring that the data remains intact and accessible for future analysis. It also plays a critical role in compliance, as many regulations require specific standards for data retention and protection.\n\n3. Analysis of Data Events\n\nThis is the process of examining event logs to extract useful information. Analysis can be performed manually by system administrators or automatically using specialised tools like SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) systems.\n\nImportance:\n\nThe analysis of log data helps in identifying trends, diagnosing system issues, and detecting\nanomalies that may indicate security incidents.\n\nIt is also key for making informed decisions about system improvements and security\nenhancements.\n\nEach of these components is interconnected.\n\nEffective logging ensures that valuable data is captured, proper storage guarantees that this data is kept secure and accessible, and thorough analysis allows organisations to derive meaningful insights from their data.\n\nTogether, these practices form a foundation for robust information system management and\nsecurity.",
    "srd.20260204203846.1": "Chain of Custody in digital forensics and information security refers to the process of\ndocumenting the handling, storage, and transfer of digital evidence.\n\nIt involves creating a chronological record of who has had access to the evidence, how and\nwhen it was transferred, and any actions performed on it.\n\nMaintaining a rigorous chain of custody is crucial in legal Factors because it establishes the\nintegrity and credibility of the evidence.\n\nIt demonstrates that the evidence has been collected, stored, and preserved in a manner\nthat prevents tampering or alteration, ensuring that it is admissible in court or other legal\nproceedings\n\nNOTE: The concepts of \"Chain of Custody\" and \"Non repudiation\" are critical in the Factor of digital forensics, cybersecurity, and legal proceedings involving digital evidence. \nUnderstanding these terms is essential for ensuring the integrity and reliability of digital\ninformation, particularly in situations where this information may be scrutinised or\nchallenged.",
    "srd.20260204203915.1": "Non repudiation in cybersecurity and digital communications refers to the assurance that\nsomeone cannot deny the validity of their digital actions or communications.\n\nThis is typically achieved through the use of digital signatures and other cryptographic\ntechniques.\n\nNon repudiation is vital in various scenarios, such as financial transactions, contract signing, and email communications, where it is important to confirm that a specific action or\ncommunication truly originated from a particular individual or entity.\n\nIt provides proof of the origin and integrity of the data, ensuring accountability and\npreventing denial of actions.\n\nNOTE: \nBoth chain of custody and non repudiation are fundamental in preserving the authenticity and\nreliability of digital data.\n\nWhile the chain of custody focuses on the traceability and handling of digital evidence, non\nrepudiation ensures that individuals or entities cannot falsely deny their involvement in digital transactions or communications.",
    "srd.20260114161206.1": "Domian 3: Cloud Platform and Infrastructure security \n17%",
    "n30.20260204184701.1": "Cloud infrastructure encompasses the physical and virtual resources within data centres,\nincluding compute nodes, storage systems, and networking hardware.\n\nIt integrates virtualisation software and management layers to support scalable and efficient\ncloud services.\n\nThe cloud infrastructure consists of data centres and the hardware that runs in\nthem, including:\n\n1. Compute, Storage, and Networking hardware\n2. Virtualisation Software\n3. A Management Layer\n",
    "n30.20260204185639.1": "The server rooms, data centers, and other physical locations where the Cloud Service Provider\n(CSP) is located constitute the physical environment.\n\nDepending on the cloud security model, the identity of the CSP may vary:\n\n1. In a private cloud developed on-premises by a company, the business or customer acts as the CSP.\n\n2. In a community cloud, the CSP is often a community member who provides space and hardware.\n\n3. If a commercial provider like Amazon Web Services (AWS) or Microsoft Azure is used to build the community cloud, then the CSP is the commercial vendor.\n\n4. For public clouds, such as those provided by AWS, Microsoft, Google, IBM, and others, the CSP is the commercial vendor providing the service.",
    "n30.20260204185712.1": "\u2022 The CSP is responsible for the networking components it houses, maintaining their security.\n\n\u2022 Customers are required to manage any components located within their own facilities (on-premise). The public Internet, which connects these two, is a primary area of concern.\n\n\u2022 The CSP must support secure methods such as HTTPS, data encryption prior to transmission, or VPN usage, which the client is expected to utilise.\n\n\u2022 Both the customer and the CSP are responsible for implementing firewalls and other technologies necessary to maintain secure communications at each end of the transmission pipeline.\n\n\u2022 In the shared security model, the CSP provides tools for secure computing, logging, encryption, and other functions.\n\n\u2022 However, it may be the customer responsibility to correctly configure and manage these services. The customer is always accountable for establishing a secure connection and securely transmitting data to the service.",
    "n30.20260204185752.1": "\u2022In the realm of cloud infrastructure, computing and storage are foundational elements that determine the performance, scalability, and efficiency of IT services.\n\n\u2022Understanding these elements is crucial for deploying, managing, and optimising cloud resources.\n\nComputing in the Cloud\n\u2022 Cloud computing resources are primarily defined by their compute capabilities, which include:\n\n1. CPU (Central Processing Units)\n\nDetermines the processing power available for applications and processes. More CPUs or  multi-core CPUs can perform multiple operations simultaneously, enhancing performance.\n\n2. RAM (Random Access Memory)\n\nVital for the temporary storage of data that is actively being used or processed. Adequate RAM ensures smooth operation and quick access to data, reducing latency.\n\nResource Management\n\u2022 Cloud platforms allow administrators to allocate computing resources using methods like:\n\n1. Reservations: Set minimum guaranteed resources for a system to ensure availability and\nperformance.\n\n2. Shares: Define the priority of resource allocation in a multi-tenant environment.\n\n3. Limits: Cap the maximum resource usage to prevent any single system from consuming disproportionate resources, ensuring fair access across all systems.",
    "n30.20260204190844.1": "Storage in the Cloud\n\u2022 Cloud storage involves storing data across multiple distributed and potentially replicated resources. Two primary storage types in cloud computing are:\n\n1. Volume Storage\n\u2022Acts like a traditional hard drive, providing block storage that can be attached to a virtual machine as if it were a physical disk.\n\n\u2022It is ideal for scenarios requiring traditional file systems and database applications, which require low latency and high-performance.\n\n2. Object Storage\n\nData is managed as objects rather than as traditional files. Each object includes the data itself, a variable amount of metadata, and a globally unique identifier, making it highly scalable and accessible via HTTP APIs\n\n\u2022 It is suited for unstructured data such as multimedia files, backups, and big data analytics. \n\nKey Technologies\n1. RAID (Redundant Array of Inexpensive Disks): Enhances data integrity and performance.\n2. SAN (Storage Area Networks): Connects storage devices on a separate high-speed network providing reliable and flexible storage infrastructure.\n\nIntegration and Optimisation\n\u2022 Integrating computing and storage resources effectively requires understanding the specific demands of applications, anticipated data growth, and access patterns.\n\u2022 Optimisation strategies include auto-scaling to adjust resources dynamically based on demand and sophisticated caching to speed up data access and enhance responsiveness.\n",
    "n30.20260204185837.1": "\u2022 Virtualisation forms the backbone of cloud environments and their various hosting models. It enables a cloud environment to offer significant customer benefits such as resource pooling,\non-demand self-service, and scalability.\n\n\u2022 Resource pooling allows multiple customers to share a common pool of resources, on-demand and self-service enables users to automatically provision computing capabilities as needed, while\nscalability refers to the ability to easily scale resources up or down as demand changes.\n\n\u2022 Virtualisation represents a significant shift from the traditional paradigms and limitations of single servers, where resources are tied to one physical server.\n\n\u2022 Instead, it allows for the creation of large resource pools that can be leveraged across many different hosts and applications. This flexibility and efficiency stand in stark contrast to the more rigid and isolated nature of traditional single-server setups.\n\n\u2022 By breaking free from these constraints, virtualisation provides the foundation for the dynamic,flexible, and efficient cloud environments that businesses and individuals rely on today\n\n",
    "n30.20260204185947.1": "Management Plane\n\n\u2022 The management plane enables remote administration of one or multiple hosts.\n\nOutside World\n\n\u2022 The primary functions of cloud infrastructure include creating, starting, and stopping virtual machine instances, along with provisioning them with essential virtual resources.\n\n\u2022 These resources include memory, CPU, network connectivity, and permanent storage.\n\n\u2022 The management plane oversees all resources across an entire equipment farm and typically operates on dedicated servers with dedicated connectivity to the physical hosts it manages\n\n",
    "srd.20260205143152.1": "\nThe logical layout of a data center is an abstract representation. Customers leverage the tools\nand services provided by the Cloud Service Provider (CSP) to create this logical data center.\n\nProper implementation of the logical design is crucial for ensuring the security of the data\ncenter.\n\nThis includes essential aspects such as access management, regulatory compliance monitoring,\npatch management, log analysis, and service configuration.\n\nTo establish a secure perimeter in a logical data center design, Identity and Access\nManagement (IAM) is essential. Monitoring all attempts at data access is a key component.\n\nVarious IAM techniques, including authentication, authorisation , security groups, Virtual\nPrivate Clouds (VPCs), and management consoles, are used to control access.\n\nConsistency in configuration is essential for every service used. The responsibility for this\nconfiguration lies with the business, which specifies how each authorised cloud service should\nbe used and configured.\n\nAdministering and managing cloud services becomes more straightforward and often more\nsecure when a uniform configuration is applied.\n\nBefore approving any deviations from the established patterns, it is crucial to assess the risk\nassociated with any variances through an exception process that each business has endorsed.\n\nSafe baseline configurations contribute to a more secure environment in data centers.\n\nMonitoring the configurations of these cloud services allows for the detection of any changes,\nwhich can then be halted, alerted, or reported.",
    "srd.20260205143210.1": "\nThe physical design of a cloud data center is typically the responsibility of the owner, who is\noften the Cloud Service Provider (CSP).\n\nWith the added complexity of supporting multiple tenants (multitenancy), the physical design\nof a data center for a CSP or cloud vendor is similar to that of on premises data centers.\n\nLOCATION\n\nThe physical location of a data center plays a critical role in reducing certain disaster risks. It's\nadvisable to avoid regions prone to natural disasters, civil unrest, or other issues when\nselecting a data center location.\n\nConsider distributing data centers across multiple locations to enhance disaster resilience.\nWhile no location is entirely immune to disasters, spreading resources across areas with\nvarying disaster risk profiles can improve cloud resource availability.\n\nIn addition to disaster risk, ensure that the chosen area has reliable utilities and access to\nmultiple communication channels.\n\nBUY AND HOLD\n\nThe decision to transition to a cloud based data center is\ntypically a long term commitment.\n\nFactors such as the sensitivity of the data, legal regulations\ngoverning data storage, and the availability of qualified\npersonnel for on premise maintenance must be considered.\n\nIn cases where legal restrictions prevent data from crossing\nborders, the customer's data may need to be kept on\npremises unless the CSP contract allows for compliance with\nsuch requirements.\n\nSome clients handle highly sensitive information, where a\nbreach could potentially endanger individuals.",
    "srd.20260205143246.1": "\nWith the exception of private clouds, the Cloud Service Provider (CSP) or cloud vendor is\nresponsible for the environmental design, similar to a physical data center location.\n\nThe design of the environment can have an impact on the accessibility of cloud resources.\n\nFor cloud customers, the vendor's risk analysis may include an assessment of the vendor's\nunderlying design or the CSP's environmental design.\n",
    "srd.20260205144531.1": "Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning\n\nEvery computing facility must prioritise electrical service as a fundamental requirement.\n\nEqually crucial is the provision of proper Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC)\nsupport within the facility.\n\nThe impact of HVAC requirements can be influenced by the facility's physical location and\nstructure.\n\nFor instance, a data center located closer to the equator will have a greater focus on air\nconditioning (AC) than heating.\n\nWhen selecting a location for an on premise data center or evaluating potential cloud vendors,\nit is crucial to thoroughly consider HVAC issues.\n\nAn HVAC failure can impact computing resources similarly to an electrical or communication\noutage.\n\nGiven the critical role of HVAC in a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) data center, evaluating the\ncapacity, redundancy of HVAC systems, and the process for switching between data centers\nwhen necessary should be integral to a CSP review.\n\nThis consideration extends to the physical location of the CSP data center.",
    "srd.20260205144901.1": "\nIn the realm of cloud computing, ensuring connectivity is paramount. Adequate planning is\nrequired to guarantee uninterrupted access to all cloud resources.\n\nOften, the issue does not lie with the broader Internet but rather with the connectivity of\neither the customer or the Cloud Service Provider (CSP).\n\nThe solution often involves the use of multiple Internet Service Providers (ISPs) or connectivity\nvendors to address these two distinct yet related issues customer Internet connectivity and\nCSP Internet connectivity.\n\nThese issues are interrelated yet independent. A breakdown in customer communication\nwould affect only that specific tenant, while the vendor and other tenants would remain\nunaffected.",
    "srd.20260205143340.1": "\nResilience refers to the ability to adapt to changing conditions and recover from disruptions or\ninterruptions.\n\nThis concept is vital across various domains, from individual buildings to entire regions,\nfocusing on proactive design to enhance robustness and flexibility against vulnerabilities.\n\nIn urban planning and architecture, resilience means constructing infrastructures capable of\nwithstanding extreme weather and other crises.\n\nFor communities, it entails developing comprehensive emergency response strategies,\npromoting economic diversity, and reinforcing social support systems.\n\nAdditionally, environmental resilience involves implementing conservation practices that\nenhance ecosystem robustness.\n\nIntegrating resilience into development reduces disruption impacts, cuts recovery costs, and\nsupports sustainable growth.\n\n\nDesigners can take the following specific actions to reduce the impact these disasters have\non the built environment:\n\nLocate the project away from known hazard regions.\n\nDesign with flexibility in mind.\n\nConstruct reliable, simple systems.\n\nRedundancy wherever it is possible.\n\nApply passive design principles.\n\nMake use of vernacular design elements.\nWhen feasible, utilise natural resources.",
    "srd.20260205153028.1": "\nSeveral industry organisations publish lists of risks, providing valuable insights and information.\n\nNonetheless, every organisation , whether providing or consuming cloud services, must\nconduct its own risk assessment.\n\nFrom an organisational and regulatory perspective, there are risks related to the following:\n\n1. Lock in: Dependence on a single cloud provider reduces flexibility.\n2. Governance: Ensuring policies manage cloud resources effectively and compliantly.\n3. Data Security and Privacy: Protecting stored data from unauthorised access or breaches.\n4. Legal and Regulatory Controls: Adhering to laws and regulations specific to cloud usage.\n5. Reporting Required: Documentation and accountability for cloud based systems and applications.",
    "srd.20260205153124.1": "\nThe fundamental risk of the cloud lies in its Internet based model, making it vulnerable to\nattacks from anyone with an Internet connection, whether targeting you, your cloud provider,\nor your Cloud Service Provider (CSP).\n\nThese threats can be aimed at the vendor, another customer of the cloud provider, or may be\nlinked to risks in the geographic location of the cloud data center.\n\nThe source of these attacks can be from anywhere in the world, even from bystanders.\nRegardless of the target, a Distributed Denial of Service (DoS) attack can ensue.\n\nOther tenants in the cloud pose additional risks, as security measures separating consumer\ndata may fail. These tenants could be competitors or malicious individuals.\n\nOne of the best forms of protection is encryption, with the customer retaining control over the\nencryption keys.\n\nCustomers should also carefully consider what sensitive information they place in the cloud.\n\nThere is a potential risk of data theft by cloud vendor employees for personal gain.\n\nWhen such risks are identified, contractual agreements may be the only viable solution. However, the best defense is often prevention.\n\nTo safeguard against data exposure, encryption is crucial, with customers managing their encryption keys externally, rather than within the cloud",
    "srd.20260205153152.1": "1: Data Encyption at Rest\n\n2: Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)\n\n3: Eliminate Shared Accounts\n\n4: Insist on a Well-defined Shared Responsibility Model\n\n5: Use Standarised Cloud Assesment Questions\n",
    "srd.20260205154358.1": "\nIn a cloud environment, the choice of hosting location for physical servers must account for\nenvironmental and physical controls.\n\nThis decision could involve a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) for a third party cloud or a public\ncloud.\n\nIn the case of a private cloud, it may be the business using the cloud. The selection of a site\nsignificantly impacts environmental and physical protection.\n\nPlacing a data center near water in a region prone to frequent storms, tornadoes, flooding, or\nseismic activity may not be advisable. Alternatively, it may involve a strategy with multiple data\ncenters dispersed across different locations to ensure redundancy.\n\nEach data center provides a higher level of assurance for availability when it possesses a unique\nrisk profile.\n\nAdditionally, all data centers, including physical cloud data centers, must adhere to specific\nphysical and environmental criteria.\n\n\nThese criteria encompass the ability to restrict physical access, the availability of an adequate\nworkforce, and reliable access to power and essential resources.\n\nFurthermore, a cloud data center requires substantial network capacity, which is a\nfundamental requirement for all data centers.\n\nHowever, because a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) may serve a multitude of clients across a vast\ngeographic area, potentially worldwide, these standards may be more stringent for a cloud\ndata center than for an on premises data center with a single tenant.\n\nIn certain scenarios, having multiple Internet Service Providers (ISPs) can enhance connectivity\nredundancy.\n\nWith the exception of an on premises private cloud, users have no control over the physical\nlocation of a cloud data center. This adjustment only shifts the customer's responsibility; it\ndoes not absolve the customer of responsibility in this regard.",
    "srd.20260205154451.1": "In the dynamic landscape of cloud computing, ensuring robust security for systems and\ncommunications alongside effective access control is crucial.\n\nThis comprehensive approach to cloud security not only protects data but also maintains the\nintegrity and availability of computing resources.",
    "srd.20260205154546.1": "\nCloud environments must employ stringent security controls to protect both stored data and\ndata in transit.\n\nKey controls include:\n\n1. Policy and Procedures\n\nEstablishing clear policies and procedures provides a foundational framework that guides all\nother security measures.\n\n2. Security Function Isolation\n\nIsolating security functions from other system operations helps prevent unauthorised access\nand ensures the integrity of security operations.\n\n3. Denial of Service Protection\n\nImplementing strategies to mitigate denial of service attacks is crucial. This includes enhancing\nsystem resilience, detecting and responding to attacks promptly, and managing bandwidth and\ncapacity effectively.\n\n4. Boundary Protection\n\nUtilising firewalls, intrusion detection systems, and data loss prevention technologies to secure\nthe network perimeter against unauthorised access and to safeguard data from exfiltration.",
    "srd.20260205154636.1": "\nManaging identities and controlling access within cloud environments are critical to\nmaintaining security:\n\n1. Migration to Cloud IAM Systems\n\nOrganisations may choose to extend their on premises IAM solutions to the cloud or adopt\ncloud based IAM services.\n\nDecisions should consider the ease of management, security requirements, and the potential\nfor achieving single sign on across multiple systems.\n\n2. Vendor IAM Systems\n\nRelying on a vendor's IAM system carries risks, necessitating a thorough evaluation of the\nvendor's security practices. Ensuring that the vendor's IAM solutions meet your organisation's\nsecurity standards is paramount.\n\n3. User Education\n\nEducating users on secure practices, such as avoiding\npassword reuse and recognising phishing attempts, is\nessential to safeguarding identity and access management\nsystems.",
    "srd.20260205154723.1": "This model delineates the security obligations of the cloud provider and the customer:\n\n1. Provider Responsibilities\n\nCloud providers are generally responsible for securing the infrastructure that supports cloud services.\n\n2. Customer Responsibilities\n\nCustomers must manage the security of their data within the cloud services, including the use of encryption for data at rest and in transit, and the implementation of robust access\ncontrols.",
    "srd.20260205154745.1": "To protect data in transit, employing encryption protocols such as TLS (Transport Layer Security), IPSec, or using Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) is recommended.\n\nThese measures ensure that data transmitted over the internet is secure from interception or tampering.",
    "srd.20260205154813.1": "\nThe purpose of an audit is to ensure that a sufficient number of risk controls exist and that they\nare operationally efficient.\n\nThere are various reasons for conducting audits, with the most obvious ones being regulatory\nor compliance related.\n\nIt makes sense to integrate audits into existing corporate governance and enterprise risk\nmanagement structures.\n\nWithin these frameworks, all requirements for risk controls can be consolidated, including\nlegal, technical, jurisdictional, contractual, and compliance requirements.\n\nThis consolidation helps streamline audit processes and ensures that all risk controls are\naligned with overall corporate objectives and legal obligations.\n\nAdditionally, integrating audits within these frameworks promotes continuous improvement\nand adherence to best practices, enhancing organisational resilience and security posture.",
    "srd.20260205154840.1": "\nIn cloud environments, the main challenge in log collection is determining which logs the customer\ncan access, beyond the typical difficulties found in traditional data centers.\n\nThe level of access is highly variable and depends on the cloud deployment and the contract with\nthe cloud provider.\n\nIn an IaaS environment, cloud customers have extensive access to logs from the OS, virtual devices,\nplatform, and application levels.\n\nWithin a PaaS (Platform as a Service) environment, the same level of comprehensive logs would be\navailable to the customer at the platform and application levels.\n\nAccess to logs at the operating system and network device levels, as well as at the application level\nin SaaS environments, depends on availability from the cloud provider.",
    "srd.20260205154858.1": "In a cloud environment, packet capture encounters many of the same challenges as log\ncollection.\n\nThe level of packet capture available will depend on the cloud deployment and the control over\nessential network appliances, as well as the point at which capture is anticipated.\n\nIf packet capture is required on the virtual machine within an IaaS environment, the cloud\ncustomer likely has the necessary level of access.\n\nHowever, within a PaaS or SaaS environment, their capability for packet capture would depend\non the services provided by the cloud provider.\n\nMoreover, the effectiveness of packet capture is influenced by the cloud provider's policies and\nthe tools they offer, which can vary significantly between providers.",
    "n30.20260204185034.1": "\nBCDR (Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery) protects against the risk of data not being\navailable and the possibility that the business processes it supports are not functional, which\ncan result in adverse consequences for the organisation.\n\nThe following are the main strategies of BCDR: \n\nLocation\n\nData Replication\n\nFunctionality Replication\n\nPlanning, Preparing and Provisioning\n\nFailover Capability\n\nReturning to Normal\n\n\n\nThree key terms to remember in this Factor are: RPO, RTO, and RSL.",
    "srd.20260205181230.1": "This term helps determine how much information must be restored and recovered.",
    "srd.20260205181258.1": "It measures how quickly each system needs to be up and running in the event of a critical failure or disaster.\n\n\n                       DISASTER\n|-----------*-------------()---------------*------------>\n           RPO                            RTO     \n\n         {----------------}\n          Lost transactions\n                          {--RecoveryTime--}\n\n                          ",
    "srd.20260205181339.1": "This is a percentage measurement (ranging from 0 to 100%) of how much computing power is needed based on the percentage of the production system required during a disaster.",
    "srd.20260205180820.1": "\nImplementing a Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery (BCDR) plan involves several critical\nsteps to ensure that an organisation can continue operations and recover swiftly from any\ndisaster or significant disruption.\n\nThe following are the key components of implementing a BCDR plan:\n\n- Scope of the BCDR plan\n\n- Gathering Requirements and Factors\n\n- Analysis of the plan\n\n- Risk Assesment\n\n- Plan Design\n\n- Oter Plan Considerations\n\n- Planning, Exercising, Accessing the Plan\n\n- Test Plan Review\n",
    "srd.20260121194711.1": "Domain 4: Cloud Application Security \n17%",
    "srd.20260205183205.1": "According to the Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) and the Software Assurance Forum for\nExcellence in Code (SAFECode), establishing collaborative accountability is both crucial and\nchallenging in the development of a safety conscious application development program.\n\nThere are three distinct components to this effort:",
    "srd.20260209161222.1": "This principle dictates that security must be a fundamental consideration at every stage of the\nprocess.\n\nSecurity is not an afterthought or a response to a security flaw; it is an integral element of the\nprocess from the initial feasibility of the application through to its retirement.",
    "srd.20260209161246.1": "Everyone, from senior management to the newest team member, holds accountability for\nsecurity.\n\nThe notion that someone is not responsible for security is explicitly rejected. In practice,\npersonal accountability and trust are the bedrock of effective security.",
    "srd.20260209161304.1": "\uf0a7\nSecurity is not just a part of business operations; it is\ncentral to them, with a focus on secure computing\npermeating every aspect.\n\nEvery business goal incorporates an element of\nsecurity, reflecting its fundamental importance.\n\nUnderstanding these core principles requires a\ncomprehensive grasp of organisational culture,\nawareness of security vulnerabilities, and an\nalignment with the organisation's vision and goals.",
    "srd.20260209161734.1": "The primary pitfall is the failure to receive immediate and ongoing support from upper\nmanagement.\n\nIf senior leadership does not demonstrate their commitment by approving, adhering to\nsecurity policies, and adequately funding and staffing security projects aligned with the\norganisation's goals, efforts to instill a security culture are likely to fail.\n\nA common mistake is not fully understanding the organisation's culture. Even businesses within\nthe same industry can have vastly different cultures.\n\nIt requires careful planning to discern an organisation's unique culture and develop program\nlevel activities that integrate well within it to foster and sustain a security culture.\n\nAnother significant challenge is staffing. The size of an organisation may limit the availability of\nsecurity experts. Rather than being concentrated in a single team, these experts might be\ndispersed, serving as resources for various developmental processes.\n\nDevelopment teams in larger organisations may either utilise security experts as consultants or\nintegrate them directly into the team.\n\nIn both scenarios, ensuring uniformity in the development of security aware applications\nacross the organisation can be challenging. This task becomes particularly difficult without an\nestablished framework for secure software development.\n\nA mature organisation typically has a well defined process that incorporates security at every\nstage.\n\nThe absence of such a framework or process leads to inconsistent security practices and\nundermines the establishment of a security conscious organisational culture.\n\nA critical issue to consider is the impact of financial constraints on training. In times of budget\ntightening, funds allocated for training, especially for what is perceived as non essential\ntraining, are often among the first to be reduced.\n\nWhile it may seem easy to cut training programs when there are no immediate repercussions,\nan organisation committed to security will find ways to continue secure computing practices\nand security awareness training, even under financial constraints.\n\nTo maintain a broad range of training options while reducing expenses, several strategies can\nbe employed.\n\nThese include subscriptions to online security courses, as well as both asynchronous and\nsynchronous training methods. These alternatives eliminate the need for travel, allowing\ntraining initiatives to persist even with significantly reduced budgets.\n\nAdditionally, bringing trainers directly to the workplace can be a cost effective solution. When\nmultiple individuals require the same training, it may prove more economical to have the\ntrainer come onsite rather than sending staff to an external training location.\n",
    "srd.20260209162124.1": "Cloud computing is vulnerable to data breaches, compromised integrity, insecure APIs, and DoS\nattacks due to its extensive reliance on network systems.\n\nApplications and data transmissions over the Internet operate in potentially hostile\nenvironments.\n\nThe following are the Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP) Top 10:",
    "srd.20260205183428.1": "There are numerous models for SDLCs, ranging from sequential and linear techniques like\nWaterfall to interactive and incremental approaches like Spiral, Agile, and the more recent\nDevelopment and Operations (DevOps), which enhance deployment frequency and speed.\n\nAny SDLC, including an SSDLC, has numerous basic phases. They are as follows:\n\n",
    "srd.20260209163127.1": "\nGathering, reviewing, and understanding the business requirements for any system or software\ndesign, or even for improvements to an existing one, is crucial in achieving organisational goals\nand meeting customer expectations.\n\nWhen a project is conceptualised, the foundation of its business requirements should be a\ncomprehensive understanding of its necessity, objectives, and target audience.\n\nIncorporating feedback from as many stakeholders as possible will enhance the likelihood of\nadequately capturing and comprehending all requirements.\n\nWhere feasible, software users, whether internal to the organisation or from the public, should\nalso be involved.\n\nThis inclusion brings diverse perspectives and insights into what both management and\ninternal audiences are likely to expect. It is particularly important given that a software's\nperformance is ultimately judged by its usability and end user satisfaction.",
    "srd.20260205183532.1": "NOTES - SSDLC: \n\nThe Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SSDLC) integrates security measures throughout the software development process, unlike the traditional Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC), which often addresses security later. Below are two correct statements comparing SSDLC with SDLC:\n\nProactive Security Integration: SSDLC embeds security considerations from the planning stage, ensuring a proactive approach to identifying and mitigating risks early. In contrast, SDLC typically adopts a reactive approach, addressing security issues after the software is developed or during the testing phase.\n\nLower Cost of Fixing Issues: By detecting and resolving security vulnerabilities early in the development process, SSDLC reduces the cost and effort required to fix issues. In SDLC, addressing security flaws later in the lifecycle often results in higher costs and delays due to rework.",
    "srd.20260209164209.1": "Cloud specific risks refer to the unique vulnerabilities and threats that arise when organisations\nutilise cloud computing services.\n\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260209164725.1": "Threat Modeling Techniques\n\nTo effectively mitigate these risks, cloud security professionals use various threat modeling\ntechniques:\n\nSTRIDE: Identifies threats by categorising them into Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation,\nInformation Disclosure, Denial of Service, and Elevation of Privilege.\n\nDREAD: Assesses threats based on Damage potential, Reproducibility, Exploitability,\nAffected users, and Discoverability.\n\nPASTA: A risk centric methodology that integrates threat analysis into the organisational\nsecurity strategy, enhancing the identification of potential security flaws.",
    "srd.20260209164844.1": "Vulnerability Mitigation Strategies\n\nRobust Encryption:\n\nImplement strong encryption standards for data at rest and in transit to protect against\nunauthorised data breaches.\n\nAccess Control Enhancements:\n\nEmploy least privilege access principles, strong authentication mechanisms, and\ncomprehensive identity management solutions to control access to cloud resources.\n\nRegular Security Assessments:\n\nConduct vulnerability scans, penetration testing, and security audits to identify and address\nsecurity weaknesses promptly.",
    "srd.20260209170659.1": "The OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS) Project provides a comprehensive\nframework for evaluating the technical security measures implemented in online applications.\n\nThe primary objective of the OWASP ASVS Project is to establish a balanced and standardised\napproach to web application security verification.\n\nIt aims to bridge the gap between the varying levels of coverage and rigor available in the market\nby promoting the use of an open standard that can be adopted commercially.\n\nThis standard serves as a foundational framework for assessing the technological security measures\nimplemented within the application environment. It plays a critical role in safeguarding against\nvulnerabilities like SQL injection and cross site scripting (XSS).",
    "srd.20260209170817.1": "\nIt provides a platform for company executives and technical experts to engage in knowledge\nsharing and collaborative discussions focused on the development, improvement, and promotion\nof scalable and efficient software security systems.\n\nThe primary goal of SAFECode is to assist businesses in establishing or enhancing their software\nassurance programs.\n\nSAFECode's areas of focus include:\n\nThese encompass key aspects of the Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC), Application\nSecurity Control, Secure Design, Threat Modeling, and Secure Coding.\n\nSAFECode also addresses emerging technical considerations, ensuring that its guidance remains\nup to date and relevant in the evolving field of software security.",
    "srd.20260209171146.1": "\nVersioning and configuration management are vital considerations for any system.\n\nIn a cloud environment, these considerations take on added significance due to the rapid\nprovisioning and self elasticity inherent in cloud services. Ensuring system stability and\ncohesiveness becomes paramount.\n\nIn conventional data center deployments, upgrades and patches are typically applied directly\nto servers and hosts.\n\nHowever, in a cloud environment, a different approach is often employed. It involves the use of\nnew images and deployments to maintain systems and versions, rather than pushing individual\nchanges.\n\nThis approach includes scanning to verify the correct application of changes and thorough\ntesting to ensure system integrity.",
    "srd.20260209171407.1": "Functional Testing\n\nFunctional testing is a focused and specific assessment of a particular function or component\nwithin a system.\n\nWhen it comes to cloud based application and software development, there are standard\nconsiderations that apply to any project. However, additional considerations come into play\ndue to the unique nature of cloud technology.\n\nOne crucial aspect is understanding all legal and regulatory requirements relevant to the\nsystem when transitioning to the cloud.\n\nThis includes compliance with regulations specific to the jurisdiction where the cloud is hosted\nand the authorities overseeing it.\n\nThese requirements should align, at a minimum, with those applicable to the applications\nbeing migrated.",
    "srd.20260209171422.1": "Non-functional Testing\n\nNon functional testing focuses on aspects of a software application, such as performance,\nusability, and dependability, which are not covered by functional testing.\n\nNon functional testing is instrumental in assessing whether a system is prepared for use. Unlike\nfunctional testing, which examines the precise behaviors of a system in line with its functional\nrequirements, non functional testing specifies how the system operates.\n\nIn essence, non functional testing is conducted to evaluate and assess all non functional\nfactors that are not addressed by functional testing.\n\nBoth functional and non functional testing are essential components of comprehensive testing.\nNon functional testing encompasses critical aspects of an application, including speed,\nscalability, security, dependability, and efficiency.",
    "srd.20260209171917.1": "The purpose of security testing is to verify that the organisation's security strategy and\narchitecture are followed and that all security requirements have been satisfied.",
    "srd.20260209172011.1": "A white box solution refers to a testing or validation approach where the tester has full visibility into the internal structure, logic, and code of the software being evaluated. It is also called Clear Box, Structural, or Code-Based Testing and is used to ensure that the internal operations of a system work as intended, are secure, and optimized.\n\nIn this approach, testers analyze source code, understand control flows, and design test cases that cover statements, branches, conditions, and data flows. ",
    "srd.20260209172049.1": "Grey box testing technique that focuses on analyzing historical defect data to proactively identify potential failures before they occur in production. It leverages partial knowledge of the internal code to determine why a defect happened, enabling testers to design more targeted test cases.\n\nThe process involves reviewing past defect logs, identifying recurring failure patterns, and creating test cases that specifically target those weak points in the system. This approach helps in preventing defect recurrence and improving overall software quality.\n\n",
    "srd.20260209172102.1": "Black Box Testing is a software testing approach where the tester evaluates an application\u2019s functionality without any knowledge of its internal code, logic, or structure. The focus is entirely on inputs, outputs, and expected behavior based on requirements, making it a user-centric validation method.\n\nKey characteristics include:\n\nNo programming knowledge required.\n\nTests from an end-user perspective.\n\nApplicable to functional and non-functional requirements.\n\nOften used in system testing, acceptance testing, and regression testing.",
    "srd.20260209172227.1": "Abuse case testing is a method employed by software testers to assess the vulnerability of\ntheir software to potential risks.\n\nIn this process, testers start by compiling a list of known misuse cases and then proceed to\nactively attempt to exploit those use cases. This involves the application of manual and/or\nautomated attack techniques.",
    "srd.20260209172159.1": "\nToday, the foundation of digital transformation lies in cloud computing. Within the cloud\nservice model, Quality Assurance (QA) plays a pivotal role in delivering essential and high\nquality services.\n\nQA ensures the highest quality across all cloud service models, including public, private, hybrid,\nand community clouds, among others.\n\nIt encompasses planning, evaluation, and inspection to guarantee that the constructed system\naligns with and fulfills the customer's requirements.\n\nIn the realm of cloud computing, two primary concerns take center stage: security and privacy.\n\nQA actively addresses these concerns, striving to rectify privacy and trustworthiness issues and\nenhance overall system integrity.",
    "srd.20260209173224.1": "\nAPI security is paramount to the overall security of applications. The introduction of\ninadequately secured APIs, particularly when integrating APIs that are outside of an\napplication's control or security boundary, poses a significant risk.\n\nSuch a scenario can jeopardise the security of the entire dataset within the application,\npotentially leading to data exposure or loss.\n\nA Cloud Security Professional plays a critical role in ensuring that external APIs undergo the\nsame rigorous review and security testing as any internal program or code.\n\nThe absence of comprehensive testing and validation of external APIs can lead to the\nconsumption of insecure API calls and data by an application.\n\nWhether the APIs are based on REST or SOAP, it is essential to verify the appropriate security\nprotocols, such as SSL/TLS or encryption.",
    "srd.20260209173257.1": "\nSupply chain management can be divided into two key components. Firstly, it describes the\nneed for a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) and potentially the customer to engage third party\nproviders to deliver services.\n\nFor example, a CSP may rely on other vendors to provide the services required by their\ncustomers.\n\nThis aspect of supply chain management also encompasses vendor risk management. When\nestablishing relationships with these providers, it is crucial to consider both operational and\nsecurity aspects.\n\nMoreover, conventional supply chain management is increasingly transitioning to the cloud.\nThis shift introduces its own set of risks associated with cloud computing.\n\nCloud computing plays a transformative role in extending the scope and benefits of supply\nchain management. In today globally interconnected supply chains, sourcing goods often\nrequires access to both primary and secondary sources.\n\nCloud computing provides a solution to this challenge by enhancing infrastructure, leading to\noperational efficiency and financial gains within the supply chain.",
    "srd.20260209173333.1": "\nIn the recent years, cloud computing has witnessed a significant surge in popularity. This trend\nsignifies a substantial shift away from the traditional approach of storing, processing, and\nmanaging data on local servers or individual computers.\n\nInstead, businesses increasingly adopt a model that relies on a network of remote servers\nhosted on the Internet. This shift is often referred to as cloud computing.\n\nIt involves the management of cloud resources by an external company rather than an internal\none.\n\nModern software companies leverage cloud technology to deliver and license their software to\ncustomers while imposing access restrictions. Only users with a valid license are granted access\nto the software, a concept known as cloud based software licensing.\n\nThe key distinction between cloud based software license management and traditional\nlicensing methods lies in the significant reduction, if not elimination, of manual licensing\nprocedures for both the software vendor and end users.\n\nCloud based licensing is so efficient from the end user's standpoint that, ideally, they may not\neven realise it.\n\nIn other words, clients are not required to navigate additional \"license processes\" when\npurchasing, installing, and using a software application. From their perspective, licensing\nessentially becomes invisible.",
    "srd.20260209173428.1": "\nOpen source software plays a pivotal role in the IT industry and is widely embraced in many cloud environments.\n\nCloud developers frequently rely on open source software applications, development toolsets, and application frameworks to power their projects.\n\nWhat sets apart the most popular and widely used open source software packages is their rigorous development process.\n\nThey undergo extensive code review, testing, collaborative development, and scrutiny.\n\nThis level of examination often surpasses that of closed source and proprietary software packages.",
    "srd.20260209192204.1": "Supplement security devices play a crucial role in augmenting the layers and components of a defense in\ndepth architecture.\n\nIn a defense in depth architecture, the overarching strategy involves designing with multiple layers of security that overlap and mutually reinforce each other.\n\nThis approach is aimed at establishing a resilient and robust security framework.\n\nBy carefully selecting supplement security devices, a Certified Cloud Security Professional (CCSP) can enhance an organisation's security architecture.\n\nThese devices contribute to strengthening the border defenses, fortifying the overall security posture.",
    "srd.20260209193252.1": "It is a layer 7 firewall that understands HTTP traffic",
    "srd.20260209193340.1": "It is a layer 7 monitoring device that understands SQL commands\n",
    "srd.20260209193440.1": "XML transforms the sensitive data and way services to developers and cloud users",
    "srd.20260209193544.1": "Firewalls can be distributed among the SaaS, PaaS, and IaaS landscapes",
    "srd.20260209193614.1": "It is a device that filters the API traffic",
    "srd.20260209193011.1": "\nWhen working with cloud based systems, it is essential to bear in mind that they operate in\nboth trusted and untrusted networks. These environments can also be described as hostile and\nsemi hostile.\n\nIn light of this, it is crucial to implement data encryption for information stored within and\ntransmitted between services and systems operating in the cloud.\n\nEncryption serves as a fundamental safeguard for ensuring data security in such diverse and potentially challenging network settings.\n",
    "srd.20260209193843.1": "A protocol that ensures the privacy among communicating applications and their uses on the internet",
    "srd.20260209194008.1": "A standard security technology to establish an encrypted link among a browser and a web serverq",
    "srd.20260209194129.1": "A network that is constructed to connect to a private network by using public wires (usually the internet)",
    "srd.20260209194324.1": "\u2022\nA method to encrypt all the data that is associated with the operation and use of\nvirtual machine",
    "srd.20260209194416.1": "\u2022\nA method to encrypt a single volume on a drive",
    "srd.20260209194814.1": "Refers to the segregation and isolation of information or processes within the same\nsystem or application, primarily for security purposes.\n\nIt serves as a means of data isolation, ensuring that different communities and user\npopulations remain isolated from similar data, enhancing security.\n\nThe need for sandboxing can arise from internal factors, such as organisational policies, or\nexternal sources, such as regulatory or legal requirements.\n\norganisations utilise a sandbox environm```````ent to gain a comprehensive understanding of\napplication operations.\n\nIt allows them to fully test applications and closely observe file behavior for any signs of\nmalicious activity.",
    "srd.20260209201941.1": "\nInfrastructure virtualisation is a fundamental component of cloud computing, while\ncontainerisation of applications is a key element in various cloud application architectures,\nincluding microservices.\n\nClients have the flexibility to use a range of devices, such as laptops, virtual machines, or\nmobile devices, to run virtualised desktops, containers, virtual machines, or business\napplications.\n\nThese programs operate independently of the host operating system and provide secure access\nto corporate applications and data. They achieve this without the need to virtualise the entire\ndevice.\n\nApplication virtualisation through containers enables applications to be packaged with all their\ndependencies.\n\nThis approach allows for precise configuration control, patch management, and the\nimplementation of repeatable build procedures for the containerised applications.\n\nContainers play a vital role in separating corporate data and software from the user's device.\n\nThis separation allows companies to remotely wipe corporate data, applications, and access\nwithout affecting the employee's personal device. It provides a crucial layer of security for both\nemployees and employers.\n\nApplication virtualisation contributes to making the BYOD movement safer by ensuring that\ncorporate assets remain isolated.\n\nKubernetes (often abbreviated as K8s) stands out as a popular tool for orchestrating\ncontainerisation. While there are alternative containerisation solutions, Kubernetes was\npurpose built for container orchestration.\n\n\nIn addition to the management of containers and applications, orchestration software plays a\ncritical role in ensuring security and compliance with an organisation's secure computing\npolicies and standards.\n\nCloud orchestration empowers customers to efficiently and economically oversee all their\ncloud resources from a centralised platform.\n\nAs the migration to the cloud continues to accelerate, the complexity of managing corporate\ncloud requirements is set to increase.\n\nOrchestration addresses this challenge by facilitating the deployment of cloud applications,\ncontainerised solutions, and services.\n\nIt achieves this while automating workflows, managing user accounts, and ensuring adherence\nto corporate policies within the cloud environment.",
    "srd.20260205184120.1": "\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260210173651.1": "Federated identity operates much like single sign on (SSO), where a specific digital ID grants\naccess to multiple systems.\n\nWith a federated identity, a digital ID can access applications across various Cloud Service\nProviders (CSPs), multi cloud resources, and on premises resources.\n\nFederation also enables SSO across systems in different organisations. These organisations\nmight include divisions of the same corporation, multiple cloud providers, CSPs, or other\nentities.\n\nHowever, there are security challenges associated with federated identities. The primary\nconcern is that if a digital ID is compromised on one system, it becomes compromised on all\nfederated platforms.\n\nThis underscores the critical importance of robust Identity and Access Management (IAM)\nsystems across all federated systems to ensure security.\n\nIt is crucial to closely safeguard both the on premises Identity and Access Management (IAM)\nsystem and the IAM systems of each cloud vendor and service provider to prevent\nunauthorised usage.\n\nSimilar to Single Sign On (SSO), federated identification has a drawback. When one of the\nfederated systems is compromised, it exposes vulnerabilities in all federated systems.\n\nOne significant challenge stems from the fact that systems belonging to different organisations may not share the same level of trust as systems within the same organisation.\n\nAdditionally, no single organisation has control over how other organisations secure their\nsystems or evaluate their users.\n",
    "srd.20260210173935.1": "\nIdentity providers can be CSP services or services provided\nby third party entities.\n\nIn multi cloud scenarios, opting for a third party solution\ncan often be the most efficient choice for customers, as it\nprovides a unified solution.\n\nInstead of replacing existing cloud security technologies like\nCloud Access Security Brokers (CASBs), identity providers\nand CASBs work in collaboration to establish a layered\nsecurity defense.\n\nIn this setup, Identity and Access Management (IAM) plays a\ncrucial role in ensuring that users are both permitted and\nauthenticated before gaining access to cloud resources.",
    "srd.20260210174101.1": "\nSingle Sign On (SSO) streamlines access to all approved systems within a single Identity and\nAccess Management (IAM) system after a single authentication process.\n\nWith SSO, users can seamlessly switch between systems without the need for repeated\nauthentication.\n\nMonitoring all access within an SSO system is of paramount importance. If a digital ID is\ncompromised, it poses a security risk to all IAM environment systems to which the user has\naccess.\n\nHowever, SSO offers the advantage of simplifying central monitoring and access management.\nIt also reduces the complexity of resetting multiple credentials in the event of a compromise.\n\nManaging and monitoring access becomes more challenging when users have multiple\ncredentials.\n\nIt is essential to monitor the access and usage of all identities to detect any malicious activities.\n\nOrganisations also require well defined procedures for responding to malicious activity\neffectively.\n\nThe use of federation expands the capabilities of SSO. Federated access enables SSO across\nvarious Identity and Access Management (IAM) systems, including both cloud and on premise\nenvironments.\n\nHowever, this expansion also increases the risk associated with compromised identities, as\nthey potentially affect a larger number of systems.\n\nTherefore, vigilant monitoring and proactive response to malicious activities become even\nmore critical in federated SSO environments.\n",
    "srd.20260210174407.1": "\nMultifactor Authentication, also known as strong authentication or two factor authentication, operates on the fundamental principle of enhancing transaction security by adding an additional layer of verification.\n\nIn an MFA system, the user is required to provide at least two of the following:\n\nWhat they know (like a password)\n\nWhat they have (like a display token with a random number displayed)\n\nWhat they are (like biometrics)\n\nMultifactor Authentication (MFA) employs various methods \nto enhance security, including:\n\n\nOne Time Passwords: These are used during the provisioning process and when communicating first login passwords to users.\n\nStep Up Authentication: An additional layer of authentication that further validates the user's identity.\n\nCommon MFA Methods: \n\nChallenge Questions: Users answer predefined challenge\nquestions to verify their identity.\n\nOut of Band Authentication: Authentication involves a secondary, separate channel or method, adding an extra layer of security.\n",
    "srd.20260210174752.1": "\nA Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) is a software product or service positioned between a\ncompany's on premises infrastructure and a cloud provider's infrastructure.\n\nFunctioning as a gatekeeper, the CASB enables organisations to extend their reach beyond their\nnetwork boundaries and enforce security policies in the cloud environment.\n\nUsually, the CASBs offer the following:\n\n- Firewalls\n- Authentication\n- Web Application Firewalls\n- Data Loss Prevention\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260210175024.1": "Cloud secrets management refers to the methods and technologies employed by businesses to\nsafeguard and manage their digital credentials, including signatures and keys, in a cloud based\nenvironment.\n\nKey priorities of cloud secrets management include accessibility and authorisation . It is\nimportant to note that each cloud based platform may implement secrets management\nservices differently.\n\nWhile the services and outcomes may be similar, different cloud providers offer various useful\ntools to achieve these objectives.\n\nWithout proper cloud based secrets management and a knowledgeable DevOps team, keys\nand secrets could potentially be exposed to everyone, including malicious actors.\n\nSome instances of cloud based secrets include the following:\n\n- APIs\n- SSH keys\n- Encrption keys\n- Passwords\n- Tokens\n- Private Certificats",
    "srd.20260121194820.1": "Domian 5: Cloud Security Operations\n16%",
    "srd.20260210194206.1": "In a public cloud, computing resources are shared with other companies. The following are\nsome important considerations for resource sharing:\n\nLegal\n\nCompatibility\n\nControl\n\nLog data\n\nPCI (Portable Spacecraft Simulator), DSS (Decision Support System) access\n\nUpgrades and changes\n\nFailover technology\n\nCompliance\n\nRegulations\n\nOutsourcing\n\nPlacement of security\n\nVirtualisation\n\nOperating system and application files\n\nData fluidity",
    "srd.20260210185022.1": "\nHardware Specific Security Configuration Requirements refers to the tailored security measures\nand settings that must be applied to various types of hardware to mitigate vulnerabilities and\nprotect against potential threats.\n\nThis entails a broad spectrum of devices ranging from servers and workstations to routers and\nswitches, each of which may have distinct security configurations based on their roles and the\ndata they handle.\n\nThe virtualisation platform dictates the management tools to be installed on the host.\n\nThe fundamental principles applied to the security of any system are also applied to the\nsecurity of virtualisation management tools.\n\nThe most recent tools should be installed on each host, and guidelines for updating these\ntools should be incorporated into the configuration management plan.\n\nAdequate server resources should be allocated to facilitate the migration of VMs (Virtual\nMachines) during updates to the virtualisation platform, as such updates may necessitate\nserver downtime.\n\nThe following are key Aspects:",
    "srd.20260210191433.1": "Servers, whether hosting databases, applications, or web services, require stringent security\ncontrols to safeguard the data they store and process.\n\nThis typically includes using BIOS passwords to prevent unauthorised changes to system\nsettings, disabling unused ports and services to minimise attack vectors, and configuring\nfirewalls and intrusion detection systems to monitor and block malicious activities.",
    "srd.20260210191523.1": "\nWorkstations and other endpoints are common targets for attackers looking to gain entry into\nnetwork systems.\n\nSecurity configurations for these devices include installing and regularly updating antivirus and anti malware software, enabling firewalls, and implementing full disk encryption to protect data at rest.\n\nUser access should be controlled through the use of strong, complex passwords, or better yet,\nthrough multi factor authentication mechanisms that add an extra layer of security.",
    "srd.20260210185348.1": "Network Devices\n\nDevices that control data flow across networks, such as routers, switches, and firewalls, must\nbe specially configured to prevent unauthorised access and ensure data integrity.\n\nThis includes changing default passwords, updating firmware to patch vulnerabilities, and\nconfiguring secure protocols for remote management, such as using SSH instead of Telnet.\n\n\nNetwork\n\nBoth virtual and physical network devices play significant roles within a cloud environment.\n\nThe usage of virtual network devices adds a layer of complexity above and beyond the traditional data center model.\n\nAlthough virtual and physical network devices may perform identical operations, they have distinct considerations when it comes to potential problems, security, and their impact on the data center. \n",
    "srd.20260210185358.1": "Storage\n\nStorage traffic should be isolated and segregated on its own LAN whenever possible in virtualised systems.\n\nDue to their heavy network utilisation and critical importance, storage systems must be classified as LAN traffic rather than WAN traffic.\n\nLatency issues are the primary concern for storage systems, in contrast to typical network traffic,which tends to be more flexible and resilient.\n\nWhile general application traffic and web service calls usually have built in or easily added\nencryption capabilities, this is not typically the case with storage systems.\n\nWhen vendors provide this capability, it should always be used. However, in cases where it is not feasible or possible, separating storage traffic from application and user traffic on a dedicated LAN will greatly enhance the security and confidentiality of data transmissions.",
    "srd.20260210185556.1": "Memory is the second major hardware component of the system, serving as the storage bank for\nthe information that the computer requires to keep readily available.\n\nRead Only Memory\n\nROM operates exactly as the name implies; it is the memory that the PC can read but cannot\nchange. Standard ROM chip contents are permanently set at the factory and cannot be altered by\nthe end user.\n\nRead Only Memory chips often include \"bootstrap\" information that is used by computers to start up before loading an operating system from the disk.\n\nRandom Access Memory\n\nRAM is defined as readable and wriable memory, containing\ninformation used by the computer throughout processing.\n\nHowever, RAM retains its contents only when power is\ncontinuously supplied to it.\n\nUnlike read only memory, when the computer is turned off, all\nthe data stored in RAM disappears.",
    "srd.20260210193749.1": "Certain specialised devices, such as Point of Sale (POS) systems or Internet of Things (IoT)\ndevices, have specific security requirements due to the sensitive nature of their functions.\n\nFor these devices, it is essential to ensure that they are only running necessary services and that their software is regularly updated to guard against exploits.\n\nIt is also vital to monitor these devices continuously for signs of suspicious activity.",
    "srd.20260210185812.1": "\nWhile the majority of individuals are aware that CPU stands for Central Processing Unit, few fully comprehend its function.\n\nIf your company operates in the public cloud, where virtual CPUs (vCPUs) share physical CPU\nresources, understanding what a CPU does can be crucial. In a physical environment, a CPU serves as the operational center of a computer.\n\nIt receives instructions from applications and programs, executes the parts of instructions it can handle, and delegates tasks it cannot perform to other hardware components before sending the completed task to memory and instructing memory on how to handle it.\n\nIn the cloud, a CPU works a bit differently. When provisioning a Virtual Machine (VM), the CPU\ncapacity allocated by you represents a share of the physical host's CPU.",
    "srd.20260210190046.1": "\nThe virtualisation component that creates, manages, and operates the virtual machines is the\nhypervisor, also referred to as the virtual machine monitor (VMM). There are two types:",
    "srd.20260210190210.1": "The Type 1 hypervisor is a bare metal or native hypervisor. In this configuration, there is no host operating system; instead, the hypervisor is directly installed on the hardware, which typically serves as the host OS.\n\nThese hypervisors are commonly used to support server virtualisation, allowing for the\nmaximisation of hardware resources while eliminating resource or risk reductions caused by the\npresence of a host OS.",
    "srd.20260210191042.1": "\u2022\nA Type 2 hypervisor is defined as a hosted hypervisor. In this\nconfiguration, a standard operating system is present on the\nhardware, and then the hypervisor is installed as another\nsoftware application.\n\nThese hypervisors are commonly used in desktop\ndeployments, where guest OSs provide secure sandbox\nenvironments for testing new code, giving users access to the\ncapabilities of the host OS, supporting applications from\nalternate OSs, and enabling the execution of legacy\n\napplications.",
    "srd.20260210194533.1": "The fundamental principles applied to the security of any system are also applied to the\nsecurity of virtualisation management tools.\n\n\nThe combination of security protocols within the actual virtual management infrastructure\nand the use of network segregation and isolation is known as layered defense.\n\nRobust role based authentication and strict limitations on the number of employees with\naccess to the hypervisors and the management plane promote strong access control within\nthe system.\n\nLike any system, even with effective segregation and access control, consistent and\ncomprehensive logging and monitoring are crucial to ensure that only authorised access and\nappropriate system resources are used by administrative staff.\n",
    "srd.20260210194849.1": "\nThe installation of guest operating system (OS) virtualisation toolsets refers to the process of setting up and configuring software within a virtual machine (VM) to optimise the performance, management and integration of the guest OS with the host environment.\n\nThe primary purpose of these toolsets is to enhance the functionality and user experience within a VM by providing drivers and system applications tailored to the virtual environment.\n\nThese enhancements include improved video performance, mouse and keyboard integration,\nseamless window resizing, shared folders, better network adaptability, and clipboard sharing\nbetween the host and the guest OS.\n\nAdditionally, these toolsets often offer tools for automated guest OS updates and system\nmanagement tools that help in monitoring and managing the health and performance of the guest\nOS.",
    "srd.20260210195000.1": "Common Features of Virtualisation Toolsets\n\nDevice Drivers: Enhanced drivers for the virtual hardware, including graphics and network\nadapters, ensure better performance and usability of the guest OS by allowing it to run more\nefficiently and stably within the virtual environment.\n\nSystem Utilities: Tools and utilities for system management and integration tasks, such as time synchronisation between host and guest, automated logins, and file exchange mechanisms.\n\nDrag and Drop Functionality: Enables users to drag and drop files between the host and the guest operating systems, simplifying data transfer.\n\nClipboard Sharing: Allows copying and pasting of text and other data between the host and the\nguest OS.\n\nImproved Input Devices Handling: Enhances the responsiveness and synchronisation of keyboards\nand pointing devices across the host and guest operating systems.",
    "srd.20260210200220.1": "Access controls are critical for securing cloud environments, ensuring that only authorised\nindividuals can access sensitive resources, whether locally or remotely.\n\nImplementing robust access control measures involves a combination of physical security\npractices and logical controls.",
    "srd.20260210200321.1": "\nLocal access controls are concerned with securing physical access to cloud infrastructure\ncomponents such as servers, networking equipment, and data storage devices located in data\ncentres or other facilities. \n\nThe following are some of the key practices include:\n\n- Physical Security Measures\n- On-site Authentications\n",
    "srd.20260210200339.1": "Remote access controls focus on securing access to the cloud infrastructure from external\nlocations.\n\nAs remote work and cloud based services become more prevalent, ensuring secure remote\naccess is paramount. \n\nThe following are some of the key practices include:\n\n1 - Virtual Private Network\n2 - Multi factor Authentication\n3 - Access Control Policies\n4 - Endpoint Security\n5 - Monitoring and Logging\n6 - Zero Trust Architecture",
    "srd.20260210200839.1": "Effective network security management in cloud environments requires a comprehensive\napproach that integrates preventive, detective, corrective, and recovery controls.\n\nThis integrated strategy ensures the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of network\nresources and data.\n",
    "srd.20260210201045.1": "The foundation of robust network security starts with secure network configurations that\nminimise vulnerabilities:\n\n1. Network Device Hardening: Modify default configurations, usernames, and passwords.\nRegularly update firmware to patch known vulnerabilities.\n\n2. Segmentation and Isolation: Use VLANs and subnetting to control traffic flow and isolate\nnetwork segments, reducing the spread of attacks.\n\n3. Encryption: Encrypt data in transit using SSL/TLS, IPSec, or SSH to protect against\neavesdropping and man in the middle attacks.\n\n4. Regular Audits and Compliance Checks : Perform security audits to assess the effectiveness of implemented controls and ensure compliance with standards like HIPAA, PCI DSS, or GDPR.",
    "srd.20260210201213.1": "\nImplementing various types of network security controls is essential for protecting network\nassets:\n\n1. Preventive Controls: Firewalls and access control lists (ACLs) block unauthorised access and traffic. Antivirus and anti malware software prevent the installation of malicious software.\n\n2. Detective Controls: Intrusion detection systems (IDS) and security information and event\nmanagement (SIEM) systems detect and alert on suspicious activities.\n\n3. Corrective Controls: Patch management and change management processes address\nvulnerabilities and apply necessary modifications to thwart potential attacks.\n\n4. Recovery Controls: Implement data backup solutions and disaster recovery plans to restore IT operations and ensure data integrity following an incident.",
    "srd.20260210201434.1": "Integrating these controls involves several best practices:\n\n1. Unified Security Policies: Develop and enforce unified security policies that govern all network activities to ensure a consistent security posture.\n\n2. Comprehensive Monitoring: Utilise network monitoring tools to continuously watch for\nunusual activity that could indicate security threats or network breaches.\n\n3. Advanced Threat Protection: Employ advanced threat protection technologies such as next\ngeneration firewalls (NGFWs) and advanced persistent threat (APT) protection systems that\nprovide more sophisticated detection and response mechanisms.\n\n4. Collaborative Incident Response: Foster a collaborative incident response team that operates across different departments to quickly address and mitigate security incidents.",
    "srd.20260210201652.1": "For effective implementation of integrated network security management, consider the\nfollowing strategies:\n\n1. Security by Design: Integrate security into the network design phase to address potential risks proactively rather than reactively.\n\n2. Risk Assessment: Regularly conduct risk assessments to identify and prioritise risks based on their potential impact on network operations.\n\n",
    "srd.20260210203111.1": "OS hardening is the process of securing an operating system by reducing its surface of vulnerability. \n\nThis process involves configuring and enhancing the security settings of the OS to protect it against various types of attacks.\n\nHardening measures typically include the removal of unnecessary services, applications, and\nnetwork protocols, as well as the implementation of stringent access controls and security policies.\n",
    "srd.20260210203200.1": "Baselines are predefined sets of security configurations and settings that serve as a standard for securing an OS.\n\nThese baselines are created based on industry standards,\nregulatory requirements, and best practices. The process\ninvolves:\n\n1. Assessment: Identifying the current security posture of\nthe OS by comparing it against the baseline.\n\n2. Implementation: Applying the baseline configurations to\nensure that the OS settings align with the security\nrequirements.\n\n3. Validation: Verifying that the baseline has been correctly\nimplemented and that the OS is secure.",
    "srd.20260210203343.1": "\nMonitoring is a continuous process that involves observing and analysing the performance and\nsecurity of the OS to detect any deviations from the baseline. This includes:\n\n1. Log Management: Collecting and analysing logs from various sources such as system logs,\napplication logs, and security logs to identify potential security incidents.\n\n2. Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS): Implementing IDS to monitor network and system activities for malicious actions or policy violations.\n\n3. Regular Audits: Conducting periodic security audits to ensure that the OS remains in compliance with the established baselines and to identify any vulnerabilities that may have been introduced.",
    "srd.20260210203437.1": "Remediation involves taking corrective actions to address vulnerabilities or deviations identified during the monitoring process.\n\nThis ensures that the OS remains secure and compliant with the baseline. Key steps in remediation include:\n\n1. Patch Management: Regularly applying patches and updates to fix known vulnerabilities and\nenhance the security of the OS.\n\n2. Configuration Management: Adjusting configurations to correct deviations from the baseline and improve security settings.\n\n3. Incident Response: Implementing incident response procedures to address security breaches\npromptly and effectively, minimising the impact on the OS and the overall cloud environment.",
    "srd.20260210203741.1": "Patch management involves the process of identifying, acquiring, testing, and installing\nupdates (patches) for software applications and systems to enhance security, improve\nfunctionality, and fix bugs.\n\nEffective patch management is critical for maintaining the security and stability of cloud\ninfrastructure, protecting against vulnerabilities, and ensuring compliance with security\npolicies and regulations.\n\nChallenges in Cloud Patch Management\n\n1. Scalability: Cloud environments often involve a large number of virtual machines and\napplications spread across various regions and data centers, making the patching process\ncomplex and extensive.\n\n2. Diverse Environments: Different types of software, operating systems, and configurations are used in cloud environments, requiring a tailored approach to patch management.\n\n3. Downtime and Availability: Patching can lead to downtime, impacting the availability of\nservices. Balancing patch application with minimal disruption is crucial.\n\n4. Automation and Orchestration: Manual patching is impractical at scale. Automating patch\nmanagement processes is essential to ensure timely and efficient updates.",
    "srd.20260210203912.1": "1. Identification and Assessment:\n\nRegularly scan the cloud environment to identify missing patches and assess their criticality\nbased on the potential impact on security and operations.\n\nUtilise vulnerability management tools and threat intelligence to stay updated on new\nvulnerabilities and patches.",
    "srd.20260210204249.1": "Obtain patches from trusted sources, such as software vendors and repositories.\n\nTest patches in a staging environment that mirrors the production environment to ensure\ncompatibility and stability, preventing unforeseen issues in live systems.",
    "srd.20260210204300.1": "Prioritise patches based on the severity of vulnerabilities and the criticality of the systems\ninvolved.\n\nUse automation tools to deploy patches across multiple systems and environments. Tools\nlike AWS Systems Manager, Microsoft Azure Update Management Patch Management\nstreamline this process.\n\nImplement a phased rollout strategy to minimise the impact on operations, starting with\nless critical systems and gradually moving to more critical ones.",
    "srd.20260210204459.1": "\nInfrastructure as Code (IaC) is a key DevOps practice that involves managing and provisioning\ncomputing infrastructure through machine readable definition files, rather than through\nphysical hardware configuration or interactive configuration tools.\n\nThis approach ensures that the infrastructure is versioned and reusable, promoting\nconsistency and reducing human error.",
    "srd.20260210204618.1": "1. Consistency & Standerization\n\n2. Version control\n\n3. Automation & Effieciency\n\n4. Scalibility & Flexibility",
    "srd.20260210204822.1": "1. Configuration Management Tools:\n\nTools like Ansible, Puppet, and Chef are used to automate the management of configuration\nfiles and software installations across servers. These tools help in maintaining desired states for server configurations.\n\n2. Orchestration Tools:\n\nTools such as Kubernetes and Docker Swarm are used to manage the deployment, scaling,\nand operations of containerised applications. They orchestrate the infrastructure resources\nneeded for applications to run efficiently.\n\n3. Provisioning Tools:\n\nTools like Terraform and AWS CloudFormation are employed to define and manage\ninfrastructure resources through code.\n\n4. Monitoring and Logging:\n\nImplementing robust monitoring and logging solutions is essential for tracking the\nperformance and health of the infrastructure.\n\nTools like Prometheus, Grafana, and ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) are\ncommonly used for this purpose.",
    "srd.20260210204852.1": "1. Security and Compliance: Ensure that the IaC strategy includes security best practices such as secret management, least privilege access, and compliance with regulatory standards.\n\n2. Code Quality and Maintenance: Regularly review and refactor IaC scripts to maintain code\nquality and adapt to changing requirements.\n\nEnsuring the availability of clustered hosts is a critical aspect of maintaining the physical and logical infrastructure of a cloud environment.\n\nClustered hosts are groups of interconnected servers that work together to improve\nperformance, load balancing, and failover capabilities.",
    "srd.20260210204918.1": "\nEnsuring the availability of clustered hosts is a critical aspect of maintaining the physical and\nlogical infrastructure of a cloud environment.\n\nClustered hosts are groups of interconnected servers that work together to improve\nperformance, load balancing, and failover capabilities.",
    "srd.20260211153244.1": "In cloud environments, a guest operating system (OS) is an operating system installed on a\nvirtual machine (VM).\n\nThis OS operates within the virtualised environment provided by the host, allowing users to\nrun applications and services as if they were on a physical machine.\n\n",
    "srd.20260211153347.1": "High Availability Strategies\n\n1. Redundancy\n2. Failover Mechanisms\n3. Snapshot and Backup Management",
    "srd.20260211153458.1": "1. Proactive Monitoring:\n\nPerformance Metrics: Continuously monitor key performance metrics of the guest OS, such\nas CPU usage, memory utilisation, disk I/O, and network activity.\n\nHealth Checks: Perform regular health checks to ensure that the guest OS is running\noptimally and detect any potential issues before they escalate.",
    "srd.20260211153534.1": "2. Patch Management:\n\nRegular Updates: Keep the guest OS updated with the latest patches and security updates.\nThis reduces vulnerabilities and enhances the stability and performance of the system.\n\nAutomated Patch Deployment: Use automated tools to deploy patches and updates,\nminimising the risk of human error and reducing downtime.",
    "srd.20260211153635.1": "\nPerformance and capacity monitoring are essential to ensure that the infrastructure operates\nefficiently and meets the demands of users and applications.\n\nThese monitoring processes help identify potential bottlenecks, resource limitations, and\nperformance issues before they impact the end users.",
    "srd.20260211154327.1": "Purpose and Importance: Backup and restore functions are critical for ensuring data integrity and\navailability in both host and guest operating systems.\n\nThese functions help in recovering data during unexpected failures, cyber attacks, or system\ncrashes, ensuring business continuity.",
    "srd.20260211154414.1": "\nFull Backup: A complete copy of all data, essential for a baseline.\n\nIncremental Backup: Only the changes made since the last backup, reducing storage needs.\n\nDifferential Backup: Cumulative changes since the last full backup, balancing storage and\nrecovery time.\n",
    "srd.20260211154546.1": "Selecting Backup Software: Choose reliable backup solutions compatible with the host OS, such\nas Veeam, Acronis, or native tools like Windows Server Backup.\n\nScheduling Backups: Define backup schedules to minimise system downtime and impact on\nperformance. Typically, full backups are scheduled weekly, with incremental backups daily.\n\nStorage Solutions: Configure storage options, including local storage, Network Attached Storage\n(NAS), and cloud storage solutions like AWS S3 or Azure Blob\n\nEncryption and Compression: Enable encryption for data security and compression to optimise\nstorage space.\n\nVerification and Testing: Regularly test backup integrity and restore procedures to ensure\nreliability and readiness.",
    "srd.20260211161157.1": "\nSnapshot Based Backups:\n\nSnapshots: Take point in time snapshots of virtual machines (VMs) running guest OS. This\nmethod is efficient for VM level backup and quick restores.\n\nAgent Based Backups:\n\nBackup Agents: Install backup agents within the guest OS to facilitate file level backups. Tools\nlike Veeam Agent or Azure Backup can be used.\n\nConfiguration Steps:\n\nBackup Agent Installation: Deploy backup agents on each guest OS, ensuring they are\nconfigured to communicate with the backup server.\n\n\nHost OS Restore:\n\nBooting from Recovery Media: Use recovery media to boot the host system in case of a\ncomplete failure.\n\nRestoring System State: Restore the full system or selected components from the most recent\nbackup.\n\nGuest OS Restore:\n\nRestoring from Snapshots: Revert VMs to a specific snapshot for quick recovery. This method is\nuseful for recent failures or minor issues.\n\nFile Level Restores: Use backup agents to restore specific files or directories within the guest OS.\nThis is ideal for partial data loss scenarios.",
    "srd.20260211161504.1": "The management plane is a critical component in cloud infrastructure, responsible for the\ncontrol and administration of cloud resources.\n\nIt provides the interface and tools for administrators to manage the cloud environment\neffectively.",
    "srd.20260211161636.1": "\nUser Interface (UI): Web based consoles and dashboards for visual management and\nconfiguration.\n\nApplication Programming Interfaces (APIs): Programmatic interfaces for automating\nmanagement tasks.\n\nCommand Line Interface (CLI): Text based interface for direct interaction and scripting.\n\u00a9 The Knowledge Academy Ltd",
    "srd.20260211161725.1": "\nResource Provisioning: Adding, modifying, and deleting\ncloud resources such as virtual machines, storage, and\nnetworks.\n\nMonitoring and Logging: Collecting and analysing data\non resource usage, performance, and security events.\n\nPolicy Enforcement: Implementing and enforcing\npolicies related to security, compliance, and resource\nusage.\n\nAccess Control: Managing user identities, roles, and\npermissions to ensure only authorised users can perform\nspecific actions.",
    "srd.20260211161922.1": "Incident management within the factor of implementing operational controls and standards is\na critical process that ensures the effective handling of unexpected events or disruptions that\ncould impact the normal functioning of IT services and infrastructure.\n\nThe following are the key aspects of incident management:",
    "srd.20260211162038.1": "\nMonitoring Tools: Implementing robust monitoring tools and systems (e.g., SIEM, network\nmonitoring tools, application performance monitoring) to detect incidents promptly.\n\nUser Reporting: Establishing channels for users to report incidents, such as helpdesk systems,\nemail, or hotlines.\n\nAutomated Alerts: Configuring automated alerts to notify IT staff immediately when an\nincident is detected.\n",
    "srd.20260211162258.1": "\nSeverity Levels: Defining severity levels for incidents based on their impact on business\noperations, ranging from minor issues to critical outages.\n\nImpact Analysis: Assessing the potential impact of the incident on various business functions\nand prioritising incidents accordingly to ensure that the most critical issues are addressed first.",
    "srd.20260211162410.1": "\nIncident Response Team: Establishing a dedicated incident response team responsible for\nmanaging and resolving incidents. This team should include members with the necessary skills\nand authority to take appropriate actions.\n\nResponse Procedures: Developing and maintaining detailed incident response procedures,\nincluding steps for initial assessment, containment, eradication and recovery.\n",
    "srd.20260211162510.1": "\nInternal Communication: Ensuring clear and timely communication among IT staff,\nmanagement, and other relevant stakeholders during an incident.\n\nExternal Communication: Managing communication with external parties, such as customers,\npartners, and vendors, to provide updates and manage expectations.",
    "srd.20260211162601.1": "Root Cause Analysis: Conducting a thorough analysis to identify the root cause of the incident\nand implementing corrective actions to prevent recurrence.\n\nService Restoration: Taking steps to restore affected services to normal operation as quickly as\npossible, using predefined recovery procedures and plans.",
    "srd.20260211162709.1": "\nProblem Management in the factor of implementing operational controls and standards\ninvolves identifying, analysing, and resolving underlying issues that cause incidents within an\nIT infrastructure.\n\nThis process aims to prevent problems from recurring, reduce the impact of incidents, and\nensure the stability and reliability of IT services.",
    "srd.20260211162906.1": "\nProactive Identification: Identifying problems before they cause incidents by analysing trends\nand patterns in incident data, system logs, and performance metrics.\n\nReactive Identification: Logging problems in response to recurring incidents or significant\noutages reported by users or detected by monitoring systems.",
    "srd.20260211163053.1": "\nCategorisation: Classifying problems based on their nature, such as hardware failures,\nsoftware bugs, network issues, or configuration errors. This helps in assigning the right\nresources for resolution.\n\nPrioritisation: Assessing the impact and urgency of problems to prioritise them appropriately.\nHigh impact problems affecting critical services are addressed first.",
    "srd.20260211163225.1": "\nTechniques: Using techniques like the Five Whys, Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa), and Failure\nMode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) to systematically investigate and identify the root cause of\nproblems.\n\nData Collection: Gathering relevant data from logs, incident records, and user reports to\nsupport the analysis process.",
    "srd.20260211163424.1": "Solution Implementation: Developing and implementing solutions to address the root cause of\nthe problem. This may involve applying patches, reconfiguring systems, replacing faulty\nhardware, or updating procedures.\n\nWorkarounds: Implementing temporary workarounds to mitigate the impact of the problem\nwhile a permanent solution is being developed.",
    "srd.20260211163503.1": "Documentation: Documenting the problem, its root cause, and the solution in a knowledge\nbase. This helps in sharing knowledge across the organisation and aids in quicker resolution of\nsimilar issues in the future.\n\nLessons Learned: Conducting post implementation reviews to capture lessons learned and\nimprove problem management processes.",
    "srd.20260211163611.1": "Release Management encompasses the planning, design, build, configuration, and testing of\nhardware and software releases to create a set of release components.\n\nIt ensures that new features, improvements, and fixes are bundled into manageable releases\nthat can be effectively deployed. The release management process involves:",
    "srd.20260211163748.1": "\nRelease Planning: This involves defining the scope and content of releases, including\nidentifying which features, updates and bug fixes will be included. It also includes setting\ntimelines and resource requirements.\n\nRelease Calendar: Creating a release calendar to schedule releases in a way that minimises\nimpact on business operations. This calendar helps in coordinating with various stakeholders\nand ensuring that releases do not conflict with other organisational activities.",
    "srd.20260211163827.1": "\nRelease Design: Designing the release package, which includes detailed specifications of the\nnew or changed features and configurations. This design should align with business\nrequirements and technical constraints.\n\nBuild and Configure: Building and configuring the release components, which may involve\ndeveloping new software, configuring hardware, and integrating with existing systems.",
    "srd.20260211163903.1": "\nQuality Assurance (QA): Conducting rigorous testing to ensure that the release components\nmeet quality standards. This includes unit testing, integration testing, system testing and user\nacceptance testing (UAT).\n\nUser Training and Documentation: Preparing training materials and documentation to ensure\nthat end users and support staff can effectively use and manage the new release.",
    "srd.20260211164103.1": "\nDeployment Management involves the movement of release components from a development\nor test environment to a live production environment.\n\nThe goal is to deploy releases smoothly and efficiently while minimising disruption to existing\nservices. Deployment management includes:",
    "srd.20260211164337.1": "\nDeployment Strategy: Defining the deployment strategy, which can include phased rollouts,\nbig bang deployments, or blue green deployments, depending on the complexity and criticality\nof the release.\n\nRisk Assessment: Assessing risks associated with the deployment and planning mitigation\nstrategies to address potential issues. This includes identifying critical dependencies and\npotential points of failure.",
    "srd.20260211164451.1": "\nDeployment Execution: Carrying out the deployment according to the planned strategy. This\ninvolves coordinating with various teams, such as development, operations, and support, to\nensure a seamless deployment process.\n\nReal Time Monitoring: Monitoring the deployment process in real time to identify and address\nany issues immediately. This includes monitoring system performance, application functionality\nand user feedback.",
    "srd.20260211164532.1": "\nPost Deployment Testing: Conducting validation and verification tests to ensure that the\nrelease has been deployed correctly and is functioning as expected in the live environment.\n\nUser Feedback: Collecting feedback from end users to identify any issues or areas for\nimprovement. This feedback is crucial for continuous improvement and ensuring that the\nrelease meets user expectations.",
    "srd.20260211164742.1": "\nConfiguration management is a crucial process in implementing operational controls and\nstandards within an IT environment.\n\nIt involves systematically managing and maintaining the configuration of hardware, software,\nand other IT assets to ensure their integrity, security, and performance.\n\nThis process helps organisations maintain a consistent and accurate record of configurations,\nwhich is essential for effective governance, compliance, and operational efficiency.\n\nConfiguration management also facilitates quick identification and resolution of issues,\nenhances change management processes, and supports disaster recovery by providing a\nreliable baseline for system restorations.\n\nThrough automation tools and best practices, configuration management streamlines the\ndeployment, maintenance, and monitoring of IT assets, ensuring that all components function\nas intended and contribute to a stable and secure IT infrastructure.\n",
    "srd.20260211164902.1": "\nService Level Management (SLM) is a critical IT Service Management (ITSM) process that\nfocuses on negotiating, defining, and managing the performance and quality of IT services\ndelivered to customers.\n",
    "srd.20260211165137.1": "\nDefinition and Negotiation: SLAs are formal agreements between the service provider and the\ncustomer that define the expected level of service. These agreements specify metrics such as\nuptime, response time, and resolution time.\n\nDocumentation: SLAs must be clearly documented and communicated to all stakeholders. This\ndocumentation serves as a reference point for measuring service performance and handling\nany disputes that may arise.",
    "srd.20260211170210.1": "\nRequirements Gathering: SLRs are the customer specific needs and expectations regarding the\nservice. Gathering SLRs involves understanding the customer business processes, critical\napplications, and operational needs.\n\nTranslation to SLAs: Once SLRs are gathered, they are translated into measurable service level\ntargets that can be included in the SLAs.",
    "srd.20260211170312.1": "\nPerformance Monitoring: Continuous monitoring of service performance against the agreed\nupon SLA metrics is essential. This involves tracking key performance indicators (KPIs) and\nusing monitoring tools to collect data.\n\nReporting: Regular reports are generated to show how well the services are performing\nrelative to the SLAs. These reports highlight areas of success and areas needing improvement.\nThey are shared with customers and internal stakeholders to maintain transparency.",
    "srd.20260211170505.1": "\nAvailability Management is a crucial process within IT Service Management (ITSM) that ensures\nIT services and infrastructure components are available to meet business requirements\nconsistently and reliably.\n\nThe primary objective of availability management is to optimise the capability of the IT\ninfrastructure, services, and support organisation to deliver a cost effective and sustained level\nof availability that enables the business to meet its objectives.\n\nThis involves not only minimising downtime and disruptions but also ensuring that\nperformance levels meet or exceed agreed upon service level agreements (SLAs).\n\nBy proactively identifying potential issues and implementing preventive measures, availability\nmanagement helps in maintaining service continuity.\n\nAdditionally, it supports the alignment of IT resources with business priorities, ensuring that\ncritical services are prioritised and adequately supported.",
    "srd.20260211170558.1": "\nEnhanced Service Reliability: By proactively managing and optimising availability,\norganisations can ensure more reliable IT services, reducing the frequency and impact of\noutages.\n\nImproved Customer Satisfaction: High availability of services leads to greater customer\nsatisfaction and trust, as users can rely on IT services to be available when needed.\n\nOperational Efficiency: Effective availability management reduces the time and resources\nspent on handling outages and their aftermath, allowing the IT team to focus on other strategic\ninitiatives.\n\nBusiness Continuity: Ensuring that critical services are always available supports business\ncontinuity, enabling the organisation to maintain operations even in the face of disruptions.\n\nCost Effectiveness: Preventive maintenance and proactive measures can reduce the cost\nassociated with emergency repairs, downtime, and lost productivity.\n\n",
    "srd.20260211170702.1": "\nCapacity Management is a fundamental process within IT Service Management (ITSM) that\nfocuses on ensuring that the IT infrastructure and services have the right amount of resources\nto meet current and future business demands.\n\nThe primary purpose of capacity management is to optimise the use of IT resources, balancing\ncost and performance while ensuring that the capacity of IT services aligns with business\nrequirements.\n\nThis process involves monitoring, analysing, and forecasting the demand for IT resources, and\nplanning accordingly to ensure that adequate capacity is available at all times.\n\nCapacity management encompasses three main sub processes business capacity management,\nservice capacity management, and component capacity management.\n\nBy accurately predicting future needs based on historical data and business growth projections,\ncapacity management helps prevent resource shortages and avoids over provisioning, thus\noptimising costs and improving service reliability.",
    "srd.20260211170811.1": "\nImproved Service Performance: By ensuring that IT resources are adequately provisioned,\ncapacity management helps maintain optimal performance levels for services, preventing\nslowdowns and disruptions caused by resource bottlenecks.\n\nCost Optimisation : Capacity management helps in avoiding both under utilisation and over\nprovisioning of resources. By accurately matching capacity to demand, organisations can\noptimise their IT spending and reduce unnecessary costs.\n\nEnhanced Business Agility: Through proactive planning and forecasting, capacity management\nenables organisations to quickly adapt to changing business needs and market conditions,\nsupporting business growth and innovation.\n\nIncreased Customer Satisfaction: Ensuring that IT services consistently meet performance and\navailability expectations leads to higher customer satisfaction and trust, as users experience\nfewer service interruptions and better service quality.",
    "srd.20260210180931.1": "\nForensic data collection methodologies are critical for ensuring that digital evidence is\naccurately and securely gathered, preserved, and analysed\n\nThis process involves a series of steps designed to maintain the integrity of the data while\nmaking it available for investigation.\n\nThe following are the detailed look at various methodologies:",
    "srd.20260211172617.1": "Evidence management is a crucial component of digital forensics, ensuring that digital evidence\nis accurately collected, preserved, and presented in a manner that maintains its integrity and\nadmissibility in legal contexts.\n\nThis process encompasses various practices and methodologies designed to handle digital\nevidence systematically and securely.",
    "srd.20260211172830.1": "1 - Chain of Custody\n\n2 - Evidence Preservation\n\n3 - Secure Staorage \n\n4 - Documetation\n\n5 - LegalCompliance \n\n6- Audit & Review\n",
    "srd.20260211172909.1": "1. Collection: Gather digital evidence using appropriate tools and techniques to avoid data\nalteration. Ensure collection is documented thoroughly.\n\n2. Transport: Securely transport evidence to avoid tampering or damage. Document the\ntransportation process meticulously.\n\n3. Analysis: Conduct forensic analysis on copies of the evidence to preserve the original. Use\nvalidated tools and techniques to ensure reliable results.\n\n4. Storage: Store evidence in a secure, controlled environment. Implement redundant storage\nsolutions and regularly verify the integrity of stored data.\n\n5. Presentation: Prepare evidence for presentation in legal proceedings. Ensure that all\ndocumentation, including the chain of custody and analysis reports, is complete and accurate.",
    "srd.20260211173332.1": "The processes of collecting, acquiring, and preserving digital evidence are critical components\nof digital forensics. These steps ensure that evidence remains intact and unaltered,\nmaintaining its integrity and admissibility in legal proceedings.\n",
    "srd.20260211173444.1": "Collection refers to the initial gathering of digital evidence from various sources. This step\ninvolves identifying, locating, and securing potential evidence while minimising the risk of data\nalteration.\n\n1 - Identification\n2 - Documentation\n3 - Securing the Scene\n4 - Handeling Procedures\n",
    "srd.20260211172530.1": "Acquisition involves creating an exact, bit by bit copy of the original data for analysis. This\nprocess is crucial for ensuring that the original evidence is preserved intact.",
    "srd.20260211172037.1": "\nLive data acquisition involves capturing data from a system that is still running. This method is\nessential when there is a need to collect volatile data that would be lost if the system were\npowered down.\n\nExamples include RAM contents, active network connections, running processes, and system\nlogs.",
    "srd.20260211172220.1": "\nStatic data acquisition involves collecting data from a powered off system. This method is used\nwhen there is no need to capture volatile data. It focuses on non volatile storage such as hard\ndrives, SSDs, and other storage media.",
    "srd.20260211172235.1": "\nNetwork forensics involves capturing and analysing network traffic to gather evidence of a\nsecurity incident. This includes monitoring network traffic in real time or analysing stored\ntraffic data.",
    "srd.20260211172417.1": "Mobile device forensics focuses on collecting data from\nsmartphones, tablets, and other mobile devices. This includes\ntext messages, call logs, app data, and geolocation information.\n",
    "srd.20260211172243.1": "\nCloud forensics is increasingly important as more data and services migrate to cloud\nenvironments. This methodology involves collecting and analysing data stored in cloud services\nand platforms.",
    "srd.20260211172427.1": "Memory forensics involves analysing the volatile memory\n(RAM) of a computer to find artifacts related to running\nprocesses, open network connections, loaded drivers, and\nother live system data.",
    "srd.20260211174031.1": "Preservation involves protecting the integrity of the digital evidence to ensure it remains\nunchanged from the moment of collection through to its analysis and presentation in court.\n\n1 - Imaging\n2 - Hashing\n3 - Write-Blockers\n4 - Storage\n5 - Access Conrtol\n6 - documentation",
    "srd.20260211174512.1": "\nManaging communication with vendors is a crucial aspect of maintaining effective and efficient\noperations within an organisation.\n\nThis process involves establishing clear and consistent communication channels to ensure that\nall relevant parties, including internal stakeholders and external vendors, are informed, aligned,\nand engaged. The following are some of the key components of vendors:\n\n1. Establishing Communication Protocols:\n\nDefine clear guidelines and protocols for communication with vendors, including preferred\nchannels (e.g., email, phone, collaboration tools), frequency of updates, and escalation\nprocedures for issues.\n\n2. Regular Updates and Meetings:\n\nSchedule regular meetings and updates with vendors to discuss ongoing projects,\nperformance metrics, and any issues or concerns.\n\n3. Performance Monitoring and Reporting:\n\nMonitor vendor performance against agreed upon service levels and deliverables. Regularly\nreport on these metrics to ensure vendors meet their commitments and address any\ndeviations promptly.\n\n4. Issue Resolution and Escalation:\n\nImplement a structured process for resolving issues and disputes with vendors. This includes\nclear escalation paths and timelines for addressing and resolving problems.\n\n5. Documentation and Record Keeping:\n\nMaintain detailed records of all communications, agreements, and performance evaluations\nwith vendors. This ensures accountability and provides a reference for future interactions\nand decisions.",
    "srd.20260211174541.1": "\nEffective communication with customers is vital for building trust, ensuring satisfaction and\nfostering long term relationships.\n\nManaging communication with customers involves several key components to keep them\ninformed, engaged and satisfied:\n\n1. Clear Communication Channels: Establish and maintain clear communication channels such as\nemail, phone, chat, and social media to ensure customers can easily reach support and receive\ntimely responses.\n\n2. Regular Updates and Notifications: Provide regular updates on service status, product\nchanges, and other relevant information. This includes proactive notifications about planned\nmaintenance, new features, or potential issues that may affect them.\n\n3. Feedback Collection: Actively seek customer feedback through surveys, reviews, and direct\ninteractions. Use this feedback to improve products, services, and customer support\nprocesses.",
    "srd.20260211174552.1": "\nManaging communication with partners is essential for fostering strong, collaborative\nrelationships and ensuring that all relevant parties are aligned and informed. The following are\nsome of the key components of this process include:\n\n1. Establishing Communication Protocols: Define clear communication guidelines with partners,\nincluding preferred methods, frequency of updates, and procedures for addressing issues or\nchanges.\n\n2. Regular Updates and Meetings: Schedule regular meetings and updates to discuss progress,\nshare information, and address any concerns.\n\n3. Performance Monitoring and Reporting: Track the performance of partnerships against\nagreed objectives and deliverables. Provide regular reports to ensure both parties are meeting\ntheir commitments and to identify areas for improvement.\n\n4. Issue Resolution and Escalation: Implement a structured process for resolving issues with\npartners, including clear escalation paths and timelines for addressing problems.\n\u00a9 The Knowledge Academy Ltd",
    "srd.20260211174606.1": "\nManaging communication with regulators is a critical aspect of ensuring compliance and\nmaintaining a good standing with relevant authorities.\n\nEffective communication with regulators helps organisations navigate complex regulatory\nlandscapes, mitigate risks, and demonstrate their commitment to legal and ethical standards.\nKey components of this process include:\n\n1. Establishing Communication Protocols:\n\nDefine and document protocols for interacting with regulators, including designated points\nof contact, preferred communication channels (e.g., formal letters, emails, secure portals),\nand timelines for responding to regulatory requests.\n\n2. Regular Reporting and Updates:\n\nEnsure timely and accurate submission of required reports and updates to regulators. This\nincludes periodic compliance reports, incident notifications, and updates on corrective\nactions taken in response to regulatory findings.\n\n3. Regulatory Compliance Monitoring:\n\nContinuously monitor and review compliance with relevant laws, regulations, and industry\nstandards. Maintain up to date records and documentation to support regulatory audits\nand inspections.\n\n4. Issue Resolution and Escalation:\n\nImplement a structured process for addressing regulatory concerns and findings. This\ninvolves timely investigation and resolution of issues, clear documentation of actions taken,\nand effective communication with regulators throughout the process.\n\n5. Documentation and Record Keeping:\n\nMaintain comprehensive and organised records of all communications with regulators,\nincluding submissions, correspondence, meeting notes, and audit findings.",
    "srd.20260211174637.1": "\nManaging communication with other stakeholders, such as employees, customers, investors,\nand the community, is vital for ensuring the smooth operation and success of an organisation.\nThe following are some of the k ey components include in this process are as:\n\n1. Defining Communication Strategies: Develop tailored communication strategies for different\nstakeholder groups, taking into account their specific needs, interests, and preferred\ncommunication channels (e.g., newsletters, social media, meetings, reports).\n\n2. Regular Updates and Engagement: Provide regular updates and engage with stakeholders\nthrough scheduled communications such as town hall meetings, quarterly reports, or\nnewsletters.\n\n3.Feedback Mechanisms: Establish mechanisms for stakeholders to provide feedback, ask\nquestions, and express concerns.\n\n4. Transparency and Accountability: Ensure transparency in communication by providing\naccurate and timely information. Address stakeholder concerns openly and honestly to build\ntrust and demonstrate accountability.",
    "srd.20260210181304.1": "A Security Operations Center (SOC) is a centralised unit that deals with security issues on an\norganisational and technical level.\n\nThe following are the steps to illustrate security operations center:",
    "srd.20260211191240.1": "A Security Operations Center (SOC) is a centralised unit that deals with security issues on an\norganisational and technical level. The following are the steps to illustrate security operations\ncenter:",
    "srd.20260211191354.1": "\nSkilled security analysts, incident responders, and SOC managers who operate and manage\nthe SOC functions. Their expertise and continuous training are vital for effective security\noperations.",
    "srd.20260211191440.1": "\nWell defined procedures and playbooks for monitoring, incident response, threat hunting,\nand communication. These processes ensure consistency, efficiency, and effectiveness in\nmanaging security operations.",
    "srd.20260211191537.1": "Advanced security tools and technologies like Security Information and Event Management\n(SIEM) systems, intrusion detection/prevention systems (IDS/IPS), endpoint detection and\nresponse (EDR), and threat intelligence platforms.",
    "srd.20260211191307.1": "1. In House SOC:\n\nThis model offers full control over security operations but requires significant investment in\nresources and expertise.\n\n2. Managed SOC:\n\nOutsourced to a third party provider who manages the security operations on behalf of the\norganisation. This can be cost effective and provides access to specialised expertise and\ntechnology.\n\n3. Hybrid SOC:\n\nCombines elements of both in house and managed SOCs, where some functions are\nmanaged internally, and others are outsourced. This approach offers flexibility and can be\ntailored to meet specific organisational needs.",
    "srd.20260211192217.1": "1. Enhanced Security Posture:\n\nContinuous monitoring and proactive threat management significantly improve an\norganisation\u2019s security posture.\n\n2. Quick Incident Response:\n\nThe ability to detect and respond to incidents quickly minimises the impact and reduces\nrecovery time.",
    "srd.20260211192423.1": "\nIntelligent monitoring of security controls involves the use of advanced technologies and\nmethodologies to continuously oversee and analyse security mechanisms.\n\nThis proactive approach aims to detect, prevent, and respond to security incidents efficiently.\nBy leveraging automation, machine learning, and artificial intelligence, organisations can\nenhance their ability to monitor and manage security controls dynamically.\n\nKey Components\n\n1 - Automation\n2 - Machine Learning\n3 - Artificial Intelligence",
    "srd.20260211192644.1": "It is a critical component in managing security operations within cloud environments.\n\nThis process involves several key elements:\n\n01 -  Collection\n02 - Storage\n03 - Analysis\n04 - Real time Monitoring\n05 - Compliance and Auditing\n06 - Integration with Incident Response",
    "srd.20260211193126.1": "Vulnerability Assessments are a vital element of managing security operations, particularly in\ncloud environments where resources and services are dynamically scaled and updated.\n\nThe following are the vulnerability assessments are structured and implemented:\n\n1. Identification of Assets\n2. Scanning for Vulnerabilities\n3. Risk Assessment\n4. Remediation\n5. Verification and Reporting\n6. Continuous Assessment\n",
    "srd.20260121194856.1": "Domian 6: Legal, Risk and Complaince\n13%",
    "srd.20260212153547.1": "\nConflicting international legislation refers to the challenges and complexities that arise when\nlaws and regulations related to data protection, privacy, and cybersecurity vary significantly\nacross different countries or regions.\n\nThis conflict can pose significant challenges for organisations, particularly those operating\nglobally. Below is a closer look at the issue:\n\n\nConflicting international legislation presents an ongoing challenge in the global digital economy.\n \nOrganisations must stay informed and agile to navigate this complex legal environment effectively.",
    "srd.20260212153751.1": "\nMany countries have their own sets of data protection and privacy laws, such as the General\nData Protection Regulation (GDPR) in the European Union, the California Consumer Privacy\nAct (CCPA) in the United States, and others worldwide.\n\nThese laws often have different requirements regarding data consent, processing, storage,\nand transfer, creating a complex legal landscape for international organisations.",
    "srd.20260212153840.1": "When data crosses international borders, it becomes subject to the laws of multiple\njurisdictions, which can sometimes be contradictory or incompatible.\n\nOrganisations must navigate these varying legal requirements, which can be particularly\nchallenging when laws in one country demand actions that are illegal in another.",
    "srd.20260212153915.1": "\nEnsuring compliance with conflicting international legislation often requires substantial\nresources and can lead to increased operational complexities.\n\nOrganisations may need to implement different policies and procedures in different regions,\nimpacting everything from IT infrastructure to employee training.",
    "srd.20260212153953.1": "Non compliance with relevant laws can result in significant legal risks, including fines,\npenalties, and reputational damage.\n\nThe risk is compounded when conflicting laws make it difficult to comply universally, forcing\norganisations to prioritise compliance in one region over another.",
    "srd.20260212154039.1": "Some countries have data sovereignty laws requiring certain types of data to be stored and\nprocessed within the country's borders, complicating global data management strategies.\n\nData localisation requirements can conflict with the global nature of cloud computing and\nother international data services.",
    "srd.20260212154225.1": "\nOrganisations must develop strategic plans for data\ngovernance that consider these conflicting international\nlaws.\n\nThis often involves legal expertise, data mapping,\nunderstanding data flows, and implementing flexible\nand adaptable data management systems.",
    "srd.20260212154320.1": "Evaluating legal risks specific to cloud computing involves understanding the unique challenges\nand considerations that arise when using cloud services.\n\nThese risks stem from the nature of cloud computing, where data is stored, processed, and\nmanaged remotely on servers operated by third party service providers. Key legal risks include:",
    "srd.20260212154423.1": "\nDifferent countries have varying data protection laws, and compliance with these laws\nbecomes complex when data is stored in multiple jurisdictions via the cloud.\n\nOrganisations must ensure that their cloud providers comply with relevant privacy\nregulations like GDPR, HIPAA, or CCPA, depending on the nature of the data and where it is\nprocessed and stored.",
    "srd.20260212154533.1": "\nCloud computing introduces concerns about data security, including the risk of data breaches.\n\nLegal risks arise regarding who is responsible for a data breach the cloud service provider (CSP)\nor the client. This responsibility is often defined in the service level agreements (SLAs).",
    "srd.20260212154609.1": "\nDependence on cloud services for critical operations raises risks related to service outages and\nthe provider\u2019s ability to ensure continuous availability.\n\nLegal risks involve the CSP's liability for any loss of business or damage resulting from downtime\nor data loss.",
    "srd.20260212154701.1": "\nData stored in the cloud can be physically located in different countries, subjecting it to the laws\nof those jurisdictions.\n\nLegal risks include government access to data, cross border data transfer restrictions, and\nvarying legal standards for data protection.",
    "srd.20260212154728.1": "\nCloud environments can complicate the enforcement and protection of intellectual property\nrights, especially when data and applications are spread across multiple jurisdictions.\n\nLegal considerations include ensuring that using cloud services does not infringe on third party IP\nrights and protecting one\u2019s own IP in the cloud.",
    "srd.20260212154824.1": "The legal framework and guidelines in the Factor of information technology, particularly in areas\nlike data protection, privacy, and cyber law, are composed of a set of laws, regulations, standards,\nand ethical guidelines that govern the handling, processing, and storage of data.\n\nThese frameworks are designed to protect individuals' privacy, secure sensitive data, and ensure\nfair and lawful business practices. Here\u2019s an overview:",
    "srd.20260212154937.1": "\nRegulations such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) in the EU, the California\nConsumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and others globally, set standards for data privacy and user\nconsent.\n\nThese laws dictate how personal data should be collected, processed, stored, and shared, and\nthey grant individuals rights over their data.",
    "srd.20260212155020.1": "Laws like the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) in the U.S., the Network and Information\nSystems (NIS) Directive in the EU, and others, aim to protect against unauthorised access, cyber\nattacks, and data breaches.\n\nThey include provisions for reporting breaches, securing networks and information systems,\nand penalising cyber crimes.",
    "srd.20260212155100.1": "\nCertain industries are subject to additional regulations, such as the Health Insurance Portability\nand Accountability Act (HIPAA) for healthcare in the U.S., the Payment Card Industry Data\nSecurity Standard (PCI DSS) for the payment card industry, and others.\n\nThese regulations impose strict data handling and security requirements in their respective\nfields.",
    "srd.20260212155140.1": "\nLaws governing intellectual property (IP) protect the rights of creators and innovators. This\nincludes copyright, patents, trademarks, and trade secrets.\n\nIn the digital realm, these laws cover software, digital media, and online content.",
    "srd.20260212155215.1": "\nThese laws govern electronic commerce, digital signatures, and online transactions, ensuring\nlegal recognition and enforceability.\n\nExamples include the Electronic Signatures in Global and National Commerce Act (E Sign Act) in\nthe U.S. and the Electronic Commerce Directive in the EU.",
    "srd.20260212155259.1": "\neDiscovery, or electronic discovery, refers to the process of identifying, collecting, preserving, and\nreviewing electronic information that can be used as evidence in legal cases.\n\nThis process is particularly relevant in the Factor of cybersecurity and cloud computing, which the\nCertified Cloud Security Professional (CCSP) credential addresses.\n\nAccording to the CCSP framework, eDiscovery in cloud environments involves several key\nconsiderations:\n",
    "srd.20260212155359.1": "\nIdentifying relevant data across diverse cloud environments and platforms.\n\nOvercoming challenges due to the distributed nature of cloud storage and the potential for data\nto reside in multiple jurisdictions.",
    "srd.20260212155436.1": "\nEnsuring that identified data is preserved in a manner that maintains its integrity, preventing\nalteration or deletion.\n\nImplementing legal holds to protect relevant data from routine deletion or modification\nprocesses.",
    "srd.20260212155518.1": "Processing collected data to make it suitable for review and analysis.\n\nUtilising specialised tools to handle large volumes of data common in cloud environments.",
    "srd.20260212155552.1": "\nAddressing legal and compliance issues related to data sovereignty, as data stored in the cloud\nmay be subject to the laws of the country where the data center is located.\n\nNavigating different jurisdictions\u2019 laws on data privacy and cross border data transfers during the\neDiscovery process.",
    "srd.20260212155633.1": "\nMaintaining the confidentiality and security of data during the eDiscovery process, especially\nwhen handling sensitive or personally identifiable information (PII).\n\nImplementing adequate access controls and encryption to protect data integrity and\nconfidentiality.",
    "srd.20260212155801.1": "\nForensic requirements particularly for cybersecurity and cloud computing environments,\ninvolve a set of standards, practices, and tools designed to investigate and analyse digital\nevidence following a security incident or breach.\n\nThese requirements are essential for uncovering the cause and extent of an incident,\nidentifying perpetrators, and taking appropriate remedial actions. Key forensic requirements\ninclude:",
    "srd.20260212155850.1": "Maintaining a documented and unbroken chain of custody for digital\nevidence. This ensures that any evidence collected is traceable, and its integrity is preserved\nthroughout the investigation process.\n",
    "srd.20260212155957.1": "Implementing standardised procedures for collecting and\npreserving digital evidence. This includes capturing volatile data (like RAM contents), creating\ndigital copies (forensic imaging), and securely storing physical devices.",
    "srd.20260212160044.1": "Detailed documentation of the incident timeline, actions taken, and\nfindings. This includes logs, system statuses, network traffic records, and any other relevant\ninformation.\n",
    "srd.20260212160118.1": "Ensuring compliance with relevant laws and regulations during the forensic\ninvestigation. This involves understanding legal boundaries, such as privacy laws and\nauthorisation requirements.",
    "srd.20260212160144.1": "Employing appropriate forensic tools and techniques\nfor data analysis. Tools should be validated and recognised in the forensic community to\nensure the reliability of findings.\n",
    "srd.20260212160219.1": "Analysing evidence in a secure, controlled environment to\nprevent contamination or tampering. This often involves using isolated networks and\ndedicated forensic workstations.\n",
    "srd.20260212161246.1": "Definition - Data protected as per agreements or contracts between parties.\n\nExamples - Business contracts, Non disclosure agreements (NDAs), Customer agreements\n\nProtection Motivation - Driven by mutual agreement and the interests of the contracting parties.\n\nScope  -  Defined by the terms of the contract and can vary significantly.\n\nPenalties for Breach - Consequences as outlined in the contract, potentially including damages or termination.\n\nControl - Determined by the parties involved in the contract.\n\nFlexability - Can be negotiated and tailored to specific needs of the parties.\n\nFocus - On fulfilling contractual obligations and maintaining business relationships.\n\nEnforcement - Managed and enforced by the parties in the contract.",
    "srd.20260212161258.1": "Definition - Data protected under specific regulatory frameworks or laws.\n\nExamples - Health records (protected by HIPAA), Consumer data (protected by GDPR)\n\nProtection Motivation - Driven by legal compliance and the need to adhere to specific regulations.\n\nScope  -  Defined by specific regulations and generally consistent across entities.\n\nPenalties for Breach - Legal penalties, including fines and sanctions, as dictated by law.\n\nControl - Set by regulatory bodies and applicable across all relevant entities.\n\nFlexability - Generally rigid, with limited room for negotiation.\n\nFocus - On ensuring compliance with legal and regulatory standards.\n\nEnforcement - Overseen and enforced by governmental or regulatory authorities.",
    "srd.20260212162438.1": "Country specific legislation related to private data often varies significantly, reflecting the different\ncultural, legal, and political environments in each country.",
    "srd.20260212162507.1": "\nA comprehensive data protection law.\n\nFocuses on data consent, rights to access, and the right to be forgotten.\n\nApplicable to entities handling data of residents within the region, regardless of the entity's\nlocation.",
    "srd.20260212162551.1": "\nGrants residents rights over their personal information.\n\nIncludes rights to know what personal data is collected and to request data deletion.",
    "srd.20260212162633.1": "\nRegulates the use and disclosure of health related information by healthcare entities.\n\nSets standards for safeguarding medical information.",
    "srd.20260212162723.1": "\nGoverns how private sector organisations collect, use, and disclose personal information in\ncommercial activities.\n\nEstablishes principles for the protection of personal information.",
    "srd.20260212162751.1": "\nRegulates the handling of personal information by government agencies and some private\nsectors. Outlines principles for data collection, use, and disclosure..",
    "srd.20260212162835.1": "\nJurisdictional differences in data privacy laws are substantial and can significantly impact how\norganisations handle personal data.\n\nSome regions, like the European Union with its General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), have\nrobust and comprehensive data protection frameworks, emphasising individual rights, strict\nconsent requirements, and severe penalties for non compliance.\n\nThese differences extend to cross border data transfers, enforcement mechanisms, government\naccess to data, and notification requirements for data breaches.\n\nAdditionally, data localisation requirements, which mandate that data about a region's citizens\nmust be stored within that region, can further complicate data management for global\norganisations.\n\nUnderstanding and navigating these jurisdictional variations is essential for organisations\noperating internationally to ensure compliance and data protection.",
    "srd.20260212185804.1": "When organisations embark on the journey of transitioning their services to the cloud, there is avital need for ongoing assurances to ensure that the necessary controls are in place.\n\nThese assurances are crucial for maintaining data security and compliance. One of the mechanisms\nfor providing these assurances is through internal audits, which serve as a third line of defense in\nthe following ways:",
    "srd.20260212185857.1": "Internal audits independently verify the effectiveness of the cloud program. This verification\ninvolves assessing whether the controls and security measures put in place for cloud services are functioning as intended.\n",
    "srd.20260212185913.1": "Internal audits provide a level of assurance to the organisation's board and risk management\nfunctions regarding the risks associated with cloud adoption.\n\nAuditors assess and report on the maturity and effectiveness of cloud risk management\npractices, offering insights into the organisation's risk exposure.",
    "srd.20260212185943.1": "In addition to internal audits, external auditors may also perform audits related to the\norganisation's use of cloud services.\n\nHowever, the focus of external audits is often on internal controls over financial reporting.",
    "srd.20260212190043.1": "The scope of internal audits is typically broader than that of external audits. Internal audits\nencompass various aspects of the cloud environment, including IT and business functions that\nsupport the organisation's overall operations, not just financial reporting.\n",
    "srd.20260212190118.1": "The adoption of cloud services has a significant impact on audit requirements for both Cloud\nService Providers (CSPs) and their customers.\n\nBelow are some of the key considerations regarding the impact of cloud services on audit\nrequirements:",
    "srd.20260212190743.1": "Auditors, whether internal or external, need to reevaluate\ntheir audit approaches when dealing with cloud services. Traditional audit methods may not\nfully apply to the cloud environment, given its unique characteristics and distributed nature.\n",
    "srd.20260212191102.1": "Cloud services introduce new challenges for auditors. Data may be\nstored in various geographic locations and across multiple servers and data centers.",
    "srd.20260212191415.1": "In cloud computing, there is often a shared responsibility model\nbetween the CSP and the customer. Auditors need to understand this model and assess which\nsecurity and compliance aspects are the responsibility of each party.\n\n\n",
    "srd.20260212192136.1": "Auditors may need to assess data access and transparency\nwithin the cloud environment. This involves understanding who has access to data, how data is\nencrypted and protected, and whether audit logs are available for review.\n",
    "srd.20260212192218.1": "Both CSPs and customers must ensure that their cloud services\ncomply with relevant standards and regulations. Auditors play a crucial role in verifying this\ncompliance.",
    "srd.20260212192436.1": "Cloud services enable continuous monitoring of security and\ncompliance. Auditors should leverage tools and technologies that provide real time insights\ninto the cloud environment, allowing for proactive identification of issues.\n",
    "srd.20260212192509.1": "Auditors must consider data sovereignty and jurisdiction\nissues in a cloud Factor. The location of data storage and processing can have legal and\ncompliance implications.\n",
    "srd.20260212192728.1": "Assurance challenges related to virtualisation and cloud environments are crucial\nconsiderations for Certified Cloud Security Professionals (CCSPs) and auditors. Below are some\nof the key assurance challenges and the actions CCSPs must take to address them:",
    "srd.20260212192845.1": "\nVirtualisation introduces a layer of complexity in the IT infrastructure.\n\nCCSPs need to thoroughly understand the virtualisation management architecture, including\nhow virtual machines (VMs) are provisioned, managed, and secured.",
    "srd.20260212192944.1": "\nEnsuring that virtualised systems, including both VMs and hypervisors, are up to date with\nthe latest security patches and updates is a significant challenge.\n\nCCSPs must verify that systems are regularly patched to address vulnerabilities. They should\nalso assess whether a patch management process is in place and effective.",
    "srd.20260212193417.1": "\nHardening involves configuring VMs and hypervisors to minimise security risks. CCSPs\nshould verify that hardening guidelines and best practices are followed.\n\nThis includes assessing whether unnecessary services are disabled, access controls are\nproperly configured, and default settings are changed to enhance security.",
    "srd.20260212193501.1": "\nConfiguration management is critical for maintaining the security and compliance of\nvirtualised environments.\n\n\nCCSPs must verify that the configuration of hypervisors\naligns with organisational policies and security standards.\n\nThey should also assess whether changes to\nconfigurations are tracked and audited.",
    "srd.20260212193551.1": "\nVM images serve as templates for VM deployment.\nEnsuring the security of these images is challenging, as\nany vulnerabilities present in the image can propagate to\ndeployed VMs.\n\nCCSPs should assess image security, including how images\nare created, stored, and updated.\n",
    "srd.20260212193741.1": "Audit reports are essential tools used by Certified Cloud Security Professionals to validate the\neffectiveness of security controls and compliance within cloud environments.\n\n",
    "srd.20260212194454.1": "Primarily concerned with controls over financial reporting, this report is crucial for service\nproviders that affect clients' financial statements. There are two types of SOC 1 reports:\n\nType I: Evaluates the design of controls at a specific point in time.\n\nType II: Reviews both the design and operating effectiveness of controls over a specified\nperiod.",
    "srd.20260212194507.1": "Focused on non financial reporting controls relating to security, availability, processing\nintegrity, confidentiality, and privacy.\n\nThese aspects are vital for maintaining the security and reliability of services provided by cloud service providers (CSPs).\n\nType I and Type II reports, similar to SOC 1.\n\nTrust Service Criteria: These criteria provide a framework for the controls covered in the\nreport.",
    "srd.20260212194522.1": "It provides a high level overview without the detailed controls and tests found in SOC 2,\nmaking it useful for public dissemination.",
    "srd.20260212194558.1": "Demonstrates compliance with international standards for an information security\nmanagement system (ISMS).\n\nThis certification process involves a rigorous audit to confirm the implementation and\neffectiveness of the service provider's security practices.",
    "srd.20260212194908.1": "Scope statements define the boundaries of an audit, ensuring that it remains focused and\nrelevant while minimising disruption to normal business operations. The following are some of\nthe important aspects are as:\n\nOperational Impact Limitation: -\n\nEnsures that audit activities do not adversely affect day to\nday operations.\n\nProtection of Critical Components: -\n\nHigh priority components are often shielded from\nexhaustive audit activities to prevent any potential disruption.\n\nAsset Restrictions: Certain critical or sensitive assets may be explicitly excluded from the\naudit scope to protect them.\n\nAccepted Times and Time Periods: Audits are scheduled during times that are least\ndisruptive to business operations.",
    "srd.20260212195047.1": "Gap analysis in the context of cloud security is a methodical process aimed at identifying\nshortfalls or vulnerabilities that might compromise the security or performance of cloud\nservices. The process typically follows these stages:\n",
    "srd.20260212195136.1": "Engaging stakeholders to ensure alignment on the purpose and scope of the\ngap analysis.\n",
    "srd.20260212195228.1": "Information Gathering: Collecting data from various sources such as documentation,\ninterviews, and system analysis.\n\nVerification: Ensuring the accuracy and completeness of the information gathered.\n\nRisk Identification: Analysing the data to identify potential risks and vulnerabilities.",
    "srd.20260212195247.1": "Documentation of Findings: Creating a detailed record of all identified gaps and\npotential risks.\n\nRecommendations: Developing actionable suggestions for addressing these gaps.\n",
    "srd.20260212195413.1": "Audit planning is a critical phase in the audit process, where auditors carefully plan and prepare for an effective and efficient audit. The planning phase sets the foundation for the entire audit engagement. Below are the key aspects and steps involved in audit planning:",
    "srd.20260212195509.1": "The first step in audit planning is to clearly understand the\naudit objectives. Auditors need to know what they are trying to achieve with the audit, whether it is compliance verification, risk assessment, or process improvement.\n",
    "srd.20260212195533.1": "Define the scope of the audit, which includes specifying the areas, processes, or\nsystems that will be audited. The scope should be well defined to ensure that audit efforts are\nfocused and relevant.\n",
    "srd.20260212195553.1": "Establish the audit criteria, which serve as the benchmark against which audit\nfindings will be assessed. Criteria can include industry standards, regulations, internal policies, and best practices.",
    "srd.20260212195720.1": "Conduct a risk assessment to identify\npotential risks and areas of concern within the audit scope.\nThis helps prioritise audit resources and focus on high risk\nareas.\n",
    "srd.20260212195911.1": "Assemble the audit team with individuals possessing the necessary skills and expertise to\nconduct the audit effectively.",
    "srd.20260212195924.1": "Develop a detailed audit plan that outlines the approach, methodologies, and procedures to\nbe used during the audit.\n",
    "srd.20260212195942.1": "Allocate the required resources for the\naudit, including personnel, tools, and technology. Ensure\nthat the audit team has access to all necessary resources.\n",
    "srd.20260212200154.1": "An Information Security Management System (ISMS) is a crucial component for medium to large\nscale entities aiming to reduce risks related to the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) of information and assets. Below are key points regarding ISMS and its alignment with standards like ISO 27001:2024:",
    "srd.20260212202758.1": "An ISMS requires organisations to perform systematic risk assessments to identify threats and vulnerabilities that could impact information assets. \n\nUnder ISO 27001:2024, the organisation must define risk acceptance criteria and implement appropriate risk mitigation strategies to manage or reduce these risks to an acceptable level.",
    "srd.20260212202819.1": "The foundation of an effective ISMS is the establishment of clear\nsecurity policies and objectives that are aligned with the organisation overall business objectives.",
    "srd.20260212202951.1": "ISO 27001:2024 outlines requirements for the organisation of information security, including the assignment of responsibilities. It requires defining roles and responsibilities for information security management and ensuring that these roles are communicated to all stakeholders.",
    "srd.20260212203319.1": "This includes classification of assets, defining and implementing appropriate\naccess controls and maintaining an accurate inventory of assets throughout their lifecycle.\n",
    "srd.20260212203336.1": "Before, during, and after employment, ISO 27001:2024 requires that\norganisations implement policies and procedures to manage and protect against security threats\narising from human resources.",
    "srd.20260212203400.1": "ISO 27001:2024 includes requirements for protecting physical areas (data centers, offices, etc.), managing access to these areas, protecting against environmental hazards, and securing equipment both on and off premises.",
    "srd.20260212203417.1": "This involves managing technical and procedural aspects of information systems and handling. It includes clear procedures for system operations, responsibilities, and data management in accordance with security policies.",
    "srd.20260212203717.1": "\nISO/IEC 27001:2021 is a comprehensive standard that provides a framework for establishing and\nmaintaining an Information Security Management System (ISMS).\n\nIt defines a set of domains that cover various aspects of information security and risk management. \n\nThe following domains are included in ISO/IEC 27001:2021:",
    "srd.20260212203811.1": "This involves understanding the internal and external factors that\naffect the organisation's ability to achieve the intended outcomes of its ISMS. It includes identifying\nstakeholders and their requirements relevant to information security.\n",
    "srd.20260212203821.1": "This emphasises the role of top management in establishing the ISMS, including setting\nthe information security policy, ensuring integration with business processes, and demonstrating\ncommitment to continual improvement.\n",
    "srd.20260212204013.1": "This involves identifying and addressing risks and opportunities related to information\nsecurity. It includes setting measurable objectives and planning actions to achieve them, ensuring\nthat they are consistent with the organisation's strategic direction.",
    "srd.20260212204109.1": "This covers the resources needed for the ISMS, including human resources, awareness,\ncommunication, and documentation requirements. It ensures that the organisation has the\nnecessary support to implement and maintain the ISMS.\n",
    "srd.20260212204122.1": "This involves the processes and procedures for implementing and controlling the ISMS.\nIt includes risk assessment and treatment plans, managing changes, and ensuring that outsourced\nprocesses are controlled.\n",
    "srd.20260212204139.1": "This focuses on monitoring, measurement, analysis, and evaluation of the\nISMS. It includes internal audits, management reviews, and tracking progress against information\nsecurity objectives.\n",
    "srd.20260212204211.1": "This involves taking actions to improve the ISMS based on the results of\nperformance evaluations, audits, and management reviews. It includes addressing nonconformities\nand implementing corrective actions to prevent recurrence.\n\n",
    "n30.20260213092216.1": "They provide the foundational framework for various functional policies, all of which\ncollectively work to mitigate risks and ensure the security of an organisation's data. Below are\nthe key organisational and functional policies commonly used to strengthen data security:\n\n1. Information Security Policy: This policy outlines the organisation's commitment to information\nsecurity and sets the overall direction for security objectives and controls.\n\n2. Information Technology Policy: It defines the standards and guidelines for the use and\nmanagement of IT resources within the organisation.\n\n3. Data Classification Policy: This policy categorises data based on sensitivity and defines how\ndifferent types of data should be handled, accessed, and protected.\n\n4. Acceptable Usage Policy: It specifies the acceptable use of IT resources, including computers,\nnetworks, and internet access, by employees and users.\n\n5. Network Security Policy: This policy lays out the rules and procedures for securing the\norganisation's network infrastructure against unauthorised access and threats.\n\n6. Internet Use Policy: It governs how employees and users can use the internet while adhering\nto security and productivity guidelines.\n\n7. Email Use Policy: This policy addresses the proper use of email systems, including security\npractices and etiquette.\n\n8. Password Policy: It defines password requirements, including complexity, length, and\nfrequency of changes, to enhance access security.\n\n9. Virus and Spam Policy: This policy outlines measures to protect the organisation from\nmalware, viruses, and spam.\n\n10.Software Security Policy: It sets guidelines for the secure development and use of software\napplications to prevent vulnerabilities.\n",
    "n30.20260213092442.1": "\u2022 The identification and involvement of relevant stakeholders in cloud computing discussions are\ncrucial for the success of any cloud adoption initiative.\n\n\u2022 Engaging stakeholders helps in understanding their perspectives, concerns, and requirements, and it\npaves the way for a smoother transition to cloud services.\n\n\u2022 Below are some key points related to stakeholder identification and involvement in cloud computing\ndiscussions:\n\n\n",
    "n30.20260213092603.1": "Identifying the key stakeholders is the first step in the process. These\nstakeholders may include top management, IT personnel, business units, legal and compliance\nteams, and end-users who will be impacted by the cloud adoption.",
    "n30.20260213092641.1": "Once stakeholders are identified, it is important to assess the potential impacts\nof cloud adoption on current services, operations, and delivery models. This assessment helps in\nunderstanding how each stakeholder group will be affected.",
    "n30.20260213092725.1": "o Defining Enterprise Architecture: If the organisation lacks a well-defined enterprise architecture,\ncreating one can be a substantial task. Enterprise architecture provides a blueprint for aligning IT\nwith business objectives and is crucial in cloud planning.\n\no Service and Provider Selection: Objectively choosing the right cloud service and provider is\nchallenging due to the multitude of options available. Stakeholders' input can help in making\ninformed decisions.\n\no Risk Management: Extending risk management and enterprise risk management to account for\ncloud-related risks is essential. Identifying and mitigating potential risks require collaboration with\nvarious stakeholders.\n\no Cost Assessment: Identifying both direct and indirect costs associated with cloud adoption is vital.\nThis includes costs related to training, up-skilling of staff, reallocating resources, and adapting to\nnew tasks and responsibilities.\n\n",
    "n30.20260213092934.1": "When it comes to security, privacy, and compliance in different environments or systems, there are\nseveral layers of requirements to consider. Below is a breakdown of these requirements:\n\n",
    "n30.20260213093116.1": "\n\u2022These are legal and regulatory requirements that are specific to the geographic location where data\nis stored or processed.\n\n\u2022Different countries and regions may have their own data protection laws, and organisations must\ncomply with these laws when operating within those jurisdictions.\n\n\u2022For example, the European Union GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation) imposes strict data\nprivacy requirements on organisations handling EU citisens data.\n",
    "n30.20260213093217.1": "\n\u2022 Many industries and organisations have audit requirements to ensure that security controls are in\nplace and effective.\n\n\u2022 These audits may be conducted internally or by external auditors. Compliance with audit\nrequirements demonstrates adherence to security standards and practices.",
    "n30.20260213093254.1": "\n\u2022 Organisations are often required to report security incidents, breaches, and other relevant\ninformation to regulatory bodies, customers, and affected parties.\n\n\u2022 Effective communication is crucial in managing and mitigating security incidents.",
    "n30.20260213093339.1": "\n\nCertain types of data, regardless of their physical location or hosting model, have specific\ncompliance requirements. Below are some examples:\n\n\n\n",
    "n30.20260213093623.1": "HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act): This regulation applies to\nhealthcare organisations and imposes strict requirements for protecting patients' health\ninformation.\n\n\u2022 The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)\n\no Purpose: HIPAA is designed to protect the privacy and security of electronic protected health\ninformation (ePHI) in the healthcare industry.\n\no Entities Covered: HIPAA applies to covered entities such as healthcare providers, health plans,\nand healthcare clearinghouses, as well as their business associates who handle ePHI.\n\nKey Provisions:\n\n\u27a2 Privacy Rule: Establishes rules and restrictions on the use and disclosure of individuals' health\ninformation.\n\n\u27a2 Security Rule: Requires the implementation of safeguards to protect ePHI, including\nadministrative, physical, and technical measures.\n\n\u27a2 Breach Notification Rule: Mandates the reporting of breaches of ePHI to affected individuals, the\nDepartment of Health and Human Services (HHS), and, in some cases, the media.\n\n\u2022 Compliance Challenges: Covered entities and business associates must ensure that their use of cloud\ncomputing solutions complies with HIPAA rules, especially regarding the protection of ePHI. This may\ninvolve secure data storage, access controls, and breach notification procedures.",
    "n30.20260213093637.1": "\nPCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard): Organisations that handle credit card\ntransactions must comply with PCI DSS to ensure the security of cardholder data.",
    "n30.20260213093924.1": "North American Electric Reliability Corporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) \n\n\u2022 Purpose: NERC CIP is focused on ensuring the security and reliability of the North American electrical\npower grid by establishing standards and controls for critical infrastructure protection.\n\n\u2022 Entities Covered: NERC CIP applies to organisations within the electric power sector, including\nutilities and grid operators.\n\nKey Provisions\n\n\u2022 Standards Suite: NERC CIP provides a suite of standards that address various aspects of security for\nsystems that control power grids and related assets.\n\n\u2022 Asset Identification: Requires the identification of key assets and the development of control\nmechanisms to protect them.\n\n\u2022 Physical and Logical Security: Addresses both physical\nand logical security measures to safeguard critical\nassets.\n\n\u2022 Incident Recovery: Includes provisions for recovery\nplans in case of incidents or disruptions.\n\nCompliance Challenges: \n\nOrganisations subject to NERC CIP\nmust adhere to the specific security standards and controls\noutlined in the regulation to protect the power grid and\nrelated systems from cyberthreats and ensure reliability.",
    "n30.20260213093646.1": "\nNIST/FedRAMP: These are standards and requirements for federal government systems in the\nUnited States. They include security controls and assessment processes to protect government\ndata.rity incidents.",
    "n30.20260213093438.1": "\n\nHighly sensitive data, such as healthcare records, financial\ndata, and government information, often require additional\nlayers of security and compliance due to their critical nature.",
    "n30.20260213093511.1": "    \n\u2022Some industries have their own regulatory bodies and\ncompliance standards.\n\n\u2022For example, the financial industry is subject to regulations\nfrom entities like the SEC (U.S. Securities and Exchange\nCommission) and FINRA (Financial Industry Regulatory\nAuthority).",
    "n30.20260213095616.1": "Adopting distributed IT systems can bring several advantages, but it is crucial to ensure that\nthese models do not negatively impact essential factors such as communication, coordination,\nmanagement, governance, and distributed computing.\n\nBelow is an overview of these considerations:",
    "n30.20260213095812.1": "Drivers for Adopting Distributed IT Systems\n\n\u2022 Increasing Enterprise Productivity: Distributed IT systems can enhance productivity by\nenabling remote work, improving access to resources, and optimising workflows.\n\n\u2022 Reduced Development Cost: Leveraging distributed systems can often lead to cost savings in\ndevelopment and infrastructure.",
    "n30.20260213095926.1": "Key Considerations for CCSP (Certified Cloud Security Professional)\n\n\u2022 Clear Communications: \nEffective communication is vital in distributed environments. CCSPs\nshould ensure that there are clear communication channels and tools in place to facilitate\ncollaboration among distributed teams and stakeholders.\n\n\u2022 Coordination and Management of Activities:\nDistributed IT systems may involve teams and components located in\ndifferent regions or time zones. CCSPs should focus on\ncoordinating activities and managing resources efficiently\nto ensure project success.\n\n\u2022 Governance of Processes and Activities:\nEstablishing governance frameworks and policies is essential to\nmaintain control and compliance in distributed systems.\n\n\u2022 Distributed Computing: \nDistributed computing involves breaking down software components and tasks into\nsmaller parts that can run on multiple computers.\n\n\nIndeed, there are various forms of distributed computing architectures, each with its own\ncharacteristics and use cases. Below is a brief overview of some common distributed\ncomputing architectures:\n\nClient Server\nN-tier system\nPeer-to-Peer\n",
    "n30.20260213102539.1": "\u2022 It is essential to understand and assess the risk management\nprogram and processes when transitioning to any hosting\nenvironment with a service provider.\n\n\u2022 As the cloud customer hosts their services and data in this\nenvironment, the hosting provider's risk management processes\nand risk acceptance will directly impact the customer's security\nand risk management programs.\n\n\u2022 Certification becomes particularly valuable for the cloud provider\nin this Factor, as it informs the cloud customer about the type of\nrisk management programs and policies that the cloud provider\nhas in place.\n\n\u2022 It also specifies what level of risk can be accepted or what\nmeasures are required to mitigate risks.",
    "n30.20260213110253.1": "\n\u2022 The goal of this step is to analyse the controls that have been implemented or are planned for\nimplementation by the organisation to minimise or eliminate the likelihood or probability of\nexploiting a system vulnerability or threat.\n\n\u2022 The implementation of current or planned controls must be taken into consideration to derive\nan overall likelihood rating that indicates the probability that a potential vulnerability may be\nexploited within the Factor of the associated threat environment.\n\n\u2022 Analysing the controls involves a thorough examination of both existing and anticipated\nsecurity measures designed to mitigate identified risks.\n\n\u2022 This process not only assesses the adequacy of each control but also evaluates their collective\neffectiveness in the operational environment.",
    "n30.20260213110546.1": "\n\u2022 The risk policy definition of an organisation typically defines risk as an unexpected event that can\nimpact the organisation's ability to achieve its policy or financial objectives.\nGoals and Steps of WHO's Risk Management Policy\n\n\u2022 The World Health Organisation (WHO) is committed to managing risks effectively to safeguard its\nmission and objectives. WHO's risk management policy outlines specific goals and steps to achieve\nthis aim:\n\nGoals\n\n\u2022 Ensure Continuity of Essential Services: WHO's primary goal is to ensure the uninterrupted delivery\nof essential healthcare services and support, even in the face of unforeseen events or crises.\n\n\u2022 Protect Human and Financial Resources: The organisation seeks to protect its human resources and\nfinancial assets, ensuring they are utilised efficiently and responsibly.",
    "n30.20260213110722.1": "Risk Profiles\n\n\u2022 The data owners and custodians collaborate with the\norganisation's management to establish the overall risk\nprofile for their systems and applications.\n\n\u2022 The risk profile serves to document and identify the level of\nrisk that management is willing to accept.\n\n\u2022 It also outlines the process for evaluating and approving risk\nfor appropriate use requests.\n\n\u2022 In a cloud environment, the risk profile can become\nconsiderably more complex due to the multitude of systems\nand services that exist beyond the organisation's direct\ncontrol.",
    "n30.20260213110842.1": "\n\u2022 The willingness of management to embrace and accept\nstrategic risks contributes to the organisation's risk appetite.\n\n\u2022 This, in turn, shapes the overall security culture and\ndetermines the extent to which specific systems and services\ncan be utilised, in conjunction with the classification of the\ndata being handled.\n\n\u2022 A clear understanding of an organisation's risk appetite\nempowers systems staff and application managers to make\ntimely decisions for development and operations without\nrequiring consultation with management for every decision.",
    "n30.20260213111000.1": "\u2022 To comprehend risk as it pertains to data, it is essential to first\nhave a clear understanding of the various roles and their\nassociated responsibilities.\n\n\u2022 In many organisations, these roles are formalised and come\nwith dedicated responsibilities.\n\n\u2022 Regulatory and certification bodies often mandate the\nappointment of individuals as data owners and data\ncustodians, placing upon them significant responsibilities\nconcerning liability.\n\n\u2022 For these purposes, the key roles involved can be defined as\nthe Data Owner and Data Custodian.\n\n",
    "n30.20260213111131.1": "Data Owner or Data Controller: \n\nThis is an individual, either independently or as part of a\ngroup, who defines the purposes for processing any personal data.\n\nData controllers typically bear responsibility for managing data Factor, content, and related\nbusiness rules.\n\nData controllers/ owners, possess legal rights and full control over individual pieces or sets of data\nelements. \n\no They both may share responsibilities for defining distribution and related policies.",
    "n30.20260213111150.1": "Data Custodian or Data Processor: \n\nThis role refers to any person who processes data on behalf of the data controller.\n\nData custodians are responsible for data storage,\nsafekeeping, transportation, and the enforcement of\nbusiness rules.\n\no They both may share responsibilities for defining distribution and related policies.",
    "n30.20260213112009.1": "\n\u2022The HIPAA Breach Notification Rule mandates that HIPAA\ncovered entities, along with their business associates, must\nprovide notification in the event of a breach involving\nunsecured protected health information.\n\n\u2022Additionally, similar breach notification provisions, enforced\nby the Federal Trade Commission (FTC), are applicable to\npersonal health record vendors and their third-party service\nproviders.\n\n\u2022A breach is defined as an impermissible use or disclosure\nunder the Privacy Rule, which compromises the privacy and\nsecurity of protected health information.\n\n\u2022The covered entity or business associate, as applicable, can demonstrate a low probability of\ncompromised protected health information through a risk assessment that considers at least\nthe following factors, an unauthorised use or disclosure of protected health information is\npresumed to be a breach:\n\no Nature and Extent: This includes the types of identifiers within the protected health\ninformation and the likelihood of re-identification.\n\no Unauthorised User or Recipient: Consideration of who accessed or received the protected\nhealth information without authorisation.\n\no Access and Viewing: Whether the protected health information was viewed or obtained by\nthe unauthorised party.",
    "n30.20260213112214.1": "\n\u2022 While SOX does not primarily focus on preserving privacy or IT security, it plays a crucial role in\nregulating the accounting and financial practices employed by organisations.\n\n\u2022 This act was enacted to safeguard the interests of stakeholders and shareholders by addressing\nimproper practices and errors.\n\n\u2022 It establishes stringent rules for compliance, which are regulated and enforced by the\nSecurities and Exchange Commission (SEC).\n\n\u2022 SOX imposes substantial requirements, often surpassing those mandated by other regulatory\nor certification standards, on virtually every aspect of a financial system's operations and\ncontrols.",
    "n30.20260213112256.1": "\n\u2022 Cloud computing has become an integral part of most global businesses, revolutionising the\nway industries operate and thrive.\n\n\u2022 However, complying with various data protection and data privacy standards has posed\nsignificant challenges for the cloud industry. Much has changed for most businesses with the\nenforcement of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).\n\n\u2022 The enforcement of GDPR has had a profound impact on cloud service providers and their\noperations, particularly concerning operational implementation and security control\nmechanisms.\n\n\u2022 Cloud service providers are now obligated to fully understand their responsibilities regarding\ndata protection and privacy. They must adapt and adjust their services, contracts, and\nprocesses accordingly.\n",
    "n30.20260213112430.1": "\n\u2022Risk measures the threats that an organisation, its IT systems, and its data are exposed to.\n\n\u2022Risk assessment involves evaluating the organisation's vulnerability level, identifying the threats it\nencounters, assessing the likelihood of a threat succeeding, and considering mitigation measures\nthat can be implemented to further reduce the risk level.\n\n\u2022The organisation has two options: it can actively work to reduce and minimise the level of risk\nfacing its systems, or it can choose to accept and manage the existing level of risk.",
    "n30.20260213112620.1": "Framing Risk\n\nAssessing Risk\n\nResponding to Risk\n\nMonitoring Risk",
    "n30.20260213112917.1": "\n\n\u2022 An organisation may choose to accept the risk associated with a specific exploit and the threats\nit poses.\n\n\u2022 This decision typically follows a comprehensive risk assessment, including an evaluation of\nmitigation costs.\n\n\u2022 In cases where the cost of mitigation exceeds the potential costs of accepting the risk and\naddressing any potential consequences, the organisation may decide to address the exploit if\nand when it occurs.\"",
    "n30.20260213113028.1": "\n\u2022 An organisation may choose to take proactive measures to ensure that a risk is never realised,\ninstead of accepting or mitigating it.\n\n\u2022 This often involves decisions not to utilise certain services or systems. For instance, if a company\ndetermines that enabling direct purchasing through its website poses a significant risk to its\nsystems and data protections, it may opt not to offer online ordering at all.",
    "n30.20260213113114.1": "\n\u2022 Risk transfer involves another entity assuming the risk on behalf of the organisation. However, it is\nimportant to note that not all risks can be transferred.\n\n\u2022 A common example of risk transfer is through insurance policies, which can help cover the\nfinancial costs of successful risk exploits.",
    "n30.20260213113158.1": "\n\n\u2022 Risk mitigation is the most commonly expected and understood\nstrategy.\n\n\u2022 In risk mitigation, an organisation takes proactive measures,\nwhich may include investing in new systems or technologies, to\nrectify and prevent potential exploits.\n\n\u2022 These actions can range from completely eliminating a specific\nrisk to reducing the likelihood of an exploit occurring or\nmitigating the impact of a successful exploit.\n\n\u2022 The decision to pursue risk mitigation is typically based on a cost-\nbenefit analysis derived from the assessments conducted.",
    "n30.20260213113306.1": "Common risk frameworks encompass ISO 31000:2024, ENISA and NIST, \nparticularly their Cloud Computing Synopsis and Recommendations",
    "n30.20260213113421.1": "\n\n\u2022 Framework: ISO 31000 is an international standard that provides guidelines on risk management\nprinciples and practices.\n\n\u2022 The 2024 revision of this standard continues to emphasise a structured and comprehensive\napproach to risk management that is applicable across all organisational activities and sectors.\n\n\u2022 Application to Cloud Computing: While ISO 31000 is not specific to cloud computing, its principles\ncan be applied to manage risks associated with cloud services.\n\n\u2022 This includes establishing a risk management Factor, identifying risks, analysing and evaluating\nthese risks, and then implementing strategies to mitigate them.",
    "n30.20260213113536.1": "\n\u2022 The European Union Agency for Cybersecurity (ENISA) offers extensive guidance and reports\nthat address security and risk management in various aspects of information technology, with\na strong focus on cybersecurity.\n\n\u2022 NISA provides specific guidelines and recommendations for securing cloud environments.\nThese guidelines help organisations understand the security risks associated with cloud\nservices and suggest best practices to mitigate these risks.\n\n\u2022 ENISA reports often include strategies for managing data protection, resilience and regulatory\ncompliance within the cloud.",
    "n30.20260213113630.1": "NIST (National Institute of Standards & Technology)\n- Cloud Computing Synopsis and Recommendations\n\n\u2022 NIST is known for its detailed and technical guidelines that cover many aspects of information\ntechnology and cybersecurity.\n\n\u2022 The Cloud Computing Synopsis and Recommendations is a part of NIST broader efforts to\nprovide clear guidance on cloud computing technologies.\n\n\u2022 This particular document from NIST explains the security challenges and risks specific to cloud\ncomputing.\n\n\u2022 It provides a comprehensive overview of cloud service models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), deployment\nmodels (public, private, hybrid, community) and discusses strategies to mitigate risks\nassociated with cloud service adoption.\n\n\u2022 The recommendations aim to help organisations make informed decisions about cloud service\nproviders, data security and compliance with relevant standards and regulations.",
    "n30.20260213114107.1": "Metrics for Risk Management\n\n\u2022 Risks must be described in a way that is easily understandable and clear. This is essential not\nonly for internal communication but also for conveying risk information outside the\norganisation.\n\n\u2022 To achieve success in this endeavor, the organisation must agree on a set of risk management\nmetrics.\n\n\u2022 Each risk's probability and impact are assessed separately using a recommended risk scorecard.\nThe results are then combined to indicate the exposure, typically using a five-level scale for\nboth probability and impact, with the following categories:\n\no Minimal\no Low\no Moderate\no High\no Maximum",
    "n30.20260213114201.1": "\n\u2022 Properly assessing risk in a cloud environment necessitates the\nevaluation of multiple levels.\n\n\u2022 The first component is the individual application, device, or\nservice, which requires a similar analysis to that of hosting in a\ntraditional data center.\n\n\u2022 However, in the cloud, additional cloud-specific aspects must\nalso be taken into account.\n\n\u2022 Additionally, the cloud provider itself needs to undergo a risk\nassessment.\n\n\u2022 This assessment should consider factors such as the provider's\ntrack record, reliability, concentration of services, financial\nhealth, and future trajectory.",
    "n30.20260213114523.1": "\n\u2022 A definition of cloud computing encompasses core features and advantages that are common\nacross all clouds. However, cloud services may vary in their specific details.\n\n\u2022 From the perspective of service users, cloud computing services possess the following key\ncharacteristics:\n\no Provider Hosts and Maintains the Infrastructure: Cloud hosting providers purchase, host,\nand maintain the necessary hardware and software in-house. This arrangement allows users\nto avoid capital expenditures and the maintenance challenges they would face if they were\nto develop and manage the service on-premises.\n\no Self-Service via a Web Interface: Users have the ability to initiate specific service functions\nand adjust their service usage levels with ease through a web interface. This self-service\ncapability minimises the need for extensive interaction with the service provider."
  },
  "uas": {
    "srd.20260113205330.1": {
      "_mod_time": 1769126051.409
    }
  }
}